IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `family-domain`

§ `Wills Act 1838 — formal validity, revocation, revival and the privileged will`

-- ===========================================================================
-- WILLS ACT 1838 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, in force from 28/10/2022.
--
-- SCOPE. This module encodes the determinate spine of the Act:
--
--   s 4          the age bar                    -> `s 4 — will of infant invalid`
--   s 5(1)-(4)   formal validity by a law       -> `s 5 — the extent to which the will
--                outside this Act                   is treated as properly executed`,
--                                                   and the whole-will answer off it
--   s 5(9)       the commencement of s 5        -> `s 5(9) — this section applies ...`
--   s 6(1)-(2)   mode of execution              -> `s 6(1) — ...` / `s 6(2) — ...`
--   s 6(3)-(4)   the position of the signature  -> `s 6(3) — ...` / `s 6(4) — ...`
--   s 7          execution of an appointment    -> `s 7(1) — ...` / `s 7(2) — ...`
--   s 8          publication not necessary      -> `s 8 — ...`
--   s 9          incompetency of a witness      -> `s 9 — ...`
--   s 10         gifts to an attesting witness  -> `s 10(1) — ...` .. `s 10(4) — ...`
--   s 11, s 12   creditor and executor witness  -> `s 11 — ...` / `s 12 — ...`
--   s 13         revocation by marriage         -> `s 13 — ...`
--   s 14         no revocation by presumption   -> `s 14 — ...`
--   s 15         how a will may be revoked      -> `s 15 — the will has been revoked`
--   s 16         obliteration and alteration    -> `s 16(1) — ...` / `s 16(2) — ...`
--   s 17         revival                        -> `s 17 — the extent of the revival`
--   s 18         subsequent conveyance          -> `s 18 — ...`
--   s 19         the will speaks from death     -> `s 19 — ...`
--   s 26         gifts to issue do not lapse    -> `s 26 — ...`
--   s 27         soldiers and mariners          -> `s 27(1) — ...` .. `s 27(3) — ...`,
--                                                   s 27(3) both per-appointment
--                                                   (a LIST OF `Guardian`) and
--                                                   as a case-level summary
--   s 28(2),(4)-(5)  the rectification time bar -> `s 28(2) — ...`
--
-- and aggregates them in `the formal validity of the will`.
--
-- The sections it does NOT encode are named, one by one and with the reason,
-- in the closing section `What is NOT encoded, and why`. A reader who cannot
-- tell a provision that is absent from the Act from one that is merely absent
-- from this file has been misled, so nothing is dropped silently.
--
-- SOURCE. Every quoted string is copied mechanically out of
-- source/WA1838.txt in this directory — a `pdftotext -layout` extraction of
-- the official SSO PDF — with the running headers, page numbers and the
-- "Informal Consolidation" watermark lines discarded and line-wrapping
-- normalised to single spaces. Nothing here was reconstructed from memory.
-- The Act's own curly quotation marks and apostrophes (“soldier”, testator’s)
-- are preserved.
--
-- QUOTATIONS AND CITATIONS TO THE OTHER THREE ACTS. This module ENCODES from
-- WA1838.txt and from nothing else — no rule below takes a fact from another
-- Act's text — and every quotation from the WILLS ACT is first hand and
-- checkable against source/WA1838.txt.
--
-- THE CROSS-MODULE ROUND AMENDED WHAT THAT MEANS FOR THE NEIGHBOURS, and the
-- amendment is stated here because the earlier wording is still visible in the
-- repository history and a reader must not apply it to the new comments. It
-- used to say that citations to the other three Acts were at SECOND HAND, from
-- family-domain.l4. That was true of everything written before the
-- cross-module audit, and it is still true of the comments written then. But
-- the audit asked this module to say what its neighbours do and why, and a
-- claim about another Act repeated at second hand is exactly the kind of
-- borrowed claim that gets sharpened in transit. So from that round on:
--   * quotations from GIA1934.txt, PAA1934.txt and ISA1967.txt are FIRST HAND
--     and are checkable against those files, which sit in the same source/
--     directory as WA1838.txt and are extractions of the same kind. Each such
--     quotation NAMES its file and its section at the site;
--   * quotations still taken at second hand say so at the site, and name the
--     module they are taken from;
--   * claims about what another MODULE does — as distinct from what another
--     ACT says — are measurements of a file at a moment, are labelled as such,
--     and will go stale. They are worth making anyway, because the alternative
--     is a hand-off contract stated as though somebody were performing it.
--
-- THE SECTION NUMBERS ARE THE TEXT'S, NOT A MAP MADE BEFOREHAND. Three of
-- them are easy to get wrong and are called out here because the working
-- brief for this encoding had them one section out: the position of the
-- signature is s 6(3), NOT s 7 (s 7 is the execution of an appointment by
-- will); revival is s 17, NOT s 16 (s 16 is obliteration, interlineation and
-- alteration); and the privileged will is s 27 alone, NOT ss 26-27 (s 26 is
-- the lapse-saving for gifts to issue). The text wins.
--
-- ONTOLOGY. The nouns come from family-domain.l4 and are not redeclared here.
-- What this module declares is exactly the facts that only the Wills Act
-- asks — the s 5 connecting factors, the s 6(3) signature positions, the
-- s 6(4) position of a disposition relative to the signature, the s 15 modes
-- of revocation, the s 16 alteration facts, the s 17 revival facts, and the
-- per-section contrary intentions — wrapped around the shared `Will` and
-- `Deceased person`.
--
-- HOUSE STYLE. Inert: nothing is @export'ed. `GIVEN`, never `ASSUME`.
-- Verbatim statutory prose rides inline as an inert string; `...` is AND-sugar
-- and `..` is OR-sugar, and the last rung of a chain spells its keyword. A
-- string in boolean position is the identity of its context, so it never
-- changes a result.
--
-- THE DENIAL IDIOM, used seven times and explained once here. Several
-- sections of this Act do not create a consequence — they DENY one: "the will
-- shall not on that account be invalid" (s 9), "No will shall be revoked by
-- any presumption of an intention on the ground of an alteration in
-- circumstances" (s 14), "shall be admitted a witness to prove the execution
-- of the will" notwithstanding a gift, a charge or an executorship
-- (ss 10(2), 11, 12), "no such will shall be affected by the circumstance"
-- (s 6(3)), "shall not be treated as improperly executed by reason only that"
-- (s 5(4)). Each is encoded as a decision that NAMES ITS ANTECEDENT and then
-- conjoins FALSE:
--
--     `s 14 — the will is revoked by a presumption of an intention on the
--      ground of an alteration in circumstances` MEANS
--             <the subsection, verbatim, as an inert string>
--         ...     <the antecedent, spelled out>
--             AND FALSE
--
-- The `AND FALSE` is not dead code and must not be tidied away: it IS the
-- section. A reader can see which fact the section is about and that the fact
-- has no effect, and an #EVAL can be run on a case where the antecedent holds
-- and still print FALSE. The alternative — leaving the antecedent out of the
-- file entirely — makes a denial indistinguishable from a silence.
--
-- NO REGULATIVE RULE. The Act is constitutive from end to end: it says when a
-- will is valid, revoked, revived or void, never who must do what by when.
-- The one deadline in it, s 28(2)'s six months, is a condition on an
-- application nobody is obliged to make, so it is a constitutive test and not
-- a `PARTY ... MUST ... WITHIN`. Encoding it as a duty would say that somebody
-- must apply to rectify a will, which the Act does not.
--
-- AMBIGUITY REGISTER. Eleven interpretive forks are marked `-- AMBIGUITY A1` to
-- `A11` at the site where each bites, with both readings, the reading taken
-- and why. They are tabulated again in the closing section.
--
-- WHAT THE ADVERSARIAL REVIEW CHANGED, so that a later editor does not
-- silently un-change a repair. Sixteen findings were reported against this
-- module; each repair is marked `REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW Vn` at the site,
-- with the defect it fixes spelled out, and the whole of the run section was
-- converted from `#EVAL` to `#ASSERT`. In summary:
--
--   V1  s 18       A subsequent conveyance and a revocation were merely
--                  CO-PRESENT, so a will revoked by marriage with an unrelated
--                  later conveyance reported the opposite of what s 18 says.
--                  A per-act fact now ties the exception to the act, and only
--                  the s 15 limbs an act of that description can work count.
--   V2  s 6(3)     The deeming was computed and read by nothing, so the
--                  subsection that relaxes "signed at the foot or end" could
--                  not relax it. It is now the third alternative in that limb.
--   V3  s 6(3)     The positional half of the "so ... that" was dropped, which
--                  deemed valid a signature above every dispositive word. It is
--                  now a field and a conjunct.
--   V4  s 5(3)     The three "so far as" paragraphs were OR'ed into a
--                  whole-will boolean that produced `valid`. They are now split
--                  from s 5(3)(a) and reported under their own outcome.
--   V5  s 7(1)     A prohibition had become a permission: the subsection says
--                  "shall be valid, unless the will is executed in the manner
--                  required by this Act", and the rule returned that the
--                  appointment WAS valid if it was. The decision is now named
--                  and returned in the negative, as the section is.
--   V6  s 28(2)    Six months after 31 August rolled forward into March. It now
--                  clamps to the end of the month.
--   V7  s 5(9)     An unproved date of death drew a definite verdict whose
--                  stated reason was false. The aggregate now reports that s 5
--                  could not be applied, as it already did for s 4.
--   V8  s 28(2)    The permission branch sat below the evidence branch, and its
--                  outcome asserted lateness not in evidence. Reordered and
--                  renamed.
--   V9-V16         Paperwork: a self-contradicting note on ss 24-25, a fixture
--                  whose comment misstated an age, three unencoded or
--                  under-noted matters named (s 10(1)'s claiming-under class,
--                  s 27(2)'s "was of such age", s 28(4)'s enum hazard), a
--                  paraphrased field name restored to the Act's two limbs, and
--                  two stray citations.
--   V15            123 `#EVAL`s became `#ASSERT`s. Four `#EVAL`s remain, each
--                  beside an assertion that pins the same value. See the count
--                  in the run section, which is where it is kept up to date.
--
-- Two further defects of the same SHAPE as V1 and V7 were found by looking for
-- them, and are repaired and marked at s 26 (three limbs of one sentence asked
-- of a list rather than of one person) and at s 28(2) (an undated grant
-- silently dropped, so the clock started from a later one).
--
-- WHAT THE SECOND REVIEW ROUND CHANGED. The first round left every reported
-- defect repaired but two of the repairs, and much of the Act besides,
-- UNTESTED — the review measured this by mutating the code rather than the
-- expectations, and found conjuncts that could be deleted with all 218
-- assertions still green — thirty of them, on the measurement recorded in the
-- run section. Eleven findings came back; the repairs are marked
-- `ROUND 2, Rn` at the site.
--
--   R2   s 7(1)    The polarity flip at V5 turned a silence into an assertion
--                  of invalidity, and s 5(1)'s "notwithstanding any other
--                  provisions of this Act" forbids that assertion on a will
--                  s 5(3)(d) has treated as properly executed as to the very
--                  appointment. Registered as AMBIGUITY A11 and encoded on the
--                  reading that does not contradict s 5.
--   R11  s 6(3)    The subsection deems valid the position of "the signature of
--                  the testator, or of the person signing for him" — so it
--                  presupposes such a signature. That presupposition rode in a
--                  field NAME, and a note claimed the code enforced it. It is a
--                  fact and a conjunct now.
--   R6, R7         The only `Will` constructor hardcoded the executors EMPTY
--                  and the apparent intention TRUE, so s 12's antecedent could
--                  never hold and s 6(3)'s evidential conjunct could never
--                  fail. Both are parameters now.
--   R8   s 10(1)   The one fixture aimed at "other than and except charges and
--                  directions for the payment of any debt" recorded the charge
--                  as NOT beneficial, which did the exception's work twice and
--                  left both the exception and the `beneficial` conjunct
--                  untested.
--   R9             A block of encoded limbs no case file could reach: six of
--                  s 5's nine connecting factors, s 27(1)'s mariner and
--                  s 27(4)'s naval route (both hardcoded FALSE in the `Person`
--                  constructor, while the aggregate's verdict names the mariner
--                  in terms), s 28(5)'s kind test, s 27(3)'s appointment-exists
--                  conjunct, and s 26's merged traversal.
--   R3, R4, R5     Fixtures that discriminate rather than over-determine: at
--                  s 18 for each of V1's two new conjuncts, at s 6(2) for the
--                  acknowledgement limb, and at s 10(3) for the two-witness
--                  count and its subscription filter.
--   R9 (s 26)      A note claimed the "leaving issue" limb and the survivorship
--                  limb were separable and gave a counterexample that is not
--                  one. Corrected: the second entails the first as modelled.
--   R1, R10        Bookkeeping. See the run section for the assertion count and
--                  for what does and does not gate a wrong answer.
--
-- WHAT THE CROSS-MODULE ROUND CHANGED. The first two reviews read this module
-- on its own. The third read all four Act modules TOGETHER, and found things
-- neither of the others could see: two Acts answering one question differently,
-- a hand-off contract written by one side and unknown to the other, and a
-- shared ontology field written by every fixture and read by nobody. Repairs
-- are marked `CROSS-MODULE ROUND, Xn` at the site.
--
--   X0  THE RENAME. The four Act modules COULD NOT BE IMPORTED TOGETHER: L4
--       raises a hard error at the first use of a colliding name. Measured
--       across the five modules: FIFTEEN distinct top-level names collided, in
--       twenty-one module-pair collisions (WA/ISA 12, WA/PAA 3, WA/GIA 1,
--       ISA/PAA 4, ISA/GIA 1; no rule name collided anywhere). Thirteen of the
--       fifteen were this module's — nine fixture people and four constructors
--       for shared records. Every fixture person and every shared-record constructor here
--       now carries this Act's tag (`the WA Bala`, `a WA deceased person`); no
--       statutory decision was renamed, because those names are the
--       deliverable. Behaviour-preserving, and measured to be: 332 directives,
--       328 assertions, byte-identical results before and after.
--   X1  s 27(3)   The subsection makes good "any appointment contained in that
--                 will of any person as guardian of the infant children of the
--                 testator", and the rule asked only whether the will appointed
--                 ANY guardian. A will appointing a guardian of a stranger's
--                 child got the same answer as one appointing a guardian of the
--                 testator's own. The fact was readable and simply not read.
--                 The subsection is also distributive over the appointments,
--                 which a case-level boolean cannot express, so it is now a
--                 LIST OF `Guardian` with the boolean defined off it.
--   X2  s 27(3)   The other half of "the infant children" — infancy itself — is
--                 NOT encoded, family-domain forbidding this module the shared
--                 age-of-majority field. Stated at the site with the
--                 consequence (this decision over-includes) rather than left to
--                 be inferred, and reported as a domain gap.
--   X3  s 27(3)   The hand-off contract to the Guardianship of Infants Act was
--                 written as though somebody were performing it. Measured, and
--                 re-measured after that module moved: it now names the join
--                 but still does not read the answer. Recorded, dated, and
--                 labelled a measurement of another file — which it is, and
--                 which went stale once between measuring and writing.
--   X4  the join  No assertion in ANY of the four modules exercised a join,
--                 because every `Deceased person` constructor hardcoded the
--                 other Acts' facts EMPTY. `the infants concerned in the estate`
--                 is a parameter here now, one case file carries the same
--                 `Guardian` object on both sides of s 27(3), and five
--                 assertions compare the two sides.
--   X5  s 4       AMBIGUITY A1 resolves "age not in evidence" one way and two
--                 neighbouring modules resolve it two other ways, none of the
--                 three aware of the others. Cross-referenced, with the reason
--                 this Act is right to differ.
--   X6  s 26      The audit returned "disagree" on ISA s 3's "child" against
--                 s 26's "a child or other issue". Checked against
--                 source/ISA1967.txt and source/WA1838.txt: the reading here
--                 stands, on words this Act supplies itself and borrows from
--                 nobody. The neighbour has since resolved its own half and
--                 says the same; both measurements are recorded at the site,
--                 and what this reading does NOT decide is stated too.
--   X7  s 26      GIA s 17 reaches into this Act's answer about a gift and
--                 nothing here acknowledged it. Recorded at the site, with what
--                 this module does about it (nothing) and why.
-- ===========================================================================


§§ `Section 1 — Short title`

-- "1. This Act is the Wills Act 1838." No operative content; carried inert so
-- that the file starts where the Act starts.
`s 1 — short title` MEANS "This Act is the Wills Act 1838."


§§ `Section 2 — Interpretation`

-- Carried inert. Of the five definitions, three are consumed by provisions
-- this module encodes and two are not:
--   "will"          — s 15(b), s 17(1) and PAA s 2 all treat a codicil as a
--                     will; family-domain's `Will` carries `the codicils` as a
--                     LIST OF `Will` for that reason, and every rule below is
--                     stated of one instrument.
--   "real estate" /
--   "personal estate" — the s 27(1)/(2) split turns on them, and nothing else
--                     here does.
--   "internal law" /
--   "state"          — read only by s 5, and only to name a system of law
--                     this cleanroom does not model. See the note on
--                     `Conformity of the execution of a will to a law outside
--                     this Act` below.
@ref Wills Act 1838 s 2
`s 2 — interpretation` MEANS
        "“internal law”, in relation to any territory or state, means the law which would apply in a case where no question of the law in force in any other territory or state arose;"
    ... "“personal estate” shall extend to leasehold estates and other chattels real, and also to moneys, shares of Government and other funds, securities for money, not being real estates, debts, choses in action, rights, credits, goods and all other property whatsoever which by law devolves upon the executor or administrator, and to any share or interest therein;"
    ... "“real estate” shall extend to messuages, lands, rents and hereditaments, whether corporeal, incorporeal or personal, and to any undivided share thereof and to any estate, right or interest, other than a chattel interest, therein;"
    ... "“state” means a territory or group of territories having its own law of nationality;"
    ... "“will” includes a testament and an appointment by will or by writing in the nature of a will in exercise of a power and also a disposition by will and testament and any other testamentary disposition."


§§ `The facts this Act asks that the shared ontology does not carry`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(2)(a)-(d), s 5(3)(a)-(d)
--
-- ONE PROVED FACT PER LIMB, and that is a boundary rather than a shortcut.
-- s 5 asks whether the execution of the will "conformed to the internal law in
-- force" in a named territory or state. Whether it did is a question of
-- another country's law, and this cleanroom models no named territory or
-- state anywhere (family-domain says so expressly, in its closing section, and
-- leaves s 5 to this module on exactly that footing). So each limb arrives
-- here already proved, one boolean per connecting factor, and s 5(5) to s 5(8)
-- — the rules for deciding what a foreign law required and which of several
-- systems applies — have nothing to operate on and are not encoded.
--
-- THE SINGAPORE CASE IS NOT CIRCULAR. Where the will was executed in
-- Singapore, the internal law in force in the territory where it was executed
-- IS this Act, so s 5(2)(a) would be answered by s 6 itself. This module does
-- not compute that: the field is a proved fact either way, and a case file
-- that sets it TRUE for a Singapore execution is asserting what s 6 decides.
-- s 5 matters here for the FOREIGN execution, which is the case it was
-- inserted (by the Wills (Amendment) Act 1992) to reach.
DECLARE `Conformity of the execution of a will to a law outside this Act` HAS
    `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where it was executed`                                                                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator was domiciled at the time when the will was executed`                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator was domiciled at the time of his death`                                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator habitually resided at either of the times referred to in paragraph (b)`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the state of which the testator was a national at either of the times referred to in paragraph (b)`       IS A BOOLEAN

    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(3)(a)
    `the will was executed on board a vessel or an aircraft of any description, and the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory with which, having regard to its registration (if any) and other relevant circumstances, the vessel or aircraft may be taken to have been most closely connected` IS A BOOLEAN
    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(3)(b)
    `so far as the will disposes of immovable property, its execution conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the property was situated`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(3)(c)
    `so far as the will revokes a will which under this Act would be treated as properly executed, or revokes a provision which under this Act would be treated as comprised in a properly executed will, the execution of the later will conformed to any law by reference to which the revoked will or provision would be treated as properly executed` IS A BOOLEAN
    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(3)(d)
    `so far as the will exercises a power of appointment, the execution of the will conformed to the law governing the essential validity of the power`                            IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(3), s 6(3)(a)-(e)
--
-- WHERE THE SIGNATURE IS, AND THE FIVE CIRCUMSTANCES THAT DO NOT MATTER.
-- family-domain leaves them here — they are facts about one sheet of paper,
-- read by s 6(3) and by nothing else in any of the four Acts — and keeps the
-- fact they qualify, that the testator's intention is apparent on the face of
-- the will, on `Will` where s 6(3)'s deeming needs it.
--
-- THE FIRST FIELD IS s 6(3)'s OWN ANTECEDENT and is a REPAIR (ADVERSARIAL
-- REVIEW V3). s 6(3) is a so-that construction with two halves: the signature
-- must be "so placed at or after, or following, or under, or beside, or
-- opposite to the end of the will" — that is the first half — and it must be so
-- placed "that it shall be apparent on the face of the will that the testator
-- intended to give effect by such his signature to the writing signed as his
-- will", which is the second. The encoding used to carry only the second
-- half, and said so in terms ("on one fact"), which deemed valid a signature
-- placed above every dispositive word — the one position the subsection was
-- never meant to save, and the position s 6(3)(d) presupposes is merely one
-- PAGE among several. Neither half can carry the other: a signature can be at
-- the end without the intention being apparent, and apparent without being
-- anywhere near the end.
--
-- The field NAMES the signature it is about — "of the testator, or of the
-- person signing for him as mentioned in subsection (2)" — because the deeming
-- presupposes that there IS such a signature and excuses only its position.
-- THAT PRESUPPOSITION IS NOW ITS OWN FACT AND ITS OWN CONJUNCT (ROUND 2, R11):
-- an earlier draft of this note claimed that "a will nobody signed cannot
-- answer this field TRUE", which was a discipline on whoever wrote the case
-- file and not a property of the code. It is enforced now; see the first field
-- of the DECLARE below.
--
-- THE OTHER FIVE ARE RECORDED EVEN THOUGH THEY CHANGE NOTHING. That is the
-- point: the denial idiom above needs an antecedent to deny, and a reader has
-- to be able to see that the Act contemplated each of these five and gave it
-- no effect.
DECLARE `The position of the signature` HAS
    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(3) — "the position of the signature of the testator, or of the person signing for him as mentioned in subsection (2)"
    -- THE PRESUPPOSITION, MADE A FACT (REPAIR, ROUND 2, R11). s 6(3) relaxes
    -- s 6(2)'s first limb "as far only as regards the position of the signature
    -- of the testator, or of the person signing for him as mentioned in
    -- subsection (2)" — the POSITION, and nothing else; so it presupposes that
    -- there IS such a signature and
    -- excuses only where it sits. The encoding used to carry that presupposition
    -- in the NAME of the field below and nowhere else, and the note above it
    -- stated the guarantee as though the code enforced it ("A will nobody signed
    -- cannot answer this field TRUE"). It did not: the position field is proved
    -- directly, so a case file recording a will that nobody signed, with the
    -- position field TRUE, satisfied s 6(2)'s first limb through the deeming.
    -- The two `Will` fields cannot supply this, because each of them conflates
    -- WHO signed with WHERE — "signed at the foot or end thereof by the
    -- testator" — and it is precisely the "at the foot or end" half that s 6(3)
    -- is relaxing. So the bare existence of the signature is recorded here,
    -- beside the position it qualifies, and conjoined into the deeming.
    `there is a signature of the testator, or of a person signing for him in his presence and by his direction` IS A BOOLEAN
    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(3)
    `the signature of the testator, or of the person signing for him as mentioned in subsection (2), is so placed at or after, or following, or under, or beside, or opposite to the end of the will` IS A BOOLEAN
    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(3)(a)-(e)
    `the signature does not follow and is not immediately after the foot or end of the will`                                                                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `a blank space intervenes between the concluding word of the will and the signature`                                                                                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `the signature is placed among the words of the testimonium clause or of the clause of attestation, or follows or is after or under the clause of attestation, either with or without a blank space intervening, or follows or is after, or under, or beside the names or one of the names of the subscribing witnesses` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the signature is on a side or page or other portion of the paper or papers containing the will whereon no clause or paragraph or disposing part of the will is written above the signature` IS A BOOLEAN
    `there appears to be sufficient space on or at the bottom of the preceding side or page or other portion of the same paper on which the will is written to contain the signature` IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(4)
--
-- s 6(4) is the only provision in the Act that bites on ONE disposition rather
-- than on the instrument, so its facts hang off a disposition and not off the
-- will. family-domain's `Disposition` records what was given and to whom; where
-- on the paper it sits is asked by s 6(4) and by nothing else.
DECLARE `A disposition, and where it stands in relation to the signature` HAS
    `the disposition`                                        IS A Disposition
    `underneath or following the signature`                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `inserted after the signature was made`                  IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 15(b)-(d)
--
-- s 15(a) IS NOT A FIELD, because it is not a fact: "as provided in
-- section 13" is a cross-reference to a rule this module computes. The other
-- three limbs are facts, and s 15(d) is THREE of them rather than one,
-- because the destruction and the animus are separately provable and a
-- destruction without the intention of revoking is the case the limb exists
-- to exclude. The two actor limbs are kept apart for the same reason
-- family-domain keeps s 6(2)'s two signing limbs apart.
DECLARE `The ways in which a will may have been revoked` HAS
    `revoked by another will or codicil executed in the manner by this Act required`                                                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `revoked by some writing declaring an intention to revoke it, and executed in the manner in which a will is by this Act required to be executed`        IS A BOOLEAN
    `burnt, torn, or otherwise destroyed by the testator`                                                                                                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `burnt, torn, or otherwise destroyed by some person in his presence and by his direction`                                                               IS A BOOLEAN
    `destroyed with the intention of revoking it`                                                                                                          IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 16(1)-(2)
--
-- BOTH HALVES OF s 16 ARE HERE. s 16(1) makes an alteration ineffective unless
-- either the earlier words are no longer apparent or the alteration is itself
-- duly executed; s 16(2) then says what counts as duly executing an
-- alteration, in two alternative places. Recording only the first would leave
-- s 16(2)'s deeming with nothing to deem.
DECLARE `An alteration made in a will after its execution` HAS
    `the words or effect of the will before the alteration are apparent`                                                                                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the alteration is executed in the like manner as by this Act is required for the execution of the will`                                                IS A BOOLEAN
    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 16(2)
    `the signature of the testator and the subscription of the witnesses are made in the margin or on some other part of the will opposite or near to the alteration` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the signature of the testator and the subscription of the witnesses are made at the foot or end of or opposite to a memorandum referring to the alteration and written at the end or some other part of the will` IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 17(1)-(2)
DECLARE `The revival of a revoked will` HAS
    `revived by the re-execution thereof`                                                                                                                   IS A BOOLEAN
    `revived by a codicil executed in the manner required by this Act and showing an intention to revive the will or codicil`                                IS A BOOLEAN
    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 17(2)
    `partly revoked, and afterwards wholly revoked`                                                                                                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `an intention to the contrary is shown`                                                                                                                 IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5, s 6(2), s 6(3), s 10(3), s 15, s 17, s 18, s 19, s 26, s 27(2)
-- REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V16(ii). This citation used to list s 16. No
-- field on this record is a s 16 fact: the obliteration, interlineation and
-- alteration facts live on `An alteration made in a will after its
-- execution` above, and a stray pinpoint on a record is a claim about the
-- record that a reader checks against the fields and does not find.
--
-- THE WRAPPER. family-domain's `Will` is shared by four Acts and carries the
-- facts they all read. This record adds the ones only this Act asks, so that
-- every rule below can take a single GIVEN parameter.
--
-- `the will` HERE IS THE INSTRUMENT IN QUESTION, which may be the principal
-- will or one of its codicils: s 15(b) and s 17(1) treat a codicil as a will,
-- and family-domain makes `the codicils` a LIST OF `Will` for that reason. The
-- `Deceased person`'s own `the will` remains the principal instrument, which
-- is what the other three Acts read.
DECLARE `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks` HAS
    `the will`                                                                          IS A Will

    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(3)
    -- WHERE THE SIGNATURE SITS ON THE PAPER, which s 6(3) and nothing else asks.
    -- It is on this record and not on family-domain's `Will` for the reason
    -- family-domain gives: it is a Wills Act fact and no other Act reads it.
    -- It is REACHED FROM HERE, and that is a repair: s 6(3) used to be computed
    -- from `Will` alone and consumed by nothing, so the subsection that exists
    -- to relax s 6(2)'s "signed at the foot or end thereof" could not relax it.
    -- See the note on `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` (ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V2).
    `the position of the signature`                                                     IS A `The position of the signature`

    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(2)-(3)
    `the conformity of its execution to a law outside this Act`                         IS A `Conformity of the execution of a will to a law outside this Act`

    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 15
    `the ways in which it has been revoked`                                             IS A `The ways in which a will may have been revoked`

    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 17
    `its revival`                                                                       IS A `The revival of a revoked will`

    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 10(3) — "if the will is duly executed without his attestation and without that of any other such person"
    -- THE COUNTERFACTUAL s 6(2) PRESENCE FACT, and it is here because the
    -- shared ontology cannot supply it. `Will`'s own field records that the
    -- signature was made or acknowledged in the presence of 2 or more
    -- witnesses present at the same time — of whoever was in fact there. s
    -- 10(3) asks a different question: whether the will would still be duly
    -- executed if the benefiting witnesses were struck out. That cannot be
    -- re-derived from one boolean over the whole set, so the counterfactual is
    -- proved directly. The COUNT of the remaining witnesses, and whether each
    -- subscribed in the testator's presence, ARE derivable and are computed
    -- rather than recorded.
    `the signature was made or acknowledged by the testator as the signature to his will or codicil in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time, counting only witnesses to whom and to whose wife or husband no such disposition is given or made` IS A BOOLEAN

    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 18 — "No conveyance or other act made or done subsequently to the execution of a will of or relating to any real or personal estate therein comprised"
    `a conveyance or other act has been made or done subsequently to its execution, of or relating to any real or personal estate therein comprised` IS A BOOLEAN

    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 18 — "except an act by which the will shall be revoked as aforesaid"
    -- THE EXCEPTION IS ABOUT THAT ACT, NOT ABOUT THE FILE, and this field is a
    -- repair (ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V1). s 18's only exception is "an act by which
    -- the will shall be revoked as aforesaid" — the SAME act, not some other
    -- revocation that happens to have occurred. Without a fact tying the two
    -- together, the rule could only ask whether a conveyance existed and
    -- whether the will was revoked, which are independent; a will revoked by
    -- marriage with an unrelated later conveyance then reported that the
    -- conveyance had prevented the operation of the will, which is the opposite
    -- of what s 18 protects.
    `that conveyance or other act is an act by which the will is revoked as aforesaid` IS A BOOLEAN

    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 19 — "unless a contrary intention shall appear by the will"
    -- ONE CONTRARY-INTENTION FACT PER SECTION, not one for the Act. The Act
    -- uses the formula seven times (ss 19, 20, 21(1), 21(2), 22, 23(1), 26)
    -- and each time about a different question, so a single field would let a
    -- will that displaces the s 19 construction silently displace the s 26
    -- lapse-saving too. Only the two sections this module encodes get a field.
    `a contrary intention appears by the will as to the time from which it speaks` IS A BOOLEAN

    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 26 — "unless a contrary intention shall appear by the will"
    `a contrary intention appears by the will as to the lapse of a devise or bequest to a child or other issue` IS A BOOLEAN

    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 27(1)-(2) — "as he might have done before the making of this Act"; "in such manner and form that if the disposition had been a disposition of personal estate made by that person it would have been valid"
    -- ONE FIELD FOR BOTH SUBSECTIONS, because s 27(2) states its standard by
    -- pointing back at s 27(1)'s: the disposition of real estate is saved
    -- where it "has been made in such manner and form that if the disposition
    -- had been a disposition of personal estate made by that person it would
    -- have been valid", and a disposition of personal estate by a privileged
    -- testator is valid if made "as he might have done before the making of
    -- this Act". What that older law required is not in this Act and is not
    -- computed here.
    --
    -- AND s 27(2)'s OTHER LIMB, "was of such age", COLLAPSES — it is not
    -- dropped. The subsection validates a disposition of real estate "in any
    -- case where the person making the disposition was of such age and the
    -- disposition has been made in such manner and form that if the disposition
    -- had been a disposition of personal estate made by that person it would
    -- have been valid": two conditions under one counterfactual. The age
    -- condition can never bite inside s 27, because s 27(1) lets a privileged
    -- testator dispose of his personal estate "even though under 21 years of
    -- age" — so there is no age at which the counterfactual personal-estate
    -- disposition would fail for want of age. Recorded here rather than left to
    -- be inferred. (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V12.)
    `made in such manner and form that if the disposition had been a disposition of personal estate made by that person it would have been valid` IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(2), s 28(4)(c)
--
-- THE CASE. `Deceased person` is the record every module in this cleanroom
-- takes as its root; the will in question and the four facts s 28 needs ride
-- beside it.
--
-- s 28(4)(c)'S PROVISO IS ONE FACT PER CASE, NOT ONE PER GRANT, and that is a
-- limitation rather than a reading. The proviso asks whether "a grant limited
-- to the remainder of the testator’s estate has previously been made or is
-- made at the same time" as the part-limited grant under consideration.
-- family-domain's `Grant` carries the part as a free-text STRING
-- (`the part of the estate to which the grant is limited`) and gives a grant
-- no identity, so nothing here can decide whether one grant's part is
-- another's remainder. Recorded once per case, which is right wherever there
-- is at most one part-limited grant and wrong where two compete.
DECLARE `A case under this Act` HAS
    `the deceased`                                                                      IS A `Deceased person`
    `the will in question`                                                              IS A `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks`

    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(1)-(2)
    `an application for an order under section 28(1) has been made`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `the date of that application`                                                      IS A MAYBE DATE
    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(2) — "Except with the permission of a court"
    `a court has given permission for the application to be made after the end of that period` IS A BOOLEAN
    @ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(4)(c)
    `a grant limited to the remainder of the testator’s estate has previously been made or is made at the same time` IS A BOOLEAN


§§ `Helpers — dates, survivorship and descent`

-- None of these is a rule of the Act. They are the arithmetic the rules need,
-- kept out of the rules so that a reader comparing a rule with its section
-- sees the section and not the calendar.

GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A Will
`the will under consideration` `the case` MEANS
    `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `the will`

GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A Person
`the testator of the will under consideration` `the case` MEANS
    (`the will under consideration` `the case`)'s `the testator`

GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
-- family-domain's `Vital status` is total and names the gap: a person may be
-- living, may have died on a known date, or may be known to be dead with the
-- date not in evidence. Only the second yields a date, and every test in this
-- Act that turns on a death after a named day therefore fails on the third —
-- which is the point of the third member existing.
`the date of the death of` `the person` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the person`'s `vital status`
    WHEN `living`                            THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `died on` `the date of death`       THEN JUST `the date of death`
    WHEN `dead, the date of death not known` THEN NOTHING

-- AMBIGUITY A2 — a commencement expressed as a death after a named day, and
-- the day itself.
--   The Act fixes four such boundaries: s 5(9) ("died before 26 June 1992"
--   against "dies after that date"), s 10(4) ("dying after 29 May 1992"),
--   s 27(2) and s 27(3) ("dies after 29 August 1938"). None of them says what
--   happens to a testator who dies ON the named day. s 5(9) makes the gap
--   visible: its first limb excludes a death "before 26 June 1992" and its
--   second includes a death "after that date", so the day itself is in
--   neither.
--   Readings: (1) strictly after, so a death on the day is outside the later
--   provision; (2) on or after, reading "dies after that date" as the
--   complement of "died before 26 June 1992".
--   TAKEN: (1), uniformly, by `falls strictly after` below. Reason: each of
--   these provisions confers something the general law did not — a second
--   route to formal validity (s 5), a saving of an attestation (s 10(3)), the
--   validation of an informal disposition of real estate and of a testamentary
--   guardian (s 27) — and a provision that confers is read no wider than its
--   words. Reading (2) is available and would change the answer on exactly one
--   day per boundary; a case file on the boundary should be escalated rather
--   than answered from this module.
GIVEN `the date` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the day fixed by the Act` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`falls strictly after` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the date`
    WHEN NOTHING       THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST `the day` THEN Day `the day` GREATER THAN Day `the day fixed by the Act`

GIVEN `the date` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the day fixed by the Act` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`falls on or after` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the date`
    WHEN NOTHING       THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST `the day` THEN Day `the day` AT LEAST Day `the day fixed by the Act`

GIVEN `the earlier` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the later`   IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- FALSE where either date is missing. Every use below is a limb the party
-- asserting it must make out, so an unproved date defeats the limb rather
-- than being presumed either way.
`is proved to fall strictly before` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the earlier`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST `the first day` THEN
        CONSIDER `the later`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
        WHEN JUST `the second day` THEN Day `the first day` LESS THAN Day `the second day`

GIVEN `the date of birth` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
-- Leap-safe by construction: the YEAR is incremented and the day and month are
-- carried across, so the calendar does the work. `Date` is the lenient
-- constructor and rolls a 29 February birth forward to 1 March in a non-leap
-- twenty-first year, which is the ordinary reading of attaining 21 years of
-- age for a leap-day birth and is why `YMD` (which would refuse) is not used
-- here. `YMD` is used for the literals below, where refusing a transposed
-- month is what is wanted.
`the twenty-first anniversary of` `the date of birth` MEANS
    Date (DATE_DAY   `the date of birth`)
         (DATE_MONTH `the date of birth`)
         (DATE_YEAR  `the date of birth` PLUS 21)

GIVEN `the date` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
-- Six months on, CLAMPED to the last day of the target month.
--
-- REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V6. This used to be built by hand as
-- `Date (DATE_DAY d) (DATE_MONTH d PLUS 6) (DATE_YEAR d)`. `Date` is the
-- LENIENT constructor: it rolls a day that does not exist in the target month
-- FORWARD, so six months after 31 August 2020 came out as 3 March 2021 and an
-- application made on 2 March 2021 was reported as within the time allowed when
-- s 28(2)'s period had expired on any reading. The old comment addressed only
-- month overflow past 12, which was never the defect.
--
-- `add months` from `daydate` clamps instead: 31 August 2020 plus six months is
-- 28 February 2021, and 31 August 2023 plus six months is 29 February 2024. That
-- is the corresponding-date rule a period of months is measured by — there being
-- no 31 February, the month ends and so does the period — and it is what the
-- library already uses for anniversaries.
`six months after` `the date` MEANS `add months` `the date` 6

GIVEN `the dates` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the earliest of` `the dates` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the dates`
    WHEN EMPTY THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `the first` FOLLOWED BY `the rest` THEN
        CONSIDER `the earliest of` `the rest`
        WHEN NOTHING          THEN JUST `the first`
        WHEN JUST `the other` THEN IF   Day `the first` AT MOST Day `the other`
                                   THEN JUST `the first`
                                   ELSE JUST `the other`

GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
      `the day`    IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- AMBIGUITY A6 — s 26's "living at the time of the death of the testator",
-- where the issue died on the SAME DATE as the testator.
--   Readings: (1) a person who died on the testator's own date of death is not
--   shown to have been living at the moment of that death; (2) he is, unless
--   the contrary is shown, because a date is not a moment.
--   TAKEN: (1). Reason: family-domain records a DATE of death and not a time,
--   so reading (2) would have this module assert survivorship it has no
--   evidence for, and s 26 is an exception whose beneficiary must make it out.
--   A commorientes case is not answerable from a date and should be escalated.
`was living on` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the person`'s `vital status`
    WHEN `living`                            THEN TRUE
    WHEN `died on` `the date of death`       THEN Day `the date of death` GREATER THAN Day `the day`
    WHEN `dead, the date of death not known` THEN FALSE

GIVEN `the ancestor`  IS A Person
      `the reference` IS A STRING
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
-- Down the `children` edge only, and to any depth: family-domain's `Person`
-- has no upward edge, so this cannot cycle. s 26 needs it twice — to find the
-- donee among the testator's issue, and then to ask whether any of THAT
-- person's issue outlived the testator.
`the descendants bearing the reference` `the ancestor` `the reference` MEANS
    concat (map (GIVEN `the relation` YIELD
                     IF   `the relation`'s `the child`'s `the reference for this person` EQUALS `the reference`
                     THEN LIST (`the relation`'s `the child`)
                     ELSE `the descendants bearing the reference` (`the relation`'s `the child`) `the reference`)
                (`the ancestor`'s `children`))

GIVEN `the ancestor` IS A Person
      `the day`      IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`some descendant was living on` `the ancestor` `the day` MEANS
    any (GIVEN `the relation` YIELD
                `was living on` (`the relation`'s `the child`) `the day`
             OR `some descendant was living on` (`the relation`'s `the child`) `the day`)
        (`the ancestor`'s `children`)

GIVEN `the donee` IS A `Person or corporation`
GIVETH A STRING
-- The empty string for a donee who is not an individual. The Public Trustee, a
-- trust company and the Government have no `the reference for this person`,
-- and no rule in this Act turns on the identity of a donee who is not a human
-- being: s 10(1) avoids a gift to an attesting witness or the witness's
-- spouse, and s 26 saves a gift to a child or other issue.
`the reference for the donee` `the donee` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the donee`
    WHEN `an individual` `the individual` THEN `the individual`'s `the reference for this person`
    OTHERWISE ""


§§ `Section 4 — Will of infant invalid`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 4
--
-- A TOTAL ENUM, BECAUSE THE ABSENT CASE IS REAL. family-domain records both a
-- `date of birth` and a `the date of execution` as `MAYBE DATE`, and a probate
-- file may be missing either: family-domain's note on `the date of execution`
-- gives PAA ss 9-10 (a will lost, mislaid or destroyed and proved by copy,
-- draft or contents) as the live case, and such a will may carry no date at
-- all. A boolean would have to
-- give the same value to a will not proved to be an infant's and a will
-- proved to be an adult's, and those are different answers.
DECLARE `Whether the testator had attained 21 years of age when the will was made` IS ONE OF
    `under 21 years of age`
    `21 years of age or over`
    `not in evidence, the date of birth of the testator or the date of execution of the will not being known`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 4 — "No will made by any person under 21 years of age shall be valid"
GIVEN `the will` IS A Will
GIVETH A `Whether the testator had attained 21 years of age when the will was made`
`the age of the testator when the will was made` `the will` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the will`'s `the testator`'s `date of birth`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `not in evidence, the date of birth of the testator or the date of execution of the will not being known`
    WHEN JUST `the date of birth` THEN
        CONSIDER `the will`'s `the date of execution`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN `not in evidence, the date of birth of the testator or the date of execution of the will not being known`
        WHEN JUST `the date of execution` THEN
            IF   Day `the date of execution` LESS THAN Day (`the twenty-first anniversary of` `the date of birth`)
            THEN `under 21 years of age`
            ELSE `21 years of age or over`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 4
GIVEN `the will` IS A Will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4 — will of infant invalid` `the will` MEANS
        "No will made by any person under 21 years of age shall be valid."
    ... `the age of the testator when the will was made` `the will` EQUALS `under 21 years of age`

-- AMBIGUITY A1 — s 4 where the age when the will was made is not in evidence.
--   Readings: (1) s 4 is a disqualification, so the will stands unless
--   infancy is PROVED; (2) validity must be affirmatively established, so an
--   unproved age fails the limb. A single boolean cannot hold both answers:
--   not-proved-to-be-an-infant and proved-to-be-an-adult are different.
--   TAKEN: (2), by making the affirmative limb below require
--   `21 years of age or over` rather than merely NOT `under 21 years of age`.
--   Reason: the Act frames formal validity negatively throughout — "No will
--   shall be valid unless" (s 6(1)), "No appointment made by will, in exercise
--   of any power, shall be valid, unless the will is executed in the manner
--   required by this Act" (s 7(1)) — which puts the burden on the propounder,
--   and s 4 is in the same voice. The aggregate below does NOT report such a will as
--   invalid; it reports that the question cannot be answered, so the gap stays
--   visible instead of being resolved silently either way.
--
--   THE NEIGHBOURING ACTS ANSWER THE SAME EVIDENTIAL QUESTION THE OTHER WAY,
--   AND NOTHING SAID SO UNTIL NOW (CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X5). The question "this
--   person's age is not in evidence — now what?" is put to three of the four
--   modules and gets three different answers, each argued at its own site and
--   none of them until now aware of the others:
--     THIS ACT   a THIRD VALUE. `Whether the testator had attained 21 years of
--                age when the will was made` has a member
--                `not in evidence, ...`, and `The formal validity of the will`
--                carries a distinct verdict for it. Neither limb is made out.
--     THE PAA    FALSE. `is an infant on` folds a missing date of majority to
--                FALSE (its AMBIGUITY A2), so an unproved age passes the
--                s 21(1) bar.
--     THE GIA    NOTHING. Its infancy predicate returns `MAYBE BOOLEAN` and
--                propagates the absence rather than resolving it.
--   WHY THIS ACT IS RIGHT TO DIFFER, and it is not merely a matter of taste.
--   (i) The PAA can take its reading because ITS OWN s 6(3) routes the
--   evidential question to a forum: it lets the court "act on such prima facie
--   evidence ... as to whether or not there is a minority or life interest, as
--   may be prescribed by rules made under any written law" (checked against
--   source/PAA1934.txt in this directory, s 6(3); see the amended note on the
--   source at the head of this file). The Wills Act has no analogue: nothing in it names a
--   forum for finding a testator's age, and s 4 is addressed to whether the
--   will is valid at all, not to whether a particular applicant may be granted
--   something today.
--   (ii) The two questions are not even the same question. The PAA and the GIA
--   both ask about an ALREADY-CONSTRUED age of majority supplied from outside
--   the four Acts — the shared `the date on which the person attains the age of
--   majority`, which family-domain expressly forbids this module to read. s 4
--   asks about a number the Wills Act states itself, "under 21 years of age",
--   computed here from `date of birth` and `the date of execution`. A fold to
--   FALSE would report a will as NOT made by an infant when nobody knows when
--   the testator was born.
--   NEITHER OF THE OTHER TWO IS SAID TO BE WRONG. Each is defensible on its own
--   Act, and what the cross-module audit found missing was the cross-reference,
--   not a decision. It is recorded here so that a reader who has just read the
--   PAA does not take this module's third value for an oversight, and so that a
--   later editor tempted to "make the four consistent" can see that the
--   inconsistency is in the SHAPE of the answer and is argued.
@ref Wills Act 1838 s 4
GIVEN `the will` IS A Will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4 — the testator is proved to have been 21 years of age or over` `the will` MEANS
    `the age of the testator when the will was made` `the will` EQUALS `21 years of age or over`


§§ `Section 5 — Rules as to formal validity`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(2)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed` `the case` MEANS
        "A will shall be treated as properly executed if its execution conformed to the internal law in force —"
    ..  "(a)" ... `the connecting factors`'s `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where it was executed`
    ..  "(b)(i)" ... `the connecting factors`'s `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator was domiciled at the time when the will was executed`
    ..  "(b)(ii)" ... `the connecting factors`'s `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator was domiciled at the time of his death`
    ..  "(c)" ... `the connecting factors`'s `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator habitually resided at either of the times referred to in paragraph (b)`
    OR  "(d)" ... `the connecting factors`'s `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the state of which the testator was a national at either of the times referred to in paragraph (b)`
    WHERE
        `the connecting factors` MEANS
            `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `the conformity of its execution to a law outside this Act`

-- s 5(3) IS SPLIT IN TWO, AND THE SPLIT IS "SO FAR AS" (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL
-- REVIEW V4). Of the subsection's four paragraphs, only (a) speaks of "a will"
-- without qualification. The other three each begin "a will so far as it" — so
-- far as it disposes of immovable property, so far as it revokes an earlier
-- will or provision, so far as it exercises a power of appointment — and treat
-- the will as properly executed only to that extent. They were previously
-- OR'ed together with (a) into one whole-will boolean, which fed
-- `the will is properly executed` and `the formal validity of the will`, so a
-- will that failed s 6 in every particular and conformed to no law except the
-- lex situs of one piece of land came out simply `valid`. The field NAMES kept
-- the "so far as"; the disjunction lost it.
--
-- "Without prejudice to subsection (2)": both halves stand whether or not any
-- limb of s 5(2) is made out. So s 5(3) is a disjunction BESIDE s 5(2), never a
-- qualification of it.

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(3)(a)
--
-- THE WHOLE-WILL PARAGRAPH. A will executed on board a vessel or aircraft is
-- treated as properly executed, full stop; there is no "so far as" in (a).
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(3)(a) — a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft is treated as properly executed` `the case` MEANS
        "Without prejudice to subsection (2), the following shall be treated as properly executed:"
    ... "(a)" ... `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `the conformity of its execution to a law outside this Act`'s `the will was executed on board a vessel or an aircraft of any description, and the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory with which, having regard to its registration (if any) and other relevant circumstances, the vessel or aircraft may be taken to have been most closely connected`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(3)(b), s 5(3)(c), s 5(3)(d)
--
-- THE THREE PARTIAL PARAGRAPHS. Each treats the will as properly executed
-- SO FAR ONLY AS it does the thing the paragraph names, and no further. The
-- disjunction below therefore answers whether the will is properly executed as
-- to some part of it, and the answer is reported under its own name by
-- `s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` rather
-- than being poured into a whole-will verdict.
--
-- WHICH PART IS NOT COMPUTED, and that is the same limit as AMBIGUITY A8 on
-- s 15's "or any part thereof": nothing in the shared ontology divides a will
-- into parts, so there is nothing for a per-part answer to range over. What is
-- new here is that the partiality is REPORTED instead of being flattened away.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(3)(b)-(d) — the will is treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of it` `the case` MEANS
        "Without prejudice to subsection (2), the following shall be treated as properly executed:"
    ..  "(b)" ... `the connecting factors`'s `so far as the will disposes of immovable property, its execution conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the property was situated`
    ..  "(c)" ... `the connecting factors`'s `so far as the will revokes a will which under this Act would be treated as properly executed, or revokes a provision which under this Act would be treated as comprised in a properly executed will, the execution of the later will conformed to any law by reference to which the revoked will or provision would be treated as properly executed`
    OR  "(d)" ... `the connecting factors`'s `so far as the will exercises a power of appointment, the execution of the will conformed to the law governing the essential validity of the power`
    WHERE
        `the connecting factors` MEANS
            `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `the conformity of its execution to a law outside this Act`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(4)
--
-- THE DENIAL IDIOM (see the header). The antecedent — that the execution did
-- not comply with formal requirements in the instrument creating the power —
-- is deliberately not a field anywhere in this cleanroom, because s 5(4) and
-- s 7(2) between them give it no effect at all, and family-domain records only
-- facts that some provision operates on. So the antecedent is named here in
-- the statute's own words and the answer is FALSE.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(4) — the will is treated as improperly executed by reason only that its execution was not in accordance with formal requirements contained in the instrument creating the power` MEANS
        "A will so far as it exercises a power of appointment shall not be treated as improperly executed by reason only that its execution was not in accordance with any formal requirements contained in the instrument creating the power."
    ... FALSE

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(9)
-- See AMBIGUITY A2 on `falls strictly after` above for the day itself.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator` `the case` MEANS
        "This section shall not apply to a will of a testator who died before 26 June 1992 and shall apply to a will of a testator who dies after that date whether the will was executed before or after that date."
    ... `falls strictly after` (`the date of the death of` (`the testator of the will under consideration` `the case`))
                              (YMD 1992 6 26)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(2), s 5(3)
--
-- ANY LIMB AT ALL, LEAVING s 5(9) OUT. Read by the aggregate at the foot of
-- this module, and by nothing else, so that it can tell a will on which s 5 was
-- APPLIED AND FAILED from one on which s 5 was never reached — see the note on
-- `The formal validity of the will` (ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V7).
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5 — some connecting factor is made out, leaving aside the commencement in subsection (9)` `the case` MEANS
        `s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed` `the case`
    OR  `s 5(3)(a) — a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft is treated as properly executed` `the case`
    OR  `s 5(3)(b)-(d) — the will is treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of it` `the case`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5
--
-- A TOTAL ENUM, mirroring `The extent of the revival` at s 17(2): the section
-- does not merely treat a will as properly executed or not, it can treat it as
-- properly executed SO FAR ONLY AS it does one of three things, and a BOOLEAN
-- cannot hold the third answer. (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V4.)
DECLARE `The extent to which the will is treated as properly executed under section 5` IS ONE OF
    `not treated as properly executed under section 5`
    `treated as properly executed`
    `treated as properly executed so far only as the will disposes of immovable property, revokes a will or a provision, or exercises a power of appointment`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(1)-(3), s 5(9)
--
-- s 5(1) IS WHY THIS IS AN ALTERNATIVE TO s 6 AND NOT A GLOSS ON IT: "This
-- section shall take effect notwithstanding any other provisions of this Act."
-- A will that fails s 6 in every particular is nonetheless properly executed
-- if a whole-will limb of s 5(2) or s 5(3)(a) is made out and s 5(9) lets the
-- section apply.
--
-- THE WHOLE-WILL LIMBS ARE TESTED FIRST, because a will properly executed as a
-- whole is not also, and less usefully, properly executed as to a part.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A `The extent to which the will is treated as properly executed under section 5`
`s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` `the case` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT (`s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator` `the case`)
              THEN `not treated as properly executed under section 5`
           IF      `s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed` `the case`
               OR  `s 5(3)(a) — a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft is treated as properly executed` `the case`
              THEN `treated as properly executed`
           IF `s 5(3)(b)-(d) — the will is treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of it` `the case`
              THEN `treated as properly executed so far only as the will disposes of immovable property, revokes a will or a provision, or exercises a power of appointment`
           OTHERWISE `not treated as properly executed under section 5`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(1)-(3), s 5(9)
--
-- THE WHOLE-WILL ANSWER, which is what `the will is properly executed` and the
-- aggregate read. A will treated as properly executed so far only as regards a
-- part of it is NOT properly executed within the meaning of this decision, and
-- that is the point of the repair: the partial limbs no longer make a whole
-- will good.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed` `the case` MEANS
        "This section shall take effect notwithstanding any other provisions of this Act."
    ... `s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` `the case`
        EQUALS `treated as properly executed`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(1), s 5(2), s 5(3)(a), s 5(3)(d), s 5(9)
--
-- THE APPOINTMENT SLICE OF s 5, read by s 7(1) and by nothing else. It exists
-- because of AMBIGUITY A11, argued at s 7(1) below: s 5(1) says this section
-- takes effect "notwithstanding any other provisions of this Act", and s 7(1)
-- is one of them, so s 7(1) cannot pronounce an appointment invalid for want of
-- execution where s 5 has treated the will as properly executed as to that very
-- appointment.
--
-- WHICH LIMBS REACH THE APPOINTMENT, and it is not all of them. The two
-- whole-will routes do — s 5(2)'s five connecting factors and s 5(3)(a)'s
-- vessel or aircraft — because a will treated as properly executed as a whole is
-- treated as properly executed as to every part of it. Of the three "so far as"
-- paragraphs only (d) does: it is the one that names "a will so far as it
-- exercises a power of appointment". (b) reaches the will so far as it disposes
-- of immovable property and (c) so far as it revokes, and neither of those is
-- the appointment.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed so far as it exercises a power of appointment` `the case` MEANS
        "This section shall take effect notwithstanding any other provisions of this Act."
    ... "Without prejudice to subsection (2), the following shall be treated as properly executed:"
    ... "(d) a will so far as it exercises a power of appointment, if the execution of the will conformed to the law governing the essential validity of the power."
    ...     `s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator` `the case`
        AND (   `s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed` `the case`
             .. `s 5(3)(a) — a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft is treated as properly executed` `the case`
             OR `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `the conformity of its execution to a law outside this Act`'s `so far as the will exercises a power of appointment, the execution of the will conformed to the law governing the essential validity of the power`)


§§ `Section 6 — Mode of execution`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(2) — "and those witnesses shall subscribe the will in the presence of the testator"
--
-- AMBIGUITY A3 — whose subscription s 6(2) requires.
--   Readings: (1) EVERY attesting witness must have subscribed in the
--   testator's presence, since "those witnesses" refers to all who were
--   present; (2) at least TWO must have, since the subsection's requirement is
--   stated of the "2 or more witnesses present at the same time" and two is
--   the number it fixes.
--   TAKEN: (2). Reason: on reading (1) a supernumerary third witness who
--   signed later, outside the testator's presence, would unmake an execution
--   that was already complete when the second witness subscribed — and the
--   subsection nowhere makes the will's validity turn on the conduct of a
--   witness it does not require. Reading (1) is arguable and would change the
--   answer only where there are more than two attesting witnesses.
GIVEN `the will` IS A Will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`at least 2 of the attesting witnesses subscribed the will in the presence of the testator` `the will` MEANS
    count (filter (GIVEN `the attesting witness` YIELD
                       `the attesting witness`'s `subscribed the will in the presence of the testator`)
                  (`the will`'s `the attesting witnesses`))
    AT LEAST 2

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(3)
--
-- THE DEEMING AND THE ENUMERATION ARE TWO DIFFERENT THINGS and are two
-- decisions here. The first half of s 6(3) DEEMS the will valid so far as
-- regards the POSITION of the signature. The second half DENIES effect to five
-- circumstances. Only the first half has an operative output.
--
-- IT IS STATED BEFORE s 6(2) ONLY BECAUSE s 6(2) NOW READS IT, and L4 wants a
-- definition before its use. The Act's own order is s 6(1), s 6(2), s 6(3).
--
-- TWO CONJUNCTS, BECAUSE THE SUBSECTION HAS TWO (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW
-- V3). The deeming condition is that "the signature shall be so placed at or
-- after, or following, or under, or beside, or opposite to the end of the will,
-- that it shall be apparent on the face of the will that the testator intended
-- to give effect by such his signature to the writing signed as his will" — a
-- so-that construction, with a positional antecedent and an evidential
-- consequent, and both are required. The encoding used to carry the second
-- alone, which deemed valid a signature written above every dispositive word.
--
-- AND A THIRD CONJUNCT, WHICH IS THE SUBSECTION'S PRESUPPOSITION RATHER THAN
-- ONE OF ITS TWO CONDITIONS (REPAIR, ROUND 2, R11). What s 6(3) deems valid is
-- "the position of the signature of the testator, or of the person signing for
-- him as mentioned in subsection (2)": there must BE such a signature for its
-- position to be excused. That was carried by a field name alone, so a will
-- nobody signed could pass s 6(2)'s first limb through the deeming. It is
-- stated first because a presupposition is prior to the conditions.
GIVEN `the will and its further facts` IS A `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature` `the will and its further facts` MEANS
        "Every will shall, as far only as regards the position of the signature of the testator, or of the person signing for him as mentioned in subsection (2), be deemed to be valid under this section if the signature shall be so placed at or after, or following, or under, or beside, or opposite to the end of the will, that it shall be apparent on the face of the will that the testator intended to give effect by such his signature to the writing signed as his will;"
    ...     `the position`'s `there is a signature of the testator, or of a person signing for him in his presence and by his direction`
        AND `the position`'s `the signature of the testator, or of the person signing for him as mentioned in subsection (2), is so placed at or after, or following, or under, or beside, or opposite to the end of the will`
        AND `the will and its further facts`'s `the will`'s `apparent on the face of the will that the testator intended to give effect by such his signature to the writing signed as his will`
    WHERE
        `the position` MEANS `the will and its further facts`'s `the position of the signature`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(2), s 6(3)
--
-- THE THREE LIMBS ARE THE SUBSECTION'S THREE LIMBS, joined by its own "and",
-- in its own order. The closing words are carried as the fourth rung and
-- conjoin TRUE, because they REMOVE a requirement rather than adding one: an
-- encoding that dropped them would look identical, and a reader would not be
-- able to tell that the Act had considered attestation clauses and dispensed
-- with them.
--
-- s 6(3) IS WIRED INTO THE FIRST LIMB, and that is a repair (ADVERSARIAL
-- REVIEW V2). s 6(3) exists to relax exactly one thing — the requirement that
-- the will be "signed at the foot or end thereof" — by deeming it valid "as far
-- only as regards the position of the signature". The deeming used to be
-- computed and then read by nothing at all, so a will signed beside or opposite
-- the end failed the first
-- limb, failed s 6(1), and could not be rescued by the very subsection written
-- to rescue it. It now stands as a third alternative in the limb whose
-- requirement it relaxes.
--
-- IT RELAXES THE POSITION AND NOTHING ELSE. The other two limbs —
-- acknowledgement before two witnesses present at the same time, and their
-- subscription in the testator's presence — are untouched by it, and so is the
-- requirement that there be a signature of the testator or of a person signing
-- for him: the s 6(3) field says whose signature it is about.
GIVEN `the will and its further facts` IS A `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(2) — the manner of execution` `the will and its further facts` MEANS
        "Every will shall be signed at the foot or end thereof by the testator, or by some other person in his presence and by his direction,"
    ...     (   `the instrument`'s `signed at the foot or end thereof by the testator`
             OR `the instrument`'s `signed at the foot or end thereof by some other person in his presence and by his direction`
             OR `s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature` `the will and its further facts`)
    ... "and the signature shall be made or acknowledged by the testator as the signature to his will or codicil in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time,"
    ...     `the instrument`'s `the signature made or acknowledged by the testator as the signature to his will or codicil in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time`
    ... "and those witnesses shall subscribe the will in the presence of the testator,"
    ...     `at least 2 of the attesting witnesses subscribed the will in the presence of the testator` `the instrument`
    ... "but no form of attestation shall be necessary."
    AND TRUE
    WHERE
        `the instrument` MEANS `the will and its further facts`'s `the will`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(1)
--
-- "No will shall be valid unless" — a prohibition with an exception, and it is
-- encoded that way round. The propounder must make out both limbs; nothing
-- here reads as a permission.
GIVEN `the will and its further facts` IS A `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` AKA `executed in the manner required by this Act` MEANS
        "No will shall be valid unless it is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)."
    ...     `the will and its further facts`'s `the will`'s `in writing`
        AND `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` `the will and its further facts`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(3)(a)-(e), s 6(4)
--
-- THE DENIAL IDIOM. Every one of the five circumstances is named, the
-- disjunction of them is computed, and the answer is FALSE. s 6(4)'s opening
-- words — "The enumeration of the circumstances under subsection (3) shall not
-- restrict the generality of that subsection" — ride here rather than in the
-- s 6(4) rule below, because they are about this list.
--
-- THE LIST IS NAMED APART FROM THE DENIAL (ROUND 2), for the same reason s 9's
-- antecedent is: `#ASSERT NOT` on a rule that conjoins FALSE holds whatever the
-- list computes, so the five paragraphs had no test of their own.
GIVEN `the position` IS A `The position of the signature`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`one or more of the circumstances mentioned in paragraphs (a) to (e) is present` `the position` MEANS
        "(a)" ... `the position`'s `the signature does not follow and is not immediately after the foot or end of the will`
    ..  "(b)" ... `the position`'s `a blank space intervenes between the concluding word of the will and the signature`
    ..  "(c)" ... `the position`'s `the signature is placed among the words of the testimonium clause or of the clause of attestation, or follows or is after or under the clause of attestation, either with or without a blank space intervening, or follows or is after, or under, or beside the names or one of the names of the subscribing witnesses`
    ..  "(d)" ... `the position`'s `the signature is on a side or page or other portion of the paper or papers containing the will whereon no clause or paragraph or disposing part of the will is written above the signature`
    OR  "(e)" ... `the position`'s `there appears to be sufficient space on or at the bottom of the preceding side or page or other portion of the same paper on which the will is written to contain the signature`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(3)(a)-(e), s 6(4)
GIVEN `the position` IS A `The position of the signature`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(3)(a)-(e) — the will is affected by any of the enumerated circumstances` `the position` MEANS
        "and no such will shall be affected by the circumstance —"
    ...     `one or more of the circumstances mentioned in paragraphs (a) to (e) is present` `the position`
    ... "The enumeration of the circumstances under subsection (3) shall not restrict the generality of that subsection;"
    AND FALSE

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 6(4)
--
-- THE ONLY PROVISION IN THE ACT THAT BITES ON ONE DISPOSITION. It is stated
-- positively here — whether the signature is operative to give effect to this
-- disposition — because that is the question the estate has to answer, and the
-- subsection's two negatives are its two disqualifying facts.
GIVEN `the disposition and its position` IS A `A disposition, and where it stands in relation to the signature`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(4) — the signature is operative to give effect to the disposition` `the disposition and its position` MEANS
        "but no signature under this Act shall be operative to give effect to any disposition or direction which is underneath or which follows it, nor shall it give effect to any disposition or direction inserted after the signature shall be made."
    ... NOT (   `the disposition and its position`'s `underneath or following the signature`
             OR `the disposition and its position`'s `inserted after the signature was made`)


§§ `The will is properly executed — s 5 or s 6`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 5(1), s 6(1)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the will is properly executed` `the case` MEANS
        `s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed` `the case`
    OR  `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` (`the case`'s `the will in question`)


§§ `Section 7 — Execution of appointment by will`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 7(1)
--
-- "No appointment made by will, in exercise of any power, shall be valid,
-- unless the will is executed in the manner required by this Act."
--
-- REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V5. This rule used to be named
-- `s 7(1) — the appointment made by the will in exercise of a power IS VALID`
-- and to return (an appointment was made) AND (s 6(1)) — the shall-not-unless
-- to may-if inversion, which turns a NECESSARY condition into a SUFFICIENT one
-- and puts the burden on the wrong party. It
-- was measurably wrong, not merely mis-shaped: for a will made by a testator
-- under 21 but formally executed, the old rule said the appointment was valid
-- while `the formal validity of the will` said the will was void under s 4, and
-- an appointment by a void will is no appointment.
--
-- So the section's own proposition is what is named and returned: the
-- appointment IS INVALID where one is made by a will not executed in the manner
-- required by this Act. FALSE where the will makes no such appointment at all —
-- there is then no appointment to invalidate — and FALSE where the will is
-- executed as this Act requires, which is as far as s 7(1) goes. Whether an
-- appointment that clears s 7(1) is valid depends on the rest of the Act (s 4
-- among others) and on the instrument creating the power, and s 7(1) does not
-- say.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A11 — whether s 5 can save an appointment from s 7(1), and it is
-- the polarity flip above that made this bite (REPAIR, ROUND 2, R2).
--   s 7(1) invalidates an appointment "unless the will is executed in the
--   manner required by this Act". s 5(1) says "This section shall take effect
--   notwithstanding any other provisions of this Act", and s 5(3)(d) treats "a
--   will so far as it exercises a power of appointment" as properly executed
--   where its execution conformed to the law governing the essential validity
--   of the power.
--   Readings: (1) s 7(1) still bites, because "executed in the manner required
--   by this Act" is s 6, whereas s 5 speaks of a will "treated as properly
--   executed" — a different phrase, on this reading for a different thing;
--   (2) s 7(1) cannot
--   bite, because s 5(1)'s notwithstanding stops every other provision of the
--   Act operating against s 5, and pronouncing the appointment invalid for want
--   of execution is exactly that.
--   TAKEN: (2), and it is a conjunct below. Reason: a notwithstanding clause
--   does not redefine the other section's words, it stops the other section
--   taking effect against it — so reading (1) can be right about the PHRASE and
--   still wrong about the RESULT. And the result under reading (1) was a
--   contradiction on one case file, which is what makes this a repair and not
--   merely a fork: for a will conforming only to the law governing the
--   essential validity of the power, the module reported both that the will was
--   `treated as properly executed so far only as ... exercises a power of
--   appointment` and that the appointment was invalid for want of execution.
--   Under the AFFIRMATIVE rule this replaced, the same case merely returned
--   FALSE — uninformative rather than contradictory — which is why the defect
--   arrived with the repair that fixed the polarity.
--   Reading (1) is defensible and is what the note on s 7(2) below used to
--   assert as settled. It differs from reading (2) only where s 5 is made out
--   and s 6 is not, which is the foreign-execution case s 5 was inserted for.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 7(1) — the appointment made by the will in exercise of a power is invalid for want of execution in the manner required by this Act` `the case` MEANS
        "No appointment made by will, in exercise of any power, shall be valid, unless the will is executed in the manner required by this Act."
    ...     (`the will under consideration` `the case`)'s `made in exercise of a power of appointment`
        AND NOT (`s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` (`the case`'s `the will in question`))
        AND NOT (`s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed so far as it exercises a power of appointment` `the case`)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 7(2)
--
-- The "notwithstanding" limb is the twin of s 5(4) and is denied there; here
-- the subsection's positive content is stated.
--
-- THIS RULE DOES NOT READ THE s 5 ROUTE, AND s 7(1) NOW DOES — the two are not
-- inconsistent, and an earlier version of this note said something stronger
-- that was (ROUND 2, R2). It said that "s 7(1) and s 7(2) do NOT read the s 5
-- route: both say 'executed in the manner required by this Act', which is s 6,
-- and s 5 speaks of a will 'treated as properly executed'", and stated it as
-- settled rather than as one side of AMBIGUITY A11. What s 5(1)'s
-- notwithstanding does is stop another provision taking effect AGAINST s 5. It
-- therefore has something to disarm in s 7(1), which pronounces an appointment
-- invalid, and nothing to disarm here, where the subsection only CONFERS —
-- declining to extend a conferral is uninformative, not contradictory. So
-- s 7(2) is left on s 6 alone, and a will treated as properly executed under
-- s 5 and not executed under s 6 gets no answer from this rule either way.
GIVEN `the will and its further facts` IS A `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 7(2) — the will is, so far as respects the execution and attestation thereof, a valid execution of a power of appointment by will` `the will and its further facts` MEANS
        "Every will executed in the manner required by this Act shall, so far as respects the execution and attestation thereof, be a valid execution of a power of appointment by will, notwithstanding that it shall have been expressly required that a will made in exercise of that power should be executed with some additional or other form of execution or solemnity."
    ... `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` `the will and its further facts`


§§ `Section 8 — Publication of will not necessary`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 8
-- The section removes a requirement, so nothing anywhere in this module
-- records a publication. It is stated as a decision so that the removal is
-- visible rather than being an absence a reader has to notice.
GIVEN `the will and its further facts` IS A `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 8 — the will is valid without any other publication thereof` `the will and its further facts` MEANS
        "Every will executed in the manner required by this Act shall be valid without any other publication thereof."
    ... `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` `the will and its further facts`


§§ `Sections 9, 10(2), 11 and 12 — the competency of an attesting witness`

-- These four sections are taken together, out of strict numerical order,
-- because they answer one question and family-domain's competency field is
-- defined by subtracting exactly these three subsections from it. The rest of
-- s 10 — the avoidance of the gift — is stated in its own section below.

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 9, s 10(2), s 11, s 12
--
-- THE ACT DECIDES THREE SLICES OF COMPETENCY ITSELF and leaves the rest to the
-- law of evidence. family-domain says so on the field it declares — the
-- witness is `incompetent ... otherwise than by reason of sections 10(2), 11
-- and 12` — so this module must apply those three subsections over the gift,
-- charged-debt and executor facts rather than reading a conclusion off a case
-- file. Each is a denial, and each is stated below.
GIVEN `the will`    IS A Will
      `the witness` IS AN `Attesting witness`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the attesting witness is to be admitted as a witness to prove the execution of the will` MEANS
    NOT `the witness`'s `incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof, otherwise than by reason of sections 10(2), 11 and 12`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 9 — "If any person who attests the execution of a will shall, at the time of the execution thereof or at any time afterwards, be incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof"
--
-- s 9's ANTECEDENT, NAMED SO THAT IT CAN BE ASSERTED (ROUND 2). The denial
-- idiom conjoins FALSE, so a `#ASSERT NOT` on the denial itself holds whatever
-- the antecedent computes — which left the traversal below with no test at all,
-- and made the antecedent-FALSE half of every denial pair a tautology twice
-- over. Named, it takes assertions on both sides and the denial keeps only the
-- work of denying.
GIVEN `the will` IS A Will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a person who attests the execution of the will is incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof` `the will` MEANS
    any (GIVEN `the attesting witness` YIELD
             NOT (`the attesting witness is to be admitted as a witness to prove the execution of the will` `the will` `the attesting witness`))
        (`the will`'s `the attesting witnesses`)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 9
GIVEN `the will` IS A Will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 9 — the will is invalid by reason that a person who attests its execution is incompetent to be admitted a witness` `the will` MEANS
        "If any person who attests the execution of a will shall, at the time of the execution thereof or at any time afterwards, be incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof, the will shall not on that account be invalid."
    ...     `a person who attests the execution of the will is incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof` `the will`
        AND FALSE

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 10(1), s 10(3) — "a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1)"
--
-- s 10(3)'S OWN PHRASE FOR s 10(1)'S CLASS, defined here because s 10(2) is
-- the first provision to need it and L4 wants a definition before its use. It
-- is used again by s 10(1) and s 10(3) below. Two limbs because family-domain
-- keeps the gift to the witness and the gift to the witness's wife or husband
-- apart: they are separately provable, and s 10(1) avoids the gift "so far
-- only as concerns" whichever of them took it.
GIVEN `the witness` IS AN `Attesting witness`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1)` `the witness` MEANS
        `the witness`'s `given or made a beneficial devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment by the will, other than and except charges and directions for the payment of any debt`
    OR  `the witness`'s `the wife or husband of the witness given or made such a beneficial devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 10(2)
GIVEN `the witness` IS AN `Attesting witness`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10(2) — the gift is a ground on which the attesting witness is not to be admitted a witness` `the witness` MEANS
        "The attesting witness referred to in subsection (1) shall be admitted as a witness to prove the execution of the will or to prove the validity or invalidity thereof, notwithstanding the devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment mentioned in the will."
    ...     `a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1)` `the witness`
        AND FALSE

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 11
GIVEN `the witness` IS AN `Attesting witness`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 11 — the charge is a ground on which the creditor is not to be admitted a witness` `the witness` MEANS
        "In case by any will any real or personal estate shall be charged with any debt, and any creditor, or the wife or husband of any creditor, whose debt is so charged, shall attest the execution of the will, the creditor notwithstanding the charge shall be admitted a witness to prove the execution of the will or to prove the validity or invalidity thereof."
    ...     `the witness`'s `a creditor, or the wife or husband of a creditor, whose debt is charged on the estate by the will`
        AND FALSE

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 12
--
-- WHETHER THIS WITNESS IS AN EXECUTOR IS MEMBERSHIP, NOT A FLAG.
-- family-domain deliberately carries no `an executor of the will` boolean on
-- `Attesting witness`: the fact is membership of `the executors appointed by
-- the will`, matched by `the reference for this person`, and a second copy
-- could disagree with the first.
GIVEN `the will`    IS A Will
      `the witness` IS AN `Attesting witness`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the attesting witness is an executor appointed by the will` MEANS
    any (GIVEN `the executor` YIELD
             `the executor`'s `the executor`'s `the reference for this person`
             EQUALS `the witness`'s `the witness`'s `the reference for this person`)
        (`the will`'s `the executors appointed by the will`)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 12
GIVEN `the will`    IS A Will
      `the witness` IS AN `Attesting witness`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 12 — being an executor of the will is a ground on which the person is not to be admitted a witness` MEANS
        "No person shall, on account of his being an executor of a will, be incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution of the will or to prove the validity or invalidity thereof."
    ...     `the attesting witness is an executor appointed by the will` `the will` `the witness`
        AND FALSE


§§ `Section 10 — Gifts to attesting witness or to wife or husband of attesting witness to be void`

-- s 10(2) is stated above, with ss 9, 11 and 12. What is left is the avoidance
-- of the gift (s 10(1)), the saving of a superfluous attestation (s 10(3)) and
-- the commencement of that saving (s 10(4)).

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 10(4)
-- See AMBIGUITY A2 on `falls strictly after` for a testator dying on
-- 29 May 1992 itself.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10(4) — subsection (3) applies to the will of this testator` `the case` MEANS
        "Subsection (3) shall apply to the will of any person dying after 29 May 1992, whether executed before or after 29 May 1992."
    ... `falls strictly after` (`the date of the death of` (`the testator of the will under consideration` `the case`))
                              (YMD 1992 5 29)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 10(3) — "if the will is duly executed without his attestation and without that of any other such person"
--
-- AMBIGUITY A9 — how much of s 6 "duly executed" imports.
--   Readings: (1) the whole of s 6(1)-(2), re-asked with the benefiting
--   witnesses struck out; (2) only the attestation requirements, the writing
--   and signature limbs being unaffected by who attested.
--   TAKEN: (1). Reason: "duly executed" is the Act's own phrase for
--   compliance with s 6 as a whole (s 16(2) uses "deemed to be duly executed"
--   of exactly that), and on reading (2) an unsigned will with three witnesses
--   could satisfy s 10(3) while failing s 6 — which would save an attestation
--   for a will that has none to save. The two readings differ only where the
--   will fails s 6 for a reason having nothing to do with the witnesses, in
--   which case the gift falls with the will anyway.
--
-- THE PRESENCE LIMB IS THE ONE FACT THAT CANNOT BE RECOMPUTED, and it is a
-- field on the wrapper for that reason; see the note on the DECLARE. The
-- COUNT of surviving witnesses and their subscription in the testator's
-- presence are computed here.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the will is duly executed without the attestation of any such person` `the case` MEANS
        (`the will under consideration` `the case`)'s `in writing`
    AND (   (`the will under consideration` `the case`)'s `signed at the foot or end thereof by the testator`
         OR (`the will under consideration` `the case`)'s `signed at the foot or end thereof by some other person in his presence and by his direction`
         OR `s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature` (`the case`'s `the will in question`))
    AND `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `the signature was made or acknowledged by the testator as the signature to his will or codicil in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time, counting only witnesses to whom and to whose wife or husband no such disposition is given or made`
    AND count (filter (GIVEN `the attesting witness` YIELD
                              NOT (`a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1)` `the attesting witness`)
                          AND `the attesting witness`'s `subscribed the will in the presence of the testator`)
                      ((`the will under consideration` `the case`)'s `the attesting witnesses`))
        AT LEAST 2

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 10(3)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10(3) — the attestation of such a person is disregarded for the purposes of subsection (1)` `the case` MEANS
        "The attestation of a will by a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1) shall be disregarded for the purposes of that subsection if the will is duly executed without his attestation and without that of any other such person."
    ...     `s 10(4) — subsection (3) applies to the will of this testator` `the case`
        AND `the will is duly executed without the attestation of any such person` `the case`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 10(1)
--
-- STATED TWICE, AT THE TWO PLACES IT BITES.
--
-- (i) PER WITNESS. s 10(1) avoids the gift "so far only as concerns the
--     person attesting the execution of the will, or the wife or husband of
--     that person, or any person claiming under that person or wife or
--     husband", so the class of persons who lose is defined from the witness.
--     This decision says whether this witness's attestation costs him (or his
--     spouse) what the will gave.
--
-- (ii) PER DISPOSITION. The estate has to know which gifts fall. This
--     decision matches a `Disposition`'s donee to an attesting witness by
--     `the reference for this person` and applies the same test.
--
-- AND THE SPOUSE CASE REACHES ONLY (i). family-domain's `Marriage` is rooted
-- at the deceased, so there is no edge from an attesting witness to that
-- witness's wife or husband; the gift to the spouse is recorded as a boolean
-- on the witness and cannot be tied to a particular `Disposition`. So (ii)
-- avoids a gift whose donee IS the witness, and a gift to the witness's spouse
-- is reported by (i) alone. This is a limit of the shared ontology, not a
-- reading of s 10(1), and a file in which a witness's spouse takes under the
-- will must apply (i) by hand to the disposition concerned.
GIVEN `the case`    IS A `A case under this Act`
      `the witness` IS AN `Attesting witness`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10(1) — the gift to the attesting witness or to the witness’s wife or husband is utterly null and void` MEANS
        "If any person attests the execution of any will to whom or to whose wife or husband any beneficial devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment of or affecting any real or personal estate, other than and except charges and directions for the payment of any debt, shall be thereby given or made, the devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment shall, so far only as concerns the person attesting the execution of the will, or the wife or husband of that person, or any person claiming under that person or wife or husband, be utterly null and void."
    ...     `a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1)` `the witness`
        AND NOT (`s 10(3) — the attestation of such a person is disregarded for the purposes of subsection (1)` `the case`)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 10(1)
GIVEN `the case`        IS A `A case under this Act`
      `the disposition` IS A Disposition
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void` MEANS
        "any beneficial devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment of or affecting any real or personal estate, other than and except charges and directions for the payment of any debt"
    ...     `the disposition`'s `beneficial`
        AND NOT `the disposition is a charge or direction for the payment of a debt`
        AND any (GIVEN `the attesting witness` YIELD
                        `the attesting witness`'s `the witness`'s `the reference for this person` EQUALS `the reference for the donee` (`the disposition`'s `the donee`)
                    AND `s 10(1) — the gift to the attesting witness or to the witness’s wife or husband is utterly null and void` `the case` `the attesting witness`)
                ((`the will under consideration` `the case`)'s `the attesting witnesses`)
    WHERE
        `the disposition is a charge or direction for the payment of a debt` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the disposition`'s `the kind of disposition`
            WHEN `a charge or direction for the payment of a debt` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE


§§ `Section 13 — Will to be revoked by marriage except in certain cases`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 13(1) — the exception
--
-- AMBIGUITY A4 — the force of "when" in s 13(1)'s exception.
--   "except a will made in exercise of a power of appointment, when the real
--   or personal estate thereby appointed would not in default of such
--   appointment pass to his or her heir, executor or administrator or the
--   person entitled under the Intestate Succession Act 1967."
--   Readings: (1) "when" states a FURTHER CONDITION, so the exception saves
--   only an appointing will whose appointed estate would not fall to the
--   statutory takers in default; (2) "when" is descriptive, equivalent to
--   *in which case*, so every will made in exercise of a power escapes
--   revocation.
--   TAKEN: (1), and it is encoded as a conjunction. Reason: on reading (2) the
--   whole of the second clause does no work, and the point of the exception is
--   that revocation by marriage protects the family — which needs no
--   protection where the appointed property would never reach them.
--   family-domain records the second limb as a proved fact, because whether
--   the estate "would not in default of such appointment pass to" those takers
--   is a question about a counterfactual instrument, not about this estate.
GIVEN `the will` IS A Will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(1) — the exception for a will made in exercise of a power of appointment` `the will` MEANS
        "except a will made in exercise of a power of appointment, when the real or personal estate thereby appointed would not in default of such appointment pass to his or her heir, executor or administrator or the person entitled under the Intestate Succession Act 1967."
    ...     `the will`'s `made in exercise of a power of appointment`
        AND `the will`'s `the estate thereby appointed would not in default of such appointment pass to his or her heir, executor or administrator or the person entitled under the Intestate Succession Act 1967`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 13(1)
--
-- THE SUBSECTION'S OWN WORDS, AND NOTHING ELSE. Note what is NOT here: any
-- requirement that the marriage post-date the will. s 13(1) says nothing about
-- the order of the two events. The limitation is real but comes from the
-- general law of revocation, so it is a SEPARATE named decision below and a
-- reader can see that it is a gloss. family-domain flags the same point on
-- `Marriage`.
--
-- SUBSISTENCE IS NOT READ. A marriage that revoked a will and has since been
-- dissolved has still revoked it; s 17 requires re-execution or a codicil to
-- revive. So `subsisting at the death of the deceased ...` is deliberately
-- untouched by this rule, and family-domain says so on the field.
GIVEN `the will`     IS A Will
      `the marriage` IS A Marriage
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(1) — the will is revoked by the marriage` MEANS
        "Every will made by a man or woman shall be revoked by his or her marriage,"
    ... NOT (`s 13(1) — the exception for a will made in exercise of a power of appointment` `the will`)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 13(1)
--
-- THE GLOSS, NAMED AS ONE. FALSE where either date is missing, so an undated
-- will or an undated marriage does not revoke — which is the same discipline
-- as s 4's: the party asserting revocation must make it out.
GIVEN `the will`     IS A Will
      `the marriage` IS A Marriage
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the marriage was solemnised after the will was made` MEANS
    `is proved to fall strictly before` (`the will`'s `the date of execution`)
                                       (`the marriage`'s `the date of the solemnisation of the marriage`)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 13(2)
--
-- AMBIGUITY A5 — s 13(2) where the contemplated spouse is not identified.
--   family-domain carries two fields: that the will is expressed to be made in
--   contemplation of a marriage, and a `MAYBE STRING` reference for the person
--   with whom the marriage contemplated was to be solemnised. It says
--   expressly that this module must rule on the NOTHING case.
--   Readings: (1) the saving applies only to a marriage shown to BE the
--   marriage contemplated, so an unidentified contemplation saves nothing;
--   (2) where the testator married once and the will contemplates a marriage,
--   that marriage is the one contemplated.
--   TAKEN: (1). Reason: s 13(2) saves the will against "the solemnisation of
--   the marriage contemplated" and against no other, and the subsection's
--   closing words put it squarely in the plural-marriage cohort — "the first,
--   second or subsequent marriage of a person lawfully practising polygamy" —
--   where reading (2)'s premise — that there was only one marriage — is
--   precisely what cannot be assumed. Reading (2) is defensible on a
--   single-marriage file.
GIVEN `the will`     IS A Will
      `the marriage` IS A Marriage
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13(2) — the will is expressed to be made in contemplation of this marriage` MEANS
        "Notwithstanding subsection (1), where a will made on or after 29 August 1938 is expressed to be made in contemplation of a marriage, the will shall not be revoked by the solemnisation of the marriage contemplated; and this subsection shall apply notwithstanding that the marriage contemplated may be the first, second or subsequent marriage of a person lawfully practising polygamy."
    ...     `falls on or after` (`the will`'s `the date of execution`) (YMD 1938 8 29)
        AND `the will`'s `expressed to be made in contemplation of a marriage`
        AND `the marriage contemplated is this marriage`
    WHERE
        `the marriage contemplated is this marriage` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the will`'s `the reference for the person with whom the marriage contemplated was to be solemnised`
            WHEN NOTHING            THEN FALSE
            WHEN JUST `the intended` THEN `the intended` EQUALS `the marriage`'s `the spouse`'s `the reference for this person`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 13(1)-(2); and the general-law ordering gloss below
--
-- THE MIDDLE CONJUNCT IS NOT IN s 13. That the marriage was solemnised after
-- the will was made comes from the general law of revocation, not from either
-- subsection, and is argued at its own definition just above. It is named here
-- as well because THIS composite is what everything downstream reads, s 15(a)
-- included, and a bare citation of "s 13(1)-(2)" over a rule with a
-- non-statutory conjunct in it overstates its provenance. (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL
-- REVIEW V16(i).)
GIVEN `the will`     IS A Will
      `the marriage` IS A Marriage
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13 — the will is revoked by this marriage` MEANS
        `s 13(1) — the will is revoked by the marriage` `the will` `the marriage`
    AND `the marriage was solemnised after the will was made` `the will` `the marriage`
    AND NOT (`s 13(2) — the will is expressed to be made in contemplation of this marriage` `the will` `the marriage`)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 13
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the case` MEANS
    any (GIVEN `the marriage` YIELD
             `s 13 — the will is revoked by this marriage` (`the will under consideration` `the case`) `the marriage`)
        (`the case`'s `the deceased`'s `the family`'s `the marriages of the deceased`)


§§ `Section 14 — No will to be revoked by presumption from altered circumstances`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 14
--
-- THE DENIAL IDIOM in its purest form: the section has no antecedent to
-- record, because the presumption it abolishes is not a fact anybody proves.
-- Nothing in this cleanroom records an alteration in the testator's
-- circumstances, and this decision is where a reader learns that the omission
-- is the Act's and not the encoder's.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 14 — the will is revoked by a presumption of an intention on the ground of an alteration in circumstances` MEANS
        "No will shall be revoked by any presumption of an intention on the ground of an alteration in circumstances."
    ... FALSE


§§ `Section 15 — Revocation of will or codicil`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 15(d)
--
-- TWO ELEMENTS, AND BOTH ARE REQUIRED. The act — burning, tearing or otherwise
-- destroying, by the testator or by some person in his presence and by his
-- direction — and the animus revocandi. A will burnt by accident, or by a
-- stranger, or by the testator in a fit of temper without the intention of
-- revoking, is not revoked under this limb, and splitting the fields is what
-- lets the encoding say so.
GIVEN `the ways` IS A `The ways in which a will may have been revoked`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 15(d) — the will has been destroyed with the intention of revoking it` `the ways` MEANS
        "by the burning, tearing, or otherwise destroying the will by the testator, or by some person in his presence and by his direction, with the intention of revoking it."
    ...     (   `the ways`'s `burnt, torn, or otherwise destroyed by the testator`
             OR `the ways`'s `burnt, torn, or otherwise destroyed by some person in his presence and by his direction`)
        AND `the ways`'s `destroyed with the intention of revoking it`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 15
--
-- "shall be revoked otherwise than" — an EXHAUSTIVE list, and the encoding is
-- a closed disjunction for that reason. A mode of revocation not in (a) to (d)
-- is not a mode of revocation at all, which is why nothing here has an
-- "otherwise" limb.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A8 — "or any part thereof".
--   s 15 governs the revocation of a will "or any part thereof", and this
--   decision answers only whether the INSTRUMENT has been revoked.
--   Readings: (1) partial revocation is a separate question, asked of each
--   part; (2) it can be folded into the whole.
--   TAKEN: (1), and NOT ENCODED. Reason: nothing in the shared ontology
--   divides a will into parts — there is no clause, no paragraph, no
--   severable provision — so a per-part answer would have nothing to range
--   over. The one place partial revocation surfaces is s 17(2), whose fact
--   `partly revoked, and afterwards wholly revoked` is recorded and used. A
--   file in which only part of a will was revoked is outside what this module
--   can say, and is not to be answered by reading this decision as FALSE.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the case` MEANS
        "No will or codicil, or any part thereof, shall be revoked otherwise than —"
    ..  "(a)" ... `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the case`
    ..  "(b)" ... `the ways`'s `revoked by another will or codicil executed in the manner by this Act required`
    ..  "(c)" ... `the ways`'s `revoked by some writing declaring an intention to revoke it, and executed in the manner in which a will is by this Act required to be executed`
    OR  "(d)" ... `s 15(d) — the will has been destroyed with the intention of revoking it` `the ways`
    WHERE
        `the ways` MEANS `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `the ways in which it has been revoked`


§§ `Section 16 — Effect of obliteration, interlineation or alteration`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 16(2)
GIVEN `the alteration` IS AN `An alteration made in a will after its execution`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 16(2) — the will, with such alteration as part thereof, is deemed to be duly executed` `the alteration` MEANS
        "A will referred to in subsection (1), with such alteration as part thereof, shall be deemed to be duly executed if the signature of the testator and the subscription of the witnesses be made in the margin or on some other part of the will opposite or near to the alteration or at the foot or end of or opposite to a memorandum referring to the alteration and written at the end or some other part of the will."
    ..      `the alteration`'s `the signature of the testator and the subscription of the witnesses are made in the margin or on some other part of the will opposite or near to the alteration`
    OR      `the alteration`'s `the signature of the testator and the subscription of the witnesses are made at the foot or end of or opposite to a memorandum referring to the alteration and written at the end or some other part of the will`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 16(1)
--
-- AMBIGUITY A10 — "except so far as the words or effect of the will before
-- such alteration shall not be apparent".
--   Readings: (1) PRO TANTO — the alteration has effect only to the extent
--   that the earlier words are illegible, so a partial obliteration is partly
--   good and partly bad; (2) ALL OR NOTHING for a given alteration.
--   TAKEN: (2), because the decision below returns a BOOLEAN about one
--   alteration and nothing in the shared ontology divides an alteration into
--   the legible and illegible parts of what it struck out. This is the same
--   limit as AMBIGUITY A8 on s 15's "or any part thereof", and a case in which
--   an obliteration is only partly effective is outside what this module can
--   say. The pro tanto reading is the orthodox one and the encoding does not
--   contradict it; it declines to compute it.
--
-- The prohibition is "No obliteration, interlineation or other alteration
-- made in any will after the execution thereof shall be valid or have any
-- effect", and it has two escapes: "except so far as the words or effect of
-- the will before such alteration shall not be apparent", and "unless the
-- alteration shall be executed in the like manner as by this Act is required
-- for the execution of the will". They are independent: an obliteration that
-- makes the earlier words illegible takes effect whether or not it was
-- attested, and an attested alteration takes effect whether or not the earlier
-- words survive. So the encoding is a disjunction of the two escapes, and the
-- s 16(2) deeming is folded into the second.
GIVEN `the alteration` IS AN `An alteration made in a will after its execution`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 16(1) — the alteration is valid and has effect` `the alteration` MEANS
        "No obliteration, interlineation or other alteration made in any will after the execution thereof shall be valid or have any effect except so far as the words or effect of the will before such alteration shall not be apparent, unless the alteration shall be executed in the like manner as by this Act is required for the execution of the will."
    ..  NOT `the alteration`'s `the words or effect of the will before the alteration are apparent`
    OR      (   `the alteration`'s `the alteration is executed in the like manner as by this Act is required for the execution of the will`
             OR `s 16(2) — the will, with such alteration as part thereof, is deemed to be duly executed` `the alteration`)


§§ `Section 17 — Revival of revoked will`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 17(2)
--
-- A TOTAL ENUM NAMING THE ABSENT CASE. s 17(2) does not merely revive or not:
-- it revives in part, and the partial case has a name in the subsection's own
-- words. `MAYBE` would fold the no-revival case into a gap and lose the third
-- outcome altogether.
DECLARE `The extent of the revival` IS ONE OF
    `not revived`
    `revived, save as to so much thereof as was revoked before the revocation of the whole thereof`
    `revived in whole`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 17(1)
GIVEN `the revival` IS A `The revival of a revoked will`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 17(1) — the will has been revived` `the revival` MEANS
        "No will or codicil, or any part thereof, which shall be in any manner revoked, shall be revived otherwise than by the re-execution thereof, or by a codicil executed in the manner required by this Act and showing an intention to revive the will or codicil."
    ..      `the revival`'s `revived by the re-execution thereof`
    OR      `the revival`'s `revived by a codicil executed in the manner required by this Act and showing an intention to revive the will or codicil`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 17(1)-(2)
--
-- THE FIRST BRANCH IS s 17(1)'s OWN PREMISE: the section speaks of a will
-- "which shall be in any manner revoked", so a will that was never revoked is
-- not revived — there is nothing to revive. An encoding that omitted this
-- would report a re-executed but unrevoked will as "revived", which is not a
-- thing the Act says.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A `The extent of the revival`
`s 17 — the extent of the revival` `the case` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT (`s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the case`)      THEN `not revived`
           IF NOT (`s 17(1) — the will has been revived` `the revival`) THEN `not revived`
           IF      `the revival`'s `partly revoked, and afterwards wholly revoked`
               AND NOT `the revival`'s `an intention to the contrary is shown`
                                                                       THEN `revived, save as to so much thereof as was revoked before the revocation of the whole thereof`
           OTHERWISE                                                        `revived in whole`
    WHERE
        `the revival` MEANS `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `its revival`


§§ `Section 18 — Subsequent conveyance or other acts not to prevent operation of will`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 18; s 15(b)-(d)
--
-- THE EXCEPTION IS THE WHOLE RULE, AND IT IS AN EXCEPTION ABOUT ONE ACT.
-- s 18 says that a conveyance or other act done after the will does not stop
-- the will operating, "except an act by which the will shall be revoked as
-- aforesaid" — and "as aforesaid" is s 15. So the only subsequent act that can
-- prevent the operation of the will is one that revokes it.
--
-- REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V1. This rule used to conjoin the subsequent
-- conveyance with `s 15 — the will has been revoked`, which makes the two
-- merely CO-PRESENT rather than identical: a will revoked by MARRIAGE under
-- s 15(a), with some unrelated conveyance made afterwards, reported that the
-- conveyance had prevented the operation of the will — the exact opposite of
-- what s 18 protects, a marriage being no part of any "conveyance or other act
-- made or done subsequently to the execution of a will of or relating to any
-- real or personal estate therein comprised". The comment then stated the right
-- rule over the wrong code.
--
-- TWO CONJUNCTS NOW TIE THE EXCEPTION TO THE ACT. The first is the proved fact
-- that THIS conveyance is the act by which the will is revoked. The second is
-- "as aforesaid": the revocation must be in one of the ways s 15 allows, and
-- of s 15's four limbs only three can be worked by an act done "of or relating
-- to" the estate — (b) another will or codicil, (c) a writing declaring an
-- intention to revoke, (d) burning, tearing or otherwise destroying the will
-- with the intention of revoking it. s 15(a) is not among them: revocation by
-- marriage is not an act of that description, and reading it in is what
-- produced the defect.
--
-- THE FIRST CONJUNCT CANNOT BE ISOLATED, AND IS KEPT BECAUSE THE SUBSECTION
-- STATES IT (ROUND 2). It is s 18's own subject — that a conveyance or other act
-- has been made or done subsequently — and the second conjunct presupposes it,
-- since a fact saying THAT conveyance is the revoking act has no referent where
-- there is no conveyance. So on any well-formed file the second entails the
-- first, and no fixture below can make the first FALSE while the second is TRUE.
-- The same shape as the two limbs of s 26's "leaving issue": stated because the
-- Act states it, not because deleting it would change an answer.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 18 — a conveyance or other act made or done subsequently to the execution of the will prevents its operation` `the case` MEANS
        "No conveyance or other act made or done subsequently to the execution of a will of or relating to any real or personal estate therein comprised, except an act by which the will shall be revoked as aforesaid, shall prevent the operation of the will with respect to the estate or interest in the real or personal estate as the testator shall have power to dispose of by will at the time of his death."
    ...     `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `a conveyance or other act has been made or done subsequently to its execution, of or relating to any real or personal estate therein comprised`
        AND `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `that conveyance or other act is an act by which the will is revoked as aforesaid`
        AND (   `the ways`'s `revoked by another will or codicil executed in the manner by this Act required`
             .. `the ways`'s `revoked by some writing declaring an intention to revoke it, and executed in the manner in which a will is by this Act required to be executed`
             OR `s 15(d) — the will has been destroyed with the intention of revoking it` `the ways`)
    WHERE
        `the ways` MEANS `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `the ways in which it has been revoked`


§§ `Section 19 — Will to be construed to speak from death of testator`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 19
DECLARE `The time from which the will speaks` IS ONE OF
    `as if it had been executed immediately before the death of the testator`
    `otherwise, a contrary intention appearing by the will`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 19
--
-- The second member deliberately does not name a date of its own. s 19
-- supplies only a default and, where a contrary intention appears, says
-- nothing at all about what the will then speaks from — that is a question of
-- construing the particular contrary intention, which this module does not
-- attempt. Naming the outcome after the words that produce it, rather than
-- guessing at its content, keeps the encoding inside what the section says.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A `The time from which the will speaks`
`s 19 — the time from which the will is construed to speak` `the case` MEANS
    IF   `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `a contrary intention appears by the will as to the time from which it speaks`
    THEN `otherwise, a contrary intention appearing by the will`
    ELSE `as if it had been executed immediately before the death of the testator`


§§ `Section 26 — Gifts to children or other issue who leave issue living at testator’s death not to lapse`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 26 — "any person being a child or other issue of the testator"
--
-- DESCENT, NOT THE ISA'S DEFINED TERM. family-domain's `Child` carries ISA
-- s 3's two limbs — legitimacy and adoption — and neither is read here,
-- because ISA s 3 opens "In this Act" and defines "child" for the Intestate
-- Succession Act alone. s 26 says "a child or other issue of the testator" and
-- defines neither, so the test is descent down the `children` edge to any
-- depth: "or other issue" is what makes it any depth.
--
-- CHECKED AGAINST THE INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT ITSELF (CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X6),
-- because the cross-module audit put this reading and the Probate and
-- Administration Act's use of the same word beside each other and returned
-- "disagree". source/ISA1967.txt reads: "3. In this Act — “child” means a
-- legitimate child and includes any child adopted by virtue of an order of
-- court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore,
-- Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam;" and, three definitions later, "“issue”
-- includes children and the descendants of deceased children." The opening
-- words are "In this Act", and the Act they are in is the Intestate Succession
-- Act. source/WA1838.txt s 2 defines five terms — "internal law", "personal
-- estate", "real estate", "state" and "will" — and neither "child" nor "issue"
-- is among them. So the reading stands: nothing carries ISA s 3 across, and
-- this module does not carry it.
--
-- WHY THE NEIGHBOUR WAS NOT A COUNTER-EXAMPLE. The audit reported that
-- probate-administration-act.l4 disagreed with the Intestate Succession Act on
-- the same word: its ambiguity register borrowed that Act's distributive
-- classes BY NAME while its descent walk applied no definition of "child" at
-- all, so the two modules answered differently about the same person on the
-- same estate. That was never a reason to change anything here, because a
-- module that borrows another Act's classes by name has undertaken to borrow
-- that Act's definition of them too, or else to say why not — and THIS module
-- borrows nothing. s 26 uses its own undefined words in its own Act, so the
-- same raw traversal that owed an argument there is simply the right answer
-- here. The two modules do the same thing for opposite reasons, and only one
-- of them ever owed the argument.
-- RE-MEASURED AFTER THAT MODULE'S OWN CROSS-MODULE ROUND, and recorded because
-- the paragraph above would otherwise read as a live accusation: it has
-- resolved the point. It now carries a decision
-- `a “child” within the meaning of section 3 of the Intestate Succession Act
-- 1967`, applies it at every generation of the descent, and states in its own
-- register that "THE WILLS ACT MODULE TAKES THE OPPOSITE READING ON ITS OWN
-- s 26 AND IS RIGHT TO". The two sides now agree about why they differ. As
-- with every claim here about another file, this is a measurement of a moment.
--
-- WHAT THIS READING DOES NOT DECIDE, so that it is not read wider than it is.
-- It settles only that ISA s 3 does not GOVERN s 26. It does not settle
-- whether the general law construes "child or other issue" in a will as
-- confined to legitimate issue; that is a question of construction the Wills
-- Act does not answer and this module does not either. What the code does is
-- walk the descent recorded in `children`, whatever it records — so a case file
-- that means to exclude an illegitimate donee must exclude him when it builds
-- the family, and cannot rely on this module to do it.
GIVEN `the case`        IS A `A case under this Act`
      `the disposition` IS A Disposition
GIVETH A LIST OF Person
`the donee, so far as he is a child or other issue of the testator` MEANS
    `the descendants bearing the reference` (`the testator of the will under consideration` `the case`)
                                            (`the reference for the donee` (`the disposition`'s `the donee`))

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 26
--
-- FOUR OPERATIVE FACTS, in the section's own order. Three come from
-- family-domain — descent, "not determinable at or before the death of that
-- person", and the two survivorship comparisons off `Vital status` — and the
-- fourth, the contrary intention, is this module's, per section.
--
-- "leaving issue, and any such issue of that person shall be living at the
-- time of the death of the testator" is TWO limbs and both are encoded: the
-- donee must have left issue, and some such issue must have outlived the
-- testator.
--
-- THE SECOND LIMB ENTAILS THE FIRST AS MODELLED, AND THE ENCODING STATES BOTH
-- ANYWAY, BECAUSE THE ACT DOES (corrected, ROUND 2, R9). An earlier version of
-- this note claimed the two were separable, offering "issue born after the
-- donee's death — a posthumous grandchild" as issue that satisfy the second
-- limb and not the first. That is wrong on this ontology and on any reading: a
-- posthumous GRANDCHILD is the child of one of the donee's own children, so the
-- donee has issue recorded either way, and family-domain's `children` edge
-- carries a child whenever the child exists at all, with no birth date to
-- compare. `some descendant was living on` traverses that same edge, so it is
-- FALSE whenever the edge is empty: no case file can make the second limb TRUE
-- and the first FALSE, and no fixture below can therefore isolate the first.
-- It is kept because s 26 has two limbs and a reader holding the Act must find
-- both, not because deleting it would change an answer.
GIVEN `the case`        IS A `A case under this Act`
      `the disposition` IS A Disposition
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 26 — the devise or bequest shall not lapse` MEANS
        "Where any person being a child or other issue of the testator to whom any real or personal estate shall be devised or bequeathed for any estate or interest not determinable at or before the death of that person shall die in the lifetime of the testator leaving issue, and any such issue of that person shall be living at the time of the death of the testator, that devise or bequest shall not lapse, but shall take effect as if the death of that person had happened immediately after the death of the testator, unless a contrary intention shall appear by the will."
    -- The first conjunct is s 26's own subject — the donee must be "a child or
    -- other issue of the testator" — and it is SUBSUMED by the third, which is an
    -- `any` over the same list and is therefore FALSE whenever the list is empty.
    -- Kept for the same reason the two limbs of "leaving issue" are kept: the
    -- section says it. No fixture can isolate it. (ROUND 2.)
    ...     NOT (null `the donee as issue`)
        AND `the disposition`'s `not determinable at or before the death of the donee`
        AND `the same person died in the lifetime of the testator, left issue, and some such issue was living at the testator’s death`
        AND NOT `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `a contrary intention appears by the will as to the lapse of a devise or bequest to a child or other issue`
    WHERE
        `the donee as issue` MEANS
            `the donee, so far as he is a child or other issue of the testator` `the case` `the disposition`

        -- ALL THREE PERSON-FACTS ARE ASKED OF ONE PERSON, and that is deliberate
        -- rather than incidental. s 26 is about a single donee, who must
        -- "die in the lifetime of the testator leaving issue", and it is the
        -- issue OF THAT PERSON that must be living: "any such issue of that
        -- person shall be living at the time of the death of the testator".
        -- Asking each limb separately over the list would make them merely
        -- co-present, so one descendant who predeceased the testator and a
        -- different one who left surviving issue would together satisfy a
        -- section that requires one person to do both — the same defect the
        -- adversarial review found at s 18 (V1). `the donee as issue` should
        -- hold at most one person, references being identities, but a rule
        -- should not be sound only because its input happens to be a singleton.
        `the same person died in the lifetime of the testator, left issue, and some such issue was living at the testator’s death` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the date of the death of` (`the testator of the will under consideration` `the case`)
            WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
            WHEN JUST `the day of the death of the testator` THEN
                any (GIVEN `the person` YIELD
                             `is proved to fall strictly before` (`the date of the death of` `the person`)
                                                                 (JUST `the day of the death of the testator`)
                         AND NOT (null (`the person`'s `children`))
                         AND `some descendant was living on` `the person` `the day of the death of the testator`)
                    `the donee as issue`

-- WHERE THIS ACT STOPS, AND WHICH ACT PICKS IT UP (CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X7).
-- This is the last thing the Wills Act says about a gift: s 26 decides that a
-- devise or bequest to a child or other issue SHALL NOT LAPSE, and s 10(1)
-- above decides that a gift to an attesting witness is UTTERLY NULL AND VOID.
-- Both answer whether the gift takes effect. NEITHER ANSWERS WHETHER IT CAN BE
-- PAID OVER, and there is a provision of another of the four Acts that stands
-- exactly there.
--
-- GUARDIANSHIP OF INFANTS ACT 1934, s 17: "A guardian of the property of an
-- infant shall not, unless in any case the court otherwise orders, be
-- empowered to give a good discharge for any legacy or other capital moneys
-- payable to or receivable by an infant." (Quoted FIRST HAND from
-- source/GIA1934.txt in this directory, s 17.) A legacy is named in terms. So
-- where the donee whose gift survives s 26 is an infant, the personal
-- representative who pays it to the infant's guardian does not get a good
-- discharge and remains accountable, unless the court has ordered otherwise in
-- that case. guardianship-of-infants-act.l4 states this join from its own side
-- and says in terms that s 17 is this Act's direct bite on succession — that it
-- is what stands between an infant being entitled to a share and the share
-- being paid over. (That is a description of another MODULE as it stood at the
-- cross-module round, not a quotation of the Act.)
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE DOES ABOUT IT: NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE ANSWER RATHER THAN
-- A GAP. No provision of the Wills Act turns on who may give a discharge for a
-- legacy — the Act's subject is the instrument and what it disposes of, not
-- the administration of the estate — so there is no field for this module to
-- read and no rule for it to feed. It is recorded here because the
-- cross-module audit found the pointer running in only one direction, and a
-- silence on this side is not neutral: a reader of s 26 who is told nothing
-- cannot tell "the payability constraint was considered and belongs to another
-- Act" from "nobody noticed it".
--
-- THE THIRD SIDE OF THIS SEAM, MEASURED AND DATED. The audit found the same
-- silence over intestate-succession-act.l4, which computed shares and stopped.
-- Re-measured after that module's own cross-module round: it no longer is
-- silent — it carries a "SEAM 3" entry naming GIA s 17 against its own
-- entitlement, quotes s 17 in an `@ref`, and asserts the two `Guardian`
-- discharge fields on a fixture. So of the three modules the seam touches,
-- two now state it from their own side and this is the third.
--
-- (The Probate and Administration Act does not fill the gap either: it puts
-- its own ss 57-58 and both Schedules out of scope. So on the corpus as it
-- stands, s 26 says the gift stands and NOTHING anywhere says
-- what it takes to pay it — the GIA says only when a discharge is not good.)


§§ `Section 27 — Saving as to wills of soldiers and mariners`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 27(1), s 27(4), s 27(5)
--
-- THREE ROUTES IN, AND s 27(5) IS NOT A FOURTH. s 27(1) names the soldier in
-- actual military service and the mariner or seaman at sea; s 27(4) extends
-- the section to a member of any naval or marine forces "so circumstanced that
-- if he were a soldier he would be in actual military service". s 27(5) widens
-- the word "soldier" itself to include a member of an air force and a
-- serviceman of the digital and intelligence service of the Singapore Armed
-- Forces — so an airman in actual military service answers the FIRST field
-- TRUE, and s 27(5) is carried inert here rather than given a route of its
-- own. family-domain declares the three fields on exactly this footing.
GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 27 — this section applies to the person` `the person` MEANS
        "Notwithstanding anything in this Act, any soldier being in actual military service, or any mariner or seaman being at sea,"
    ..      `the person`'s `a soldier being in actual military service`
    ..      `the person`'s `a mariner or seaman being at sea`
    ..  "This section shall extend to any member of any naval or marine forces not only when he is at sea but also when he is so circumstanced that if he were a soldier he would be in actual military service within the meaning of this section."
    OR      `the person`'s `a member of any naval or marine forces so circumstanced that if he were a soldier he would be in actual military service`
-- s 27(5), which is not a fourth rung: "For the purposes of this section,
-- “soldier” includes a member of an air force and a serviceman serving in the
-- digital and intelligence service of the Singapore Armed Forces."
-- [Act 24 of 2022 wef 28/10/2022]

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 27(1)
--
-- "Notwithstanding anything in this Act" — so this route round s 4 and s 6 is
-- tested FIRST in the aggregate below, and neither the age bar nor the mode of
-- execution can defeat it. What the older law required of an informal
-- disposition of personal estate is not in this Act and is not computed; the
-- fact arrives proved.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the case` MEANS
        "Notwithstanding anything in this Act, any soldier being in actual military service, or any mariner or seaman being at sea, may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act and may do so even though under 21 years of age."
    ...     `s 27 — this section applies to the person` (`the testator of the will under consideration` `the case`)
        AND `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `made in such manner and form that if the disposition had been a disposition of personal estate made by that person it would have been valid`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 27(2)
--
-- THE REAL-ESTATE LIMB, and it carries a commencement the personal-estate limb
-- does not: the testator must have died after 29 August 1938. See AMBIGUITY A2
-- for the day itself. The subsection's two "notwithstanding" limbs — that the
-- testator was under 21 and that the form was not what the law required on
-- 29 August 1938 — are denials, and they are why neither `s 4` nor `s 6`
-- appears in this rule.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid` `the case` MEANS
        "A testamentary disposition of any real estate made by a person to whom this section applies, and who dies after 29 August 1938 shall, notwithstanding that the person making the disposition was at the time of making it under 21 years of age or that the disposition has not been made in such manner or form as was on 29 August 1938 required by law, be valid in any case where the person making the disposition was of such age and the disposition has been made in such manner and form that if the disposition had been a disposition of personal estate made by that person it would have been valid."
    ...     `s 27 — this section applies to the person` (`the testator of the will under consideration` `the case`)
        AND `falls strictly after` (`the date of the death of` (`the testator of the will under consideration` `the case`))
                                   (YMD 1938 8 29)
        AND `the case`'s `the will in question`'s `made in such manner and form that if the disposition had been a disposition of personal estate made by that person it would have been valid`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 27(3); Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(1)-(2), s 7(3)-(6)
--
-- ***** A JOIN TO THE GUARDIANSHIP OF INFANTS ACT 1934. *****
--
-- s 27(3) is the one place where the Wills Act creates something the GIA then
-- governs: "any appointment contained in that will of any person as guardian
-- of the infant children of the testator shall be of full force and effect".
-- The appointment itself is GIA s 7(1)-(2) — "The father of an infant may by
-- deed or will appoint any person to be guardian of the infant after his
-- death.", and the mirror for the mother. (Quoted FIRST HAND from
-- source/GIA1934.txt in this directory; see the note on the source at the head
-- of this file, which the cross-module round amended.) What s 27(3) adds is
-- that a will which would otherwise fail for want of form or for the
-- testator's infancy still makes that appointment good.
--
-- A GUARDIAN SINCE REMOVED IS STILL AN APPOINTMENT OF FULL FORCE AND EFFECT,
-- and that is why `the guardians of infant children appointed by the will` is
-- read here WITHOUT filtering out a `Guardian` whose `removed from his
-- guardianship by the court` is TRUE. s 27(3) asks whether the APPOINTMENT
-- contained in the will is good; removal ends the office and does not unmake
-- the appointment, and it is GIA s 10's, not this Act's. Recorded because the
-- opposite mistake — carrying a removed guardian forward as a serving one — is
-- a real one to make on the other side of this join, and a reader has to be
-- able to see that this decision was made rather than overlooked.
--
-- THE SECTION SPEAKS OF ONE APPOINTMENT AT A TIME, AND SO DOES THIS ENCODING
-- NOW (REPAIR, CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X1). The operative words are "any
-- appointment contained in that will of any person as guardian of the infant
-- children of the testator shall be of full force and effect" — ANY
-- appointment, which is distributive over the appointments and not a single
-- verdict about the will. A will may
-- appoint two guardians and s 27(3) may reach one of them and not the other,
-- which a case-level boolean cannot say. The list-valued decision below is
-- therefore the primary one and the boolean is defined off it; the boolean is
-- kept because every assertion and every aggregate in this file asks the
-- question of a case.
--
-- THE WORDS "of the infant children of the testator" ARE NOW A CONJUNCT (REPAIR,
-- CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X1). The rule used to ask only whether the will
-- appointed ANY guardian — `NOT (null ...)` over the list — so a will that
-- appointed a guardian of somebody else's child got the same answer as a will
-- that appointed a guardian of the testator's own. That is the defect the
-- cross-module audit found in the Probate and Administration Act's s 21(2)
-- (a decision whose name promises a test of the grantee and which reads no
-- grantee field at all), in this Act and on this join. The fact IS readable
-- here and was simply not read: family-domain makes the field a LIST OF
-- `Guardian` and gives `Guardian` a `the reference for the infant`, saying in
-- terms that WA s 27(3) and GIA s 7(1) "both identify the CHILD as well as the
-- appointee". So the appointment is matched against `children` — the
-- testator's OWN children and not his issue, because s 27(3) says "children"
-- where s 26 says "a child or other issue".
--
-- INFANCY IS NOT READ HERE, AND THAT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT
-- (CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X2). "the infant children of the testator" has two
-- limbs and only the first is encoded. Three reasons, in descending order of
-- force:
--   (1) THE SHARED ONTOLOGY FORBIDS IT. family-domain's note on
--       `the date on which the person attains the age of majority` says that
--       "The WA is different and must NOT read this field". That instruction
--       is addressed to this module as a whole, and this module honours it.
--       (Its stated REASON is about the testator's own age under s 4 and
--       s 27(1), not about the children under s 27(3), so the instruction is
--       wider than its reason. That tension is reported as a domain gap
--       rather than resolved by disobeying the ontology three other modules
--       are relying on.)
--   (2) THE WILLS ACT SUPPLIES NO AGE FOR IT. s 4's "under 21 years of age" is
--       the Act's own number and is stated of "any person" who MAKES a will;
--       nothing in the Act fixes when a child ceases to be an infant. The
--       corpus's honest position is that the age is an already-construed
--       input, which is the field (1) forbids this module to read.
--   (3) THE LIMB IS TEMPORAL AND THE OTHER SIDE OF THE JOIN OWNS IT. Whose
--       children they are is fixed at the will and never changes; whether they
--       are infants changes with the calendar, and the office of guardian is
--       spent when it does. That is GIA s 7's question at the moment the
--       guardian acts, not this Act's question about whether the appointment
--       is good.
-- CONSEQUENCE, STATED SO THAT NOBODY HAS TO INFER IT: this decision
-- OVER-INCLUDES. An appointment of a guardian of an ADULT child of the
-- testator is reported as of full force and effect. A consumer that needs the
-- infancy limb must apply it from the age of majority itself.
--
-- THE HAND-OFF IS TYPED, AND AS AT THIS CROSS-MODULE ROUND IT IS NOT TAKEN UP
-- ON THE OTHER SIDE (X3). family-domain makes
-- `the guardians of infant children appointed by the will` a LIST OF
-- `Guardian` — not of `Person` — precisely so that the will-side and the
-- GIA-side are one object, and `How the guardian was appointed` has the
-- members `appointed by the father by will` and `appointed by the mother by
-- will`. So what guardianship-of-infants-act.l4 must read from here is: which
-- of the appointments the will contains are in the list below. Everything
-- afterwards — s 7(3)'s joint acting with the surviving parent, s 7(4)'s
-- objection and the court's order, s 7(5)-(6)'s jointness, s 10's removal — is
-- the GIA's and is not touched here.
--
-- WHAT THE OTHER SIDE ACTUALLY DOES, MEASURED AND DATED, BECAUSE A READER MUST
-- NOT TAKE THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE FOR A DESCRIPTION OF WHAT HAPPENS. The
-- cross-module audit found this contract stated here and unknown there:
-- guardianship-of-infants-act.l4 then named the Wills Act once in 4,659 lines,
-- in its ambiguity register and only to disclaim s 4's "21", and had no joins
-- section. THAT HAS CHANGED SINCE, and the claim is restated against the file
-- as it stands rather than as the audit found it — re-measured on
-- guardianship-of-infants-act.l4 at 5,187 lines: it now has a
-- `Where this Act joins the other three` section whose first join is this one,
-- names the Wills Act 20 times, and names this decision by its full name.
-- WHAT IT STILL DOES NOT DO, AND SAYS SO ITSELF: it does not READ the answer.
-- No decision in it takes a `Will` (measured: zero). It takes the appointment
-- as already construed, instructs that `appointed by the father by will` and
-- `appointed by the mother by will` be set only where this decision has
-- answered TRUE, and otherwise treats a will-appointed guardian exactly as a
-- deed-appointed one. Its stated reason is that "no field the two modules
-- share carries it".
-- THAT REASON IS NOW WEAKER THAN IT WAS, and this module has made it weaker on
-- purpose. The four Act modules could not be imported together at all until
-- this round (see X0), so "no shared field" was the only channel there could
-- be. They can be now, and the hand-off is a VALUE — the list-valued decision
-- below — that a module seeing both Acts can call directly, per appointment.
-- A shared FIELD would still be better, because it would let a case file
-- record the answer without either module importing the other; that is a
-- family-domain question and is reported as a domain gap.
-- THE RESIDUAL RISK, STATED PLAINLY: on a case file where the instruction is
-- not followed, an appointment by a will that s 27(3) does NOT reach — an
-- informal soldier's will by a testator who died on or before 29 August 1938,
-- say — is good on the GIA side and bad here, and nothing computes the
-- contradiction. The two modules agree about who owns what; they do not yet
-- agree by construction.
--
-- "having made a will which is, or which, if it had been a disposition of
-- property, would have been rendered valid by this section" reaches BOTH
-- limbs: a will actually valid under s 27(1) as a disposition of personal
-- estate, and a will that would have been rendered valid under s 27(2) had it
-- been a disposition of property. Hence the disjunction.
GIVEN `the case`        IS A `A case under this Act`
      `the appointment` IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- NOT A RULE OF ITS OWN: the "of the infant children of the testator" limb of
-- s 27(3), lifted out so that the rule below reads as one sentence. `children`
-- and not the descent traversal s 26 uses, for the reason given above.
`the appointment is of a guardian of a child of the testator` MEANS
    any (GIVEN `the relation` YIELD
             `the relation`'s `the child`'s `the reference for this person`
                 EQUALS `the appointment`'s `the reference for the infant`)
        ((`the testator of the will under consideration` `the case`)'s `children`)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 27(3)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A LIST OF Guardian
-- THE PRIMARY FORM OF s 27(3), and the value the Guardianship of Infants Act
-- is invited to read. Empty where the gate fails, so the boolean below is
-- exactly its emptiness.
`s 27(3) — the appointments contained in the will that are of full force and effect` `the case` MEANS
    IF      `falls strictly after` (`the date of the death of` (`the testator of the will under consideration` `the case`))
                                   (YMD 1938 8 29)
        AND (   `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the case`
             OR `s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid` `the case`)
    THEN filter (GIVEN `the appointment` YIELD
                     `the appointment is of a guardian of a child of the testator` `the case` `the appointment`)
                ((`the will under consideration` `the case`)'s `the guardians of infant children appointed by the will`)
    ELSE EMPTY

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 27(3)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- The case-level summary: is there any appointment in this will that s 27(3)
-- makes good? Every assertion in this file and the aggregate verdict ask the
-- question this way, which is why the boolean survives the repair. Of the
-- three conjuncts it used to spell out, two — the commencement and the
-- privilege — are now the gate of the decision above, and the third, which
-- asked only whether the will appointed anybody, has become the filter that
-- asks it of each appointment.
`s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect` `the case` MEANS
        "Where any person dies after 29 August 1938 having made a will which is, or which, if it had been a disposition of property, would have been rendered valid by this section, any appointment contained in that will of any person as guardian of the infant children of the testator shall be of full force and effect."
    ... NOT (null (`s 27(3) — the appointments contained in the will that are of full force and effect` `the case`))

-- THE TWO SIDES OF THE JOIN, SO THAT A CASE FILE CAN BE CHECKED AGAINST ITSELF
-- (CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X4). NEITHER OF THE TWO DECISIONS BELOW IS A RULE OF THE
-- WILLS ACT, and neither must be read as one. They exist because the
-- cross-module audit found that the s 27(3) hand-off is TYPED on both sides —
-- the will carries `the guardians of infant children appointed by the will` and
-- the estate carries `the infants concerned in the estate`, each an infant with
-- `the guardians of the infant` — and that no assertion in any of the four
-- modules ever put a value on both sides at once, so nothing could tell a join
-- that works from a join that is merely declared. Reducing each side to the
-- references of the people appointed is what makes them comparable: `Guardian`
-- is a large record and two copies of one appointment may differ in the GIA
-- fields this module neither sets nor reads.

GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
-- The WILL side: who this Act has made a guardian of.
`the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good` `the case` MEANS
    map (GIVEN `the appointment` YIELD `the appointment`'s `the guardian`'s `the reference for this person`)
        (`s 27(3) — the appointments contained in the will that are of full force and effect` `the case`)

GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
-- The ESTATE side: who is recorded as guardian of an infant concerned in the
-- estate. This module never reads it for any other purpose; the Guardianship of
-- Infants Act and the Probate and Administration Act do.
`the references for the guardians recorded against the infants concerned in the estate` `the case` MEANS
    map (GIVEN `the appointment` YIELD `the appointment`'s `the guardian`'s `the reference for this person`)
        (concat (map (GIVEN `the infant` YIELD `the infant`'s `the guardians of the infant`)
                     (`the case`'s `the deceased`'s `the infants concerned in the estate`)))


§§ `Section 28 — Rectification of will: the time within which an application must be made`

-- s 28(1) is a discretion and is NOT encoded; see the closing section. What is
-- encoded is s 28(2)'s time bar and the two subsections that tell you when the
-- clock starts, s 28(4) and s 28(5). family-domain declares
-- `Limitation on the grant` for exactly this purpose and maps each of its
-- members to the s 28(4) paragraph it answers.

-- A HAZARD IN THE ONTOLOGY, NOT IN s 28(4). family-domain declares
-- `Limitation on the grant` as an ENUM, so a grant carries exactly one of
-- s 28(4)'s three descriptions and exactly one of the three helpers below can
-- be TRUE of it. The Act's three paragraphs are NOT mutually exclusive: a grant
-- limited to settled land is also, in the nature of the thing, a grant limited
-- to a part only of the testator's estate, and it does not permit the
-- distribution of the estate either. Under the Act it is disregarded
-- unconditionally by (a) whichever other paragraph it also answers; under this
-- ontology, a case file that classifies such a grant under (c) instead exposes
-- it to (c)'s proviso, so a grant of the remainder would RESTORE a settled-land
-- grant that the Act disregards outright. The classification therefore carries
-- legal weight and must put a grant under the FIRST paragraph that describes
-- it, (a) before (b) before (c). Not fixable here: `Limitation on the grant` is
-- family-domain's. (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V14 — the hazard was previously
-- undisclosed.)
--
-- AND NOTHING ELSE IS DISREGARDED. s 28(4) opens "The following grants are to
-- be disregarded", and the following grants are three. `Grant` also carries
-- `revoked` and `expired by effluxion of time or by the happening of the event
-- or contingency on which it was limited`, and neither is read here: a grant
-- since revoked was still a grant authorising the administration of the estate
-- when it was made, and s 28(4) does not list it. Filtering it out would be
-- adding a fourth paragraph to the subsection.
@ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(4)(a)
GIVEN `the grant` IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the grant is limited to settled land or to trust property` `the grant` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the grant`'s `the limitation`
    WHEN `limited to settled land or to trust property` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(4)(b)
GIVEN `the grant` IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the grant does not permit the distribution of the testator’s estate` `the grant` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the grant`'s `the limitation`
    WHEN `a grant that does not permit the distribution of the testator’s estate` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(4)(c)
GIVEN `the grant` IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the grant is limited to a part only of the testator’s estate` `the grant` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the grant`'s `the limitation`
    WHEN `limited to a part only of the testator’s estate` `the part` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(4)
--
-- s 28(4)(c)'s proviso reads one fact per CASE and not one per grant; the
-- reason, and what it costs, is on the DECLARE of `A case under this Act`.
GIVEN `the case`  IS A `A case under this Act`
      `the grant` IS A Grant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 28(4) — the grant is to be disregarded` MEANS
        "The following grants are to be disregarded when considering, for the purposes of this section, when a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made:"
    ..  "(a)" ... `the grant is limited to settled land or to trust property` `the grant`
    ..  "(b)" ... `the grant does not permit the distribution of the testator’s estate` `the grant`
    OR  "(c)" ...     `the grant is limited to a part only of the testator’s estate` `the grant`
                  AND NOT `the case`'s `a grant limited to the remainder of the testator’s estate has previously been made or is made at the same time`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(5); Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 47(1)
--
-- THE DEEMING IS NARROW AND THE ENCODING KEEPS IT NARROW: it applies only to a
-- probate, or letters of administration with the will annexed, sealed under
-- PAA s 47(1). A plain grant of letters of administration is outside it even
-- if sealed, so the kind is tested and not just the sealing flag.
GIVEN `the grant` IS A Grant
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
-- "For the purposes of this section, where a grant consists of any probate, or
-- letters of administration with the will annexed, sealed under section 47(1)
-- of the Probate and Administration Act 1934, the grant is deemed to be made
-- on the date of sealing of the probate or letters of administration with the
-- will annexed." (The subsection rides as a comment and not as an inert
-- string, because inert prose only carries in BOOLEAN position and this
-- decision returns a date.)
`the date on which the grant is made` `the grant` MEANS
        IF       `the grant`'s `sealed under section 47(1) of the Probate and Administration Act 1934`
             AND `the grant is a probate or letters of administration with the will annexed`
        THEN `the grant`'s `the date of the sealing`
        ELSE `the grant`'s `the date of the grant`
    WHERE
        `the grant is a probate or letters of administration with the will annexed` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the grant`'s `the kind of grant`
            WHEN `probate`                                     THEN TRUE
            WHEN `letters of administration with the will annexed` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(2), s 28(4)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A LIST OF Grant
`the grants that are not to be disregarded` `the case` MEANS
    filter (GIVEN `the grant` YIELD NOT (`s 28(4) — the grant is to be disregarded` `the case` `the grant`))
           (`the case`'s `the deceased`'s `the grants made in respect of the estate`)

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(2)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 28(2) — the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made` `the case` MEANS
    `the earliest of` (mapMaybe `the date on which the grant is made` (`the grants that are not to be disregarded` `the case`))

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(2)
--
-- A TOTAL ENUM, and four of its six members name a case in which no deadline
-- has been missed for a different reason. `MAYBE BOOLEAN` would collapse three
-- of them.
--
-- AMBIGUITY A7 — an application made before any grant.
--   Readings: (1) the six months runs FROM a grant, so before any grant the
--   period has not begun and an application cannot be late; (2) the subsection
--   presupposes a grant, so an application made before one is premature and
--   outside s 28(2) altogether.
--   TAKEN: (1). Reason: s 28(2) states a latest date and nothing else; it fixes
--   no earliest date and imposes no precondition on applying. Reading (2)
--   would have this module report a bar the section does not create. The
--   member is named so that a reader can see the case was decided rather than
--   folded into the ordinary in-time answer.
DECLARE `Whether an application for rectification is within the time allowed by section 28(2)` IS ONE OF
    `no application for an order under subsection (1) has been made`
    `within the time allowed, no grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate having yet been made`
    `within the time allowed`
    `made with the permission of a court`
    `the date of the first such grant, or the date of the application, not being in evidence`
    `made later than 6 months after the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 28(2)
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A `Whether an application for rectification is within the time allowed by section 28(2)`
-- REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V8 — THE ORDER OF THE LAST FOUR BRANCHES.
-- The permission test used to sit BELOW the evidence test, so a case in which a
-- court HAD given permission but the date of the application was not in
-- evidence came back as indeterminate. Under s 28(2) the permission is
-- dispositive whatever the dates: the excepting words "Except with the
-- permission of a court" lift the six-month bar altogether, and a period that
-- does not apply cannot be missed. The permission branch therefore now precedes
-- the evidence branch. It still sits BELOW the in-time branch, because where
-- the application was in fact made in time that is the more informative answer
-- and the permission adds nothing.
--
-- The member it reports was also renamed. It used to be
-- `made after the end of that period, but with the permission of a court`,
-- which asserts that the application WAS late — a fact not in evidence in
-- exactly the case the branch now catches. `made with the permission of a
-- court` is what the section makes turn on and all this module knows.
`s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `the case` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the case`'s `an application for an order under section 28(1) has been made`
              THEN `no application for an order under subsection (1) has been made`
           IF null (`the grants that are not to be disregarded` `the case`)
              THEN `within the time allowed, no grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate having yet been made`
           IF      `every grant that is not to be disregarded is dated`
               AND `the application was made no later than 6 months after that date`
              THEN `within the time allowed`
           IF `the case`'s `a court has given permission for the application to be made after the end of that period`
              THEN `made with the permission of a court`
           IF `the dates are not all in evidence`
              THEN `the date of the first such grant, or the date of the application, not being in evidence`
           OTHERWISE `made later than 6 months after the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made`
    WHERE
        `the first grant` MEANS
            `s 28(2) — the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made` `the case`

        -- AN UNDATED GRANT IS AN EVIDENCE GAP AND NOT AN ABSENCE, and this guard
        -- is the same repair as the one at `The formal validity of the will`
        -- (ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V7), applied where the shape repeats. `the first
        -- grant` is the earliest of the grants that HAVE a date: `mapMaybe`
        -- drops an undated one silently. So a file carrying an undated grant
        -- and a later dated one would have the clock started from the later,
        -- which gives the applicant more time than s 28(2) allows and asserts
        -- that the undated grant was not the first. The in-time branch is
        -- therefore guarded, and a file in that state is reported as
        -- indeterminate rather than answered.
        `every grant that is not to be disregarded is dated` MEANS
            all (GIVEN `the grant` YIELD isJust (`the date on which the grant is made` `the grant`))
                (`the grants that are not to be disregarded` `the case`)

        `the dates are not all in evidence` MEANS
                NOT (isJust `the first grant`)
            OR  NOT `every grant that is not to be disregarded is dated`
            OR  NOT (isJust (`the case`'s `the date of that application`))

        `the application was made no later than 6 months after that date` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the first grant`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
            WHEN JUST `the day of the grant` THEN
                CONSIDER `the case`'s `the date of that application`
                WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
                WHEN JUST `the day of the application` THEN
                    Day `the day of the application` AT MOST Day (`six months after` `the day of the grant`)


§§ `Formal validity, all together`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 4, s 5, s 6, s 27(1)
--
-- A TOTAL ENUM WITH SEVEN MEMBERS, three of which say neither that the will is
-- valid nor that it is not. Two of those three say that a section could not be
-- APPLIED for want of a fact — the age of the testator when the will was made (s 4, and see
-- AMBIGUITY A1), and the date of his death (s 5(9)) — and the third says that
-- s 5 reached only part of the will.
--
-- THE TWO MEMBERS THAT REPORT A SECTION AS INAPPLICABLE ARE THE SAME
-- DISCIPLINE (REPAIR,
-- ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V7). s 4 already had one, and it is argued at length at
-- A1: a will whose testator's age is unknown is neither proved to be an
-- infant's nor proved to be an adult's, and reporting either would resolve the
-- gap silently. s 5 was denied the same treatment without a word. `falls
-- strictly after` returns FALSE where the date of death is NOTHING, so a
-- testator known to be dead on a date not in evidence closed s 5(9), closed
-- s 5, and drew the definite verdict `not valid, the will not being in writing
-- or not executed in the manner mentioned in section 6(2), and not being
-- treated as properly executed under section 5` — whose second half was false.
-- s 5 had not been applied and failed; it had never been reached.
--
-- THE PARTIAL MEMBER IS s 5(3)(b)-(d)'s "SO FAR AS" (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL
-- REVIEW V4). It names the outcome and not the part, because nothing in the
-- shared ontology divides a will into parts; what it does is stop a will that
-- conforms to no law except the lex situs of one piece of land from coming out
-- of this module as simply `valid`.
DECLARE `The formal validity of the will` IS ONE OF
    `valid`
    `valid, being the will of a soldier being in actual military service, or of a mariner or seaman being at sea, disposing of his personal estate (section 27(1))`
    `not valid, being a will made by a person under 21 years of age (section 4)`
    `not valid, the will not being in writing or not executed in the manner mentioned in section 6(2), and not being treated as properly executed under section 5`
    `treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of the will (section 5(3)(b), (c) or (d)), and not otherwise valid`
    `the age of the testator when the will was made not being in evidence, section 4 cannot be applied`
    `the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence, section 5 cannot be applied`

@ref Wills Act 1838 s 4, s 5(1), s 6(1), s 27(1)
--
-- THE ORDER OF THE BRANCHES IS THE ORDER THE ACT FIXES:
--   s 27(1) first, because it opens "Notwithstanding anything in this Act" and
--     dispenses with both the age bar and the mode of execution;
--   then s 4, because it disqualifies the will outright;
--   then the not-in-evidence case, which is s 4's own gap;
--   then execution, which is s 6 OR — s 5(1) again being a notwithstanding —
--     s 5, taken as a whole will;
--   and then, only where the will is not good as a whole, the two cases in
--     which s 5 reached part of it or could not be reached at all.
-- This is deliberately NOT a flat conjunction: collapsing it would lose which
-- section defeated the will, which is the one thing a reader holding the Act
-- wants back.
GIVEN `the case` IS A `A case under this Act`
GIVETH A `The formal validity of the will`
`the formal validity of the will` `the case` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the case`
              THEN `valid, being the will of a soldier being in actual military service, or of a mariner or seaman being at sea, disposing of his personal estate (section 27(1))`
           IF `s 4 — will of infant invalid` (`the will under consideration` `the case`)
              THEN `not valid, being a will made by a person under 21 years of age (section 4)`
           IF NOT (`s 4 — the testator is proved to have been 21 years of age or over` (`the will under consideration` `the case`))
              THEN `the age of the testator when the will was made not being in evidence, section 4 cannot be applied`
           IF `the will is properly executed` `the case`
              THEN `valid`
           IF      `the date of the death of the testator is not in evidence`
               AND `s 5 — some connecting factor is made out, leaving aside the commencement in subsection (9)` `the case`
              THEN `the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence, section 5 cannot be applied`
           IF `s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` `the case`
              EQUALS `treated as properly executed so far only as the will disposes of immovable property, revokes a will or a provision, or exercises a power of appointment`
              THEN `treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of the will (section 5(3)(b), (c) or (d)), and not otherwise valid`
           OTHERWISE `not valid, the will not being in writing or not executed in the manner mentioned in section 6(2), and not being treated as properly executed under section 5`
    WHERE
        `the date of the death of the testator is not in evidence` MEANS
            NOT (isJust (`the date of the death of` (`the testator of the will under consideration` `the case`)))


§§ `Worked facts — constructors`

-- L4 has no record-update operator, so a scenario is either a fully spelled
-- literal or a constructor partially applied. family-domain's records are
-- large and mostly irrelevant to this Act, so what follows is a set of
-- constructors that take the fields the Wills Act varies and default the rest.
-- Nothing below is a rule; every one of them is test scaffolding.

GIVEN
    `the person’s name`                     IS A STRING
    `the reference`                         IS A STRING
    `when the person was born`              IS A MAYBE DATE
    `whether the person is living`          IS A `Vital status`
    `the person’s children`                 IS A LIST OF Child
    `in actual military service`            IS A BOOLEAN
    `at sea`                                IS A BOOLEAN
    `so circumstanced`                      IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Person
-- THE THREE s 27 ROUTES ARE ALL PARAMETERS, and that is a repair (ROUND 2, R9).
-- The soldier alone used to be settable and the other two were hardcoded
-- FALSE, so `s 27 — this section applies to the person` had two rungs no
-- fixture could reach: the mariner or seaman being at sea (s 27(1)) and the
-- member of naval or marine forces (s 27(4)). The aggregate's verdict string
-- names the mariner in terms, so the file asserted an outcome nothing could
-- produce. `a WA person` below still defaults both to FALSE.
`a WA person, in full` MEANS Person WITH
    `name`                                                                            IS `the person’s name`
    `the reference for this person`                                                   IS `the reference`
    `date of birth`                                                                   IS `when the person was born`
    `vital status`                                                                    IS `whether the person is living`
    `children`                                                                        IS `the person’s children`
    `at the date of the death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` IS FALSE
    `money or other property given, paid or settled by the intestate during his life to or for the advancement of the person` IS 0
    `the date on which the person attains the age of majority`                        IS NOTHING
    `a soldier being in actual military service`                                      IS `in actual military service`
    `a mariner or seaman being at sea`                                                IS `at sea`
    `a member of any naval or marine forces so circumstanced that if he were a soldier he would be in actual military service` IS `so circumstanced`
    `so desires that letters of administration be granted to such person as the court thinks fit` IS FALSE
    `lacks mental capacity (within the meaning of the Mental Capacity Act 2008) to make decisions with respect to the management and administration of the person's property and affairs` IS FALSE
    `absent from Singapore`                                                           IS FALSE
    `willing to act`                                                                  IS TRUE

GIVEN
    `the person’s name`                     IS A STRING
    `the reference`                         IS A STRING
    `when the person was born`              IS A MAYBE DATE
    `whether the person is living`          IS A `Vital status`
    `the person’s children`                 IS A LIST OF Child
    `in actual military service`            IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Person
-- A person who is neither a mariner or seaman at sea nor a member of any naval
-- or marine forces. Most of the cast is.
`a WA person` MEANS
    `a WA person, in full` `the person’s name` `the reference` `when the person was born`
                        `whether the person is living` `the person’s children`
                        `in actual military service` FALSE FALSE

GIVEN `the offspring` IS A Person
GIVETH A Child
`a WA legitimate child` MEANS Child WITH
    `the child`                                           IS `the offspring`
    `a legitimate child of the person whose child he is`  IS TRUE
    `adopted by that person by virtue of an order of court under any written law for the time being in force in Singapore, Malaysia or Brunei Darussalam` IS FALSE

GIVEN
    `the person who attested`                     IS A Person
    `he subscribed in the presence of the testator` IS A BOOLEAN
    `he is otherwise incompetent`                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `he took a beneficial gift`                   IS A BOOLEAN
    `his wife or husband took a beneficial gift`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `he is a creditor whose debt is charged`      IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Attesting witness`
`an attesting witness` MEANS `Attesting witness` WITH
    `the witness`                                         IS `the person who attested`
    `subscribed the will in the presence of the testator`  IS `he subscribed in the presence of the testator`
    `incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof, otherwise than by reason of sections 10(2), 11 and 12` IS `he is otherwise incompetent`
    `given or made a beneficial devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment by the will, other than and except charges and directions for the payment of any debt` IS `he took a beneficial gift`
    `the wife or husband of the witness given or made such a beneficial devise, legacy, estate, interest, gift or appointment` IS `his wife or husband took a beneficial gift`
    `a creditor, or the wife or husband of a creditor, whose debt is charged on the estate by the will` IS `he is a creditor whose debt is charged`

GIVEN
    `the person who takes`                          IS A Person
    `what kind of gift`                             IS A `Kind of disposition`
    `the value`                                     IS A Money
    `it is beneficial`                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is not determinable at or before his death` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Disposition
`a gift` MEANS Disposition WITH
    `the donee`                                             IS `an individual` `the person who takes`
    `the kind of disposition`                               IS `what kind of gift`
    `the value of the disposition`                          IS `the value`
    `beneficial`                                            IS `it is beneficial`
    `not determinable at or before the death of the donee`  IS `it is not determinable at or before his death`

GIVEN
    `the person appointed`     IS A Person
    `the reference for the child` IS A STRING
GIVETH A Guardian
-- A guardian appointed by the father by will; every other field takes the
-- value that says nothing has happened yet. None of them is read by the
-- Wills Act — s 27(3) asks only whether the appointment stands — and all of
-- them are the Guardianship of Infants Act's business.
`a guardian appointed by the father by will` MEANS Guardian WITH
    `the guardian`                                        IS `the person appointed`
    `the reference for the infant`                         IS `the reference for the child`
    `how the guardian was appointed`                        IS `appointed by the father by will`
    `of the person or of the property`                      IS `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
    `alone or jointly`                                      IS `alone`
    `refuses to act`                                        IS FALSE
    `the mother or father objects to his so acting`          IS FALSE
    `considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of the infant` IS FALSE
    `removed from his guardianship by the court`             IS FALSE
    `has given security`                                    IS TRUE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to security`          IS FALSE
    `has sold, mortgaged, exchanged, or otherwise parted with the possession of any of the movable or immovable property of the infant` IS FALSE
    `has leased land belonging to the infant`                IS FALSE
    `the term of the lease, in years`                        IS NOTHING
    `has the permission of the court to deal with the property of the infant` IS FALSE
    `has given a good discharge for a legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by the infant` IS FALSE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to giving a good discharge` IS FALSE
    `the provision made out of the income of the property of the infant for his maintenance and education` IS (`A periodical sum` WITH `the sum` IS 0, `the period` IS `monthly`)
    `has the permission of the court to apply a sum exceeding $100 per month` IS FALSE

GIVEN
    `the other party`                  IS A Person
    `when it was solemnised`           IS A MAYBE DATE
    `it subsists`                      IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Marriage
`a marriage` MEANS Marriage WITH
    `the spouse`                                     IS `the other party`
    `the date of the solemnisation of the marriage`  IS `when it was solemnised`
    `subsisting at the death of the deceased, in the sense of not dissolved or annulled before the death` IS `it subsists`

GIVEN `the marriages` IS A LIST OF Marriage
GIVETH A Family
`a family with the marriages` MEANS Family WITH
    `the marriages of the deceased`             IS `the marriages`
    `the father of the deceased`                IS NOTHING
    `the mother of the deceased`                IS NOTHING
    `the brothers and sisters of the deceased`  IS EMPTY
    `the grandparents of the deceased`          IS EMPTY
    `the uncles and aunts of the deceased`      IS EMPTY

-- No figure in this Act is a sum of money, so every value here is nil.
`an estate of no recorded value` MEANS Estate WITH
    `the value of the movable property situated in Singapore`          IS 0
    `the value of the immovable property situated in Singapore`        IS 0
    `the value of the movable property situated outside Singapore`     IS 0
    `the value of the immovable property situated outside Singapore`   IS 0
    `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated in Singapore` IS 0
    `the value of any property which the deceased possessed or was entitled to as trustee and not beneficially, situated outside Singapore` IS 0
    `the value of any moneys payable by an appointed insurer pursuant to the Dependants’ Protection Insurance Scheme or any other equivalent scheme maintained by the Central Provident Fund Board` IS 0
    `the value of the property administered by the Public Trustee`     IS 0
    `the amount of the funds in court directed to be paid, transferred or delivered to him` IS 0
    `the value of land settled previously to the death of the deceased` IS 0
    `the trustees of the settlement`                                   IS EMPTY
    `the value of the interest in property which at the time of his death is charged with the payment of money` IS 0
    `the amount of the charge on that interest`                        IS 0
    `the deceased has by will, deed or other document signified a contrary or other intention` IS FALSE
    `the debts and liabilities of the deceased`                        IS 0
    `the funeral, testamentary and administration expenses`            IS 0
    `the creditors of the deceased`                                    IS EMPTY
    `fully administered`                                               IS FALSE
    `the value of the property beneficially disposed of by the will`   IS 0

GIVEN
    `the maker of the will`                                    IS A Person
    `the date it was executed`                                 IS A MAYBE DATE
    `it is in writing`                                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `the maker signed at the foot or end`                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `another signed at the foot or end for him`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `the signature was acknowledged before 2 or more witnesses` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the witnesses`                                            IS A LIST OF `Attesting witness`
    `the gifts`                                                IS A LIST OF Disposition
    `the guardians appointed by it`                            IS A LIST OF Guardian
    `it is expressed to be made in contemplation of a marriage` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the reference for the intended spouse`                     IS A MAYBE STRING
    `it is made in exercise of a power of appointment`          IS A BOOLEAN
    `the appointed estate would not pass in default`            IS A BOOLEAN
    `the testator’s intention is apparent on the face of it`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the executors it appoints`                                 IS A LIST OF `Executor appointed by a will`
GIVETH A Will
-- THE AMANUENSIS LIMB IS A PARAMETER TOO (ROUND 2). s 6(2)'s first limb is
-- satisfied by a will "signed at the foot or end thereof by the testator, or by
-- some other person in his presence and by his direction" — and that second
-- alternative was hardcoded FALSE here, so it could be deleted from s 6(2), and
-- from s 10(3)'s counterfactual, with every assertion in the file still green.
--
-- THE LAST TWO PARAMETERS ARE A REPAIR (ROUND 2, R6 AND R7). They used to be
-- hardcoded — the apparent intention TRUE, the executors EMPTY — and this was
-- the only `Will` constructor in the file, so two encoded provisions had an
-- antecedent no case file could reach:
--   s 6(3)'s evidential conjunct, "apparent on the face of the will that the
--     testator intended to give effect by such his signature to the writing
--     signed as his will", could never be FALSE, so half of the two-conjunct
--     repair the first review round made was never exercised;
--   s 12's antecedent, that the attesting witness is an executor appointed by
--     the will, could never be TRUE, so the denial could not be seen to deny.
`a will, in full` MEANS Will WITH
    `the testator`                                     IS `the maker of the will`
    `the date of execution`                            IS `the date it was executed`
    `in writing`                                       IS `it is in writing`
    `signed at the foot or end thereof by the testator` IS `the maker signed at the foot or end`
    `signed at the foot or end thereof by some other person in his presence and by his direction` IS `another signed at the foot or end for him`
    `the signature made or acknowledged by the testator as the signature to his will or codicil in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time` IS `the signature was acknowledged before 2 or more witnesses`
    `apparent on the face of the will that the testator intended to give effect by such his signature to the writing signed as his will` IS `the testator’s intention is apparent on the face of it`
    `the attesting witnesses`                          IS `the witnesses`
    `the executors appointed by the will`              IS `the executors it appoints`
    `the guardians of infant children appointed by the will` IS `the guardians appointed by it`
    `the dispositions made by the will`                IS `the gifts`
    `expressed to be made in contemplation of a marriage` IS `it is expressed to be made in contemplation of a marriage`
    `the reference for the person with whom the marriage contemplated was to be solemnised` IS `the reference for the intended spouse`
    `made in exercise of a power of appointment`       IS `it is made in exercise of a power of appointment`
    `the estate thereby appointed would not in default of such appointment pass to his or her heir, executor or administrator or the person entitled under the Intestate Succession Act 1967` IS `the appointed estate would not pass in default`
    `the life interests arising under the will`        IS EMPTY
    `it appears by the will that the personal representative is entitled to take that part beneficially` IS FALSE
    `the codicils`                                     IS EMPTY

GIVEN
    `the maker of the will`                                    IS A Person
    `the date it was executed`                                 IS A MAYBE DATE
    `it is in writing`                                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `the maker signed at the foot or end`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `the signature was acknowledged before 2 or more witnesses` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the witnesses`                                            IS A LIST OF `Attesting witness`
    `the gifts`                                                IS A LIST OF Disposition
    `the guardians appointed by it`                            IS A LIST OF Guardian
    `it is expressed to be made in contemplation of a marriage` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the reference for the intended spouse`                     IS A MAYBE STRING
    `it is made in exercise of a power of appointment`          IS A BOOLEAN
    `the appointed estate would not pass in default`            IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Will
-- The ordinary case: the testator's intention to give effect by his signature
-- IS apparent on the face of the will, and the will appoints no executor.
`a will` MEANS
    `a will, in full` `the maker of the will` `the date it was executed` `it is in writing`
                      `the maker signed at the foot or end` FALSE
                      `the signature was acknowledged before 2 or more witnesses`
                      `the witnesses` `the gifts` `the guardians appointed by it`
                      `it is expressed to be made in contemplation of a marriage`
                      `the reference for the intended spouse`
                      `it is made in exercise of a power of appointment`
                      `the appointed estate would not pass in default`
                      TRUE EMPTY

GIVEN `the person appointed` IS A Person
GIVETH AN `Executor appointed by a will`
-- An executor expressly appointed, competent, not renouncing. None of these
-- fields is read by the Wills Act — s 12 asks only whether this person is among
-- the executors the will appoints — and all of them are the Probate and
-- Administration Act's business.
`an executor appointed by the will` MEANS `Executor appointed by a will` WITH
    `the executor`                                     IS `the person appointed`
    `the appointment, express or implied`              IS `express`
    `legally incapable of acting as such, otherwise than under section 21(1) or section 22(1)` IS FALSE
    `has renounced the right to act as such`           IS FALSE
    `appeared and extracted probate`                   IS TRUE
    `has administered all the estate of the deceased`  IS FALSE
GIVEN
    `(2)(a)`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `(2)(b)(i)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(2)(b)(ii)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(2)(c)`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `(2)(d)`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `(3)(a)`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `(3)(b)`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `(3)(c)`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `(3)(d)`    IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Conformity of the execution of a will to a law outside this Act`
-- ONE PARAMETER PER CONNECTING FACTOR, in the subsection's own order, so that
-- each of s 5's nine limbs can be made out on its own. (ROUND 2, R9: the file
-- carried three hand-spelled literals between them setting only (2)(a) and
-- (3)(b), so six of the nine limbs were FALSE on every fixture and neither the
-- vessel paragraph nor five of the connecting factors was exercised at all.)
`the execution conforming thus` MEANS `Conformity of the execution of a will to a law outside this Act` WITH
    `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where it was executed` IS `(2)(a)`
    `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator was domiciled at the time when the will was executed` IS `(2)(b)(i)`
    `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator was domiciled at the time of his death` IS `(2)(b)(ii)`
    `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the testator habitually resided at either of the times referred to in paragraph (b)` IS `(2)(c)`
    `the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the state of which the testator was a national at either of the times referred to in paragraph (b)` IS `(2)(d)`
    `the will was executed on board a vessel or an aircraft of any description, and the execution of the will conformed to the internal law in force in the territory with which, having regard to its registration (if any) and other relevant circumstances, the vessel or aircraft may be taken to have been most closely connected` IS `(3)(a)`
    `so far as the will disposes of immovable property, its execution conformed to the internal law in force in the territory where the property was situated` IS `(3)(b)`
    `so far as the will revokes a will which under this Act would be treated as properly executed, or revokes a provision which under this Act would be treated as comprised in a properly executed will, the execution of the later will conformed to any law by reference to which the revoked will or provision would be treated as properly executed` IS `(3)(c)`
    `so far as the will exercises a power of appointment, the execution of the will conformed to the law governing the essential validity of the power` IS `(3)(d)`

--                                                                        (2)(a) (b)(i) (b)(ii) (c)  (d)   (3)(a) (b)  (c)  (d)
`no conformity to any law outside this Act`                    MEANS `the execution conforming thus` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- The lex loci actus limb alone, s 5(2)(a): the will conformed to the internal
-- law of the territory where it was executed and to nothing else.
`conformity to the internal law of the territory where it was executed` MEANS `the execution conforming thus` TRUE  FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- The four remaining connecting factors of s 5(2), each alone. Each is a
-- whole-will limb, so each on its own makes the will properly executed.
`conformity to the internal law of the testator’s domicile when the will was executed` MEANS `the execution conforming thus` FALSE TRUE  FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
`conformity to the internal law of the testator’s domicile at his death`               MEANS `the execution conforming thus` FALSE FALSE TRUE  FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
`conformity to the internal law of the territory of the testator’s habitual residence` MEANS `the execution conforming thus` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE  FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
`conformity to the internal law of the state of the testator’s nationality`            MEANS `the execution conforming thus` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE  FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- s 5(3)(a), the will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft. There is no
-- "so far as" in (a), so this one is a whole-will limb too.
`conformity to the internal law of the territory the vessel is most closely connected with` MEANS `the execution conforming thus` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE  FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- The lex situs limb alone, s 5(3)(b): the will conforms to the internal law
-- of the territory where its immovable property is situated and to nothing
-- else. s 5(3)(b) treats the will as properly executed SO FAR ONLY AS it
-- disposes of that property, which is why the case built on this fixture does
-- not come out `valid`.
`conformity to the internal law of the territory where the immovable property is situated` MEANS `the execution conforming thus` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE  FALSE FALSE
-- s 5(3)(c) and s 5(3)(d), each alone: both are "so far as" paragraphs.
`conformity to the law by reference to which the revoked will would be properly executed`  MEANS `the execution conforming thus` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE  FALSE
`conformity to the law governing the essential validity of the power`                      MEANS `the execution conforming thus` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE

GIVEN
    `by a later will or codicil`         IS A BOOLEAN
    `by a writing declaring an intention` IS A BOOLEAN
    `destroyed by the testator`          IS A BOOLEAN
    `destroyed by another at his direction` IS A BOOLEAN
    `with the animus`                    IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `The ways in which a will may have been revoked`
`revoked` MEANS `The ways in which a will may have been revoked` WITH
    `revoked by another will or codicil executed in the manner by this Act required` IS `by a later will or codicil`
    `revoked by some writing declaring an intention to revoke it, and executed in the manner in which a will is by this Act required to be executed` IS `by a writing declaring an intention`
    `burnt, torn, or otherwise destroyed by the testator`  IS `destroyed by the testator`
    `burnt, torn, or otherwise destroyed by some person in his presence and by his direction` IS `destroyed by another at his direction`
    `destroyed with the intention of revoking it`          IS `with the animus`

`not revoked in any of those ways` MEANS `revoked` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

GIVEN
    `by re-execution`             IS A BOOLEAN
    `by a codicil showing an intention to revive` IS A BOOLEAN
    `partly and then wholly revoked` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a contrary intention is shown` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `The revival of a revoked will`
`revived` MEANS `The revival of a revoked will` WITH
    `revived by the re-execution thereof`                  IS `by re-execution`
    `revived by a codicil executed in the manner required by this Act and showing an intention to revive the will or codicil` IS `by a codicil showing an intention to revive`
    `partly revoked, and afterwards wholly revoked`         IS `partly and then wholly revoked`
    `an intention to the contrary is shown`                 IS `a contrary intention is shown`

`no revival` MEANS `revived` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

GIVEN
    `there is such a signature at all`                                                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `so placed at or after, or following, or under, or beside, or opposite to the end` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(a)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(b)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(c)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(d)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(e)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `The position of the signature`
`the signature placed so that` MEANS `The position of the signature` WITH
    `there is a signature of the testator, or of a person signing for him in his presence and by his direction` IS `there is such a signature at all`
    `the signature of the testator, or of the person signing for him as mentioned in subsection (2), is so placed at or after, or following, or under, or beside, or opposite to the end of the will` IS `so placed at or after, or following, or under, or beside, or opposite to the end`
    `the signature does not follow and is not immediately after the foot or end of the will` IS `(a)`
    `a blank space intervenes between the concluding word of the will and the signature`     IS `(b)`
    `the signature is placed among the words of the testimonium clause or of the clause of attestation, or follows or is after or under the clause of attestation, either with or without a blank space intervening, or follows or is after, or under, or beside the names or one of the names of the subscribing witnesses` IS `(c)`
    `the signature is on a side or page or other portion of the paper or papers containing the will whereon no clause or paragraph or disposing part of the will is written above the signature` IS `(d)`
    `there appears to be sufficient space on or at the bottom of the preceding side or page or other portion of the same paper on which the will is written to contain the signature` IS `(e)`

-- Signed at the foot, and none of the five circumstances present.
`the signature in an unremarkable position`                    MEANS `the signature placed so that` TRUE  TRUE  FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- Still at the end of the will, so s 6(3)'s antecedent holds; two of the five
-- circumstances that s 6(3)(a)-(e) denies effect to are present.
`the signature among the words of the attestation clause, after a blank space` MEANS `the signature placed so that` TRUE  TRUE  FALSE TRUE  TRUE  FALSE FALSE
-- Beside the end of the will rather than at the foot of it: within s 6(3)'s
-- list, and (a) is made out because the signature does not follow the end.
`the signature beside the end of the will`                     MEANS `the signature placed so that` TRUE  TRUE  TRUE  FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- At the HEAD of the will, above every dispositive word. That is NOT "at or
-- after, or following, or under, or beside, or opposite to the end of the
-- will", so s 6(3) cannot deem it valid however plain the testator's intention
-- was; (d) is made out, but (d) only says that circumstance does not by itself
-- affect a will the subsection has already reached.
`the signature at the head of the will, above all the dispositive words` MEANS `the signature placed so that` TRUE  FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE  FALSE
-- BESIDE THE END OF A WILL NOBODY SIGNED. The position field says the signature
-- sits beside the end and the enumerated circumstance (a) is made out, but the
-- first field says there is no signature of the testator, and none by anyone
-- signing for him in his presence and by his direction. s 6(3) deems valid only
-- "the position of the signature of the testator, or of the person signing for
-- him as mentioned in subsection (2)", so it has nothing to reach. (ROUND 2,
-- R11: this state used to be unreachable in the sense that no field recorded
-- it, and the position field carried the presupposition in its NAME alone.)
`the position stated as beside the end, though nobody signed the will` MEANS `the signature placed so that` FALSE TRUE  TRUE  FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

GIVEN
    `the instrument`                                        IS A Will
    `where the signature is`                                IS A `The position of the signature`
    `the conformity facts`                                  IS A `Conformity of the execution of a will to a law outside this Act`
    `the revocation facts`                                  IS A `The ways in which a will may have been revoked`
    `the revival facts`                                     IS A `The revival of a revoked will`
    `duly executed counting only the disinterested witnesses` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a subsequent conveyance or other act`                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `that conveyance being the act by which the will is revoked` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a contrary intention as to the time from which it speaks` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a contrary intention as to lapse`                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `it would have been valid as an informal disposition of personal estate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks`
`the facts about a will, in full` MEANS `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks` WITH
    `the will`                                                 IS `the instrument`
    `the position of the signature`                            IS `where the signature is`
    `the conformity of its execution to a law outside this Act` IS `the conformity facts`
    `the ways in which it has been revoked`                     IS `the revocation facts`
    `its revival`                                               IS `the revival facts`
    `the signature was made or acknowledged by the testator as the signature to his will or codicil in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time, counting only witnesses to whom and to whose wife or husband no such disposition is given or made` IS `duly executed counting only the disinterested witnesses`
    `a conveyance or other act has been made or done subsequently to its execution, of or relating to any real or personal estate therein comprised` IS `a subsequent conveyance or other act`
    `that conveyance or other act is an act by which the will is revoked as aforesaid` IS `that conveyance being the act by which the will is revoked`
    `a contrary intention appears by the will as to the time from which it speaks` IS `a contrary intention as to the time from which it speaks`
    `a contrary intention appears by the will as to the lapse of a devise or bequest to a child or other issue` IS `a contrary intention as to lapse`
    `made in such manner and form that if the disposition had been a disposition of personal estate made by that person it would have been valid` IS `it would have been valid as an informal disposition of personal estate`

GIVEN
    `the instrument`                                        IS A Will
    `the conformity facts`                                  IS A `Conformity of the execution of a will to a law outside this Act`
    `the revocation facts`                                  IS A `The ways in which a will may have been revoked`
    `the revival facts`                                     IS A `The revival of a revoked will`
    `duly executed counting only the disinterested witnesses` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a subsequent conveyance or other act`                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `a contrary intention as to the time from which it speaks` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a contrary intention as to lapse`                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `it would have been valid as an informal disposition of personal estate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks`
-- The nine facts most of the scenarios below vary. The two that only the
-- s 6(3) and s 18 scenarios vary are supplied here: no subsequent conveyance is
-- the act by which the will is revoked, and the position of the signature is
-- DERIVED rather than assumed — the signature is taken to be "so placed at or
-- after, or following, or under, or beside, or opposite to the end of the will"
-- exactly where the will was in fact signed at the foot or end, which is the
-- one place that can be read off the facts already recorded. A will not shown
-- to have been signed at the foot or end is not shown to have a signature
-- placed anywhere, so the default never lets s 6(3) rescue a will: it is TRUE
-- only where s 6(2)'s first limb was satisfied without it. Where the position
-- is what is in issue, use `the facts about a will, in full` and say where the
-- signature sits.
`the facts about a will` MEANS
    `the facts about a will, in full` `the instrument`
                                      (`the signature placed so that`
                                           `there is a signature`
                                           `there is a signature`
                                           FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
                                      `the conformity facts` `the revocation facts` `the revival facts`
                                      `duly executed counting only the disinterested witnesses`
                                      `a subsequent conveyance or other act` FALSE
                                      `a contrary intention as to the time from which it speaks`
                                      `a contrary intention as to lapse`
                                      `it would have been valid as an informal disposition of personal estate`
    WHERE
        -- Both the existence of the signature and its position are derived from
        -- the one thing already recorded: that the will was in fact signed at
        -- the foot or end, by the testator or by another in his presence and by
        -- his direction. A will not shown to have been signed at all is not
        -- shown to have a signature anywhere.
        `there is a signature` MEANS
                `the instrument`'s `signed at the foot or end thereof by the testator`
            OR  `the instrument`'s `signed at the foot or end thereof by some other person in his presence and by his direction`

GIVEN `the instrument` IS A Will
GIVETH A `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks`
-- A will about which nothing else has happened: no foreign law, no revocation,
-- no revival, no subsequent conveyance, no contrary intention and no privilege.
-- It is what the will-level rules — s 6(1), s 6(2), s 6(3), s 7(2), s 8 — are
-- exercised on, those rules now taking the wrapper so that s 6(2) can reach
-- s 6(3)'s facts about the position of the signature.
`the plain facts about` `the instrument` MEANS
    `the facts about a will` `the instrument`
                             `no conformity to any law outside this Act`
                             `not revoked in any of those ways` `no revival`
                             FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

GIVEN
    `what kind`                            IS A `Kind of grant`
    `the date`                             IS A MAYBE DATE
    `the limitation of the grant`          IS A `Limitation on the grant`
    `it was sealed under section 47(1)`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the date it was sealed`               IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A Grant
`a WA grant` MEANS Grant WITH
    `the kind of grant`                    IS `what kind`
    `the grantees`                         IS EMPTY
    `the date of the grant`                IS `the date`
    `the limitation`                       IS `the limitation of the grant`
    `the court that made the grant`        IS `a court within the meaning of these Acts` `the General Division of the High Court`
    `the exception subject to which the grant is made` IS NOTHING
    `expired by effluxion of time or by the happening of the event or contingency on which it was limited` IS FALSE
    `revoked`                              IS FALSE
    `has given such security as is lawfully required to be furnished` IS TRUE
    `has been extracted`                   IS TRUE
    `sealed under section 47(1) of the Probate and Administration Act 1934` IS `it was sealed under section 47(1)`
    `the date of the sealing`              IS `the date it was sealed`

GIVEN
    `the infant child`         IS A Person
    `the father of the child`  IS A MAYBE Person
    `the guardians`            IS A LIST OF Guardian
GIVETH AN Infant
-- AN INFANT ON THE ESTATE SIDE OF THE s 27(3) JOIN, and it is here because the
-- cross-module audit found that no fixture in any of the four modules ever put
-- one there (CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X4). NOTHING in the Wills Act reads an
-- `Infant`: the whole record is the Guardianship of Infants Act's and the
-- Probate and Administration Act's. It is built here so that at least one case
-- file in this module is WELL FORMED across the join — so that the guardian the
-- will appoints and the guardian recorded against the infant in the estate are
-- one object rather than two records that happen to agree — and so that a later
-- `family-cases.l4` has something to start from. The GIA fields that record
-- what has happened to the infant's property are nil: no Wills Act provision
-- asks about them, and inventing figures would be inventing facts.
`a WA infant concerned in the estate` MEANS Infant WITH
    `the infant`                                          IS `the infant child`
    `the father of the infant`                            IS `the father of the child`
    `the mother of the infant`                            IS NOTHING
    `the guardians of the infant`                         IS `the guardians`
    `some other person has parental rights with respect to him` IS FALSE
    `the income of the property of the infant`             IS 0
    `the capital of the property of the infant`            IS 0
    `of an age to express an independent opinion`          IS FALSE

GIVEN
    `the person who died`        IS A Person
    `the family of the deceased` IS A Family
    `the will of the deceased`   IS A MAYBE Will
    `the grants`                 IS A LIST OF Grant
    `the infants`                IS A LIST OF Infant
GIVETH A `Deceased person`
-- THE INFANTS ARE A PARAMETER NOW (REPAIR, CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X4). This
-- constructor used to hardcode `the infants concerned in the estate` IS EMPTY,
-- with no way to set it — which the cross-module audit measured as the reason
-- no assertion anywhere in the corpus exercises a join: the s 27(3) fixtures
-- appointed a guardian of an infant child named in the WILL while the ESTATE
-- of the same deceased contained no infant at all, so the two halves of one
-- family could not be checked against each other. The four-argument form below
-- still defaults it to EMPTY, because for every case in this file that is not
-- about the s 27(3) join the estate side is genuinely not in evidence and an
-- invented infant would be an invented fact.
`a WA deceased person, with the infants concerned in the estate` MEANS `Deceased person` WITH
    `the deceased`                                  IS `the person who died`
    `the family`                                    IS `the family of the deceased`
    `the estate`                                    IS `an estate of no recorded value`
    `the will`                                      IS `the will of the deceased`
    `domiciled in Singapore at the time of his death` IS TRUE
    `carried on business or resided in Singapore within 12 months of his death` IS TRUE
    `a Muslim`                                      IS FALSE
    `the grants made in respect of the estate`      IS `the grants`
    `the probate applications made in respect of the estate` IS EMPTY
    `a next of kin, creditor or other person has appeared and made out a claim to letters of administration` IS FALSE
    `the infants concerned in the estate`           IS `the infants`

GIVEN
    `the person who died`        IS A Person
    `the family of the deceased` IS A Family
    `the will of the deceased`   IS A MAYBE Will
    `the grants`                 IS A LIST OF Grant
GIVETH A `Deceased person`
-- No infant recorded as concerned in the estate. Every case in this file uses
-- this form except the one built to exercise the s 27(3) join.
`a WA deceased person` MEANS
    `a WA deceased person, with the infants concerned in the estate`
        `the person who died` `the family of the deceased` `the will of the deceased` `the grants` EMPTY

GIVEN
    `the deceased person`             IS A `Deceased person`
    `the will and its further facts`  IS A `A will, with the facts about it that only this Act asks`
    `an application has been made`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the date of the application`     IS A MAYBE DATE
    `permission has been given`       IS A BOOLEAN
    `a grant of the remainder`        IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `A case under this Act`
`a case` MEANS `A case under this Act` WITH
    `the deceased`                                  IS `the deceased person`
    `the will in question`                          IS `the will and its further facts`
    `an application for an order under section 28(1) has been made` IS `an application has been made`
    `the date of that application`                  IS `the date of the application`
    `a court has given permission for the application to be made after the end of that period` IS `permission has been given`
    `a grant limited to the remainder of the testator’s estate has previously been made or is made at the same time` IS `a grant of the remainder`


§§ `Worked facts — the people, the wills and the cases`

`the WA Jaya`    MEANS `a WA person` "Jaya"    "jaya"    (JUST (YMD 2005 9 9))  `living`                     EMPTY FALSE
`the WA Ismail`  MEANS `a WA person` "Ismail"  "ismail"  (JUST (YMD 1975 7 11)) (`died on` (YMD 2018 2 20)) (LIST (`a WA legitimate child` `the WA Jaya`)) FALSE
`the WA Amrita`  MEANS `a WA person` "Amrita"  "amrita"  (JUST (YMD 1950 4 2))  (`died on` (YMD 2020 1 5))  (LIST (`a WA legitimate child` `the WA Ismail`)) FALSE
`the WA Bala`    MEANS `a WA person` "Bala"    "bala"    NOTHING                `living`                     EMPTY FALSE
`the WA Chandra` MEANS `a WA person` "Chandra" "chandra" NOTHING                `living`                     EMPTY FALSE
`the WA Devi`    MEANS `a WA person` "Devi"    "devi"    NOTHING                `living`                     EMPTY FALSE
`the WA Gita`    MEANS `a WA person` "Gita"    "gita"    NOTHING                `living`                     EMPTY FALSE
`the WA Harun`   MEANS `a WA person` "Harun"   "harun"   NOTHING                `living`                     EMPTY FALSE
-- SURI IS THE PRIVILEGED CAST'S INFANT CHILD, and she exists because s 27(3)
-- now reads whose child the appointment names (CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X1). Every
-- privileged testator below whose will appoints a guardian must actually have
-- the child that appointment is of, or the appointment is not one s 27(3)
-- reaches. Jaya could not serve for all of them: she was born in 2005 and two
-- of the privileged testators died in 1938. Nothing is recorded of Suri but
-- that she is her father's child, which is the only fact s 27(3) asks about
-- her — see the note on that subsection for why the "infant" half of "the
-- infant children of the testator" is NOT read here.
`the WA Suri`    MEANS `a WA person` "Suri"    "suri"    NOTHING                `living`                     EMPTY FALSE

-- Eugene was born on 1 June 2000 and executed his will on 1 June 2019, his
-- nineteenth birthday exactly: he is 19, and so under 21. (REPAIR,
-- ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V10: three places in this file used to say he was 18,
-- with an arithmetic gloss corresponding to nothing in the data. The s 4
-- answer is unchanged; the stated fact was wrong.)
`the WA Eugene`  MEANS `a WA person` "Eugene"  "eugene"  (JUST (YMD 2000 6 1))  (`died on` (YMD 2023 4 4))  EMPTY FALSE
-- Farid is a soldier in actual military service and was 19 when he made his
-- will; s 27(1) reaches both facts.
`the WA Farid`   MEANS `a WA person` "Farid"   "farid"   (JUST (YMD 2004 1 1))  (`died on` (YMD 2023 5 5))  (LIST (`a WA legitimate child` `the WA Jaya`)) TRUE
-- Kamala died on 3 March 1990: before 26 June 1992 (s 5(9)) and before
-- 29 May 1992 (s 10(4)), so neither the s 5 route nor the s 10(3) saving is
-- open on her will.
`the WA Kamala`  MEANS `a WA person` "Kamala"  "kamala"  (JUST (YMD 1930 1 1))  (`died on` (YMD 1990 3 3))  EMPTY FALSE
-- Nirmala is known to have died, but the date is not in evidence. Nothing in
-- this Act that turns on a death after a named day can be applied to her will,
-- and the aggregate says so instead of reporting a reason it cannot support.
`the WA Nirmala` MEANS `a WA person` "Nirmala" "nirmala" (JUST (YMD 1950 2 2))  `dead, the date of death not known` EMPTY FALSE
-- Osman is a soldier in actual military service who died ON 29 August 1938 —
-- the day s 27(2) and s 27(3) both name, and which on AMBIGUITY A2's reading
-- falls outside both. s 27(1), which carries no commencement at all, still
-- reaches him. The rest of the cast is reused across the dates; the dates are
-- the only thing under test here.
`the WA Osman`   MEANS `a WA person` "Osman"   "osman"   (JUST (YMD 1915 1 1))  (`died on` (YMD 1938 8 29)) (LIST (`a WA legitimate child` `the WA Suri`)) TRUE
-- Rahim's two wills straddle 29 August 1938, which is the day from which
-- s 13(2)'s saving for a will made in contemplation of a marriage runs.
`the WA Rahim`   MEANS `a WA person` "Rahim"   "rahim"   (JUST (YMD 1900 1 1))  (`died on` (YMD 1975 6 6))  EMPTY FALSE
-- THE TWO s 27 ROUTES THE CAST DID NOT COVER (ROUND 2, R9). Pak is a mariner or
-- seaman being at sea, which is s 27(1)'s second limb; Quek is a member of
-- naval forces "so circumstanced that if he were a soldier he would be in
-- actual military service", which is s 27(4). Neither could be built before,
-- because the constructor hardcoded both facts FALSE — and the aggregate's own
-- verdict names the mariner in terms.
`the WA Pak`     MEANS `a WA person, in full` "Pak"  "pak"  (JUST (YMD 2004 3 3)) (`died on` (YMD 2023 6 6)) (LIST (`a WA legitimate child` `the WA Suri`)) FALSE TRUE  FALSE
`the WA Quek`    MEANS `a WA person, in full` "Quek" "quek" (JUST (YMD 2004 4 4)) (`died on` (YMD 2023 7 7)) (LIST (`a WA legitimate child` `the WA Suri`)) FALSE FALSE TRUE

-- THE TWO COMMENCEMENT BOUNDARIES, ONE PERSON EITHER SIDE OF EACH (ROUND 2:
-- the section-level tests for s 5(9) and s 10(4) used to be pitched at 2020 and
-- 1990, nowhere near the days the subsections name, so a transposed literal
-- would have passed). s 5(9) names 26 June 1992 and s 10(4) names 29 May 1992,
-- and on AMBIGUITY A2's reading the day itself falls outside both.
`the WA Salmah`  MEANS `a WA person` "Salmah"  "salmah"  (JUST (YMD 1930 1 1)) (`died on` (YMD 1992 5 29)) EMPTY FALSE
`the WA Tariq`   MEANS `a WA person` "Tariq"   "tariq"   (JUST (YMD 1930 1 1)) (`died on` (YMD 1992 5 30)) EMPTY FALSE
`the WA Usha`    MEANS `a WA person` "Usha"    "usha"    (JUST (YMD 1930 1 1)) (`died on` (YMD 1992 6 26)) EMPTY FALSE
`the WA Vikram`  MEANS `a WA person` "Vikram"  "vikram"  (JUST (YMD 1930 1 1)) (`died on` (YMD 1992 6 27)) EMPTY FALSE
-- And the day after the one s 27(2) and s 27(3) name, so that the 29 August 1938
-- literal is pinned from both sides rather than only from Osman's side of it.
`the WA Wahid`   MEANS `a WA person` "Wahid"   "wahid"   (JUST (YMD 1915 1 1)) (`died on` (YMD 1938 8 30)) (LIST (`a WA legitimate child` `the WA Suri`)) TRUE

-- TWO DESCENDANTS CARRYING THE SAME REFERENCE. This is NOT a legal scenario and
-- no probate file should look like this: `the reference for this person` is an
-- identity, and two different people sharing one is a malformed file. It exists
-- for one purpose, which the note on `s 26 — the devise or bequest shall not
-- lapse` asks for in terms — that rule asks three things of ONE person, and on
-- every well-formed file `the donee as issue` is a singleton, so nothing
-- distinguishes one `any` over three conjuncts from three independent `any`s.
-- Yasmin died in Amrita's lifetime and left no issue; Zainab is alive and has a
-- living child. Split across two people the three limbs are all satisfied
-- SOMEWHERE; asked of one person, none satisfies all three.
`the WA Aisha`   MEANS `a WA person` "Aisha"   "aisha"   NOTHING `living` EMPTY FALSE
`the WA Zainab`  MEANS `a WA person` "Zainab"  "zahra"   NOTHING `living` (LIST (`a WA legitimate child` `the WA Aisha`)) FALSE
`the WA Yasmin`  MEANS `a WA person` "Yasmin"  "zahra"   NOTHING (`died on` (YMD 2018 2 20)) EMPTY FALSE
-- A CIVILIAN whose informal will would, as a disposition of personal estate,
-- have been valid before the making of this Act. s 27 does not reach him, and
-- until this fixture existed the `s 27 — this section applies to the person`
-- conjunct
-- could be deleted from s 27(1) and s 27(2) with the suite still green.
`the WA Rashid`  MEANS `a WA person` "Rashid"  "rashid"  (JUST (YMD 1990 5 5)) (`died on` (YMD 2023 8 8)) (LIST (`a WA legitimate child` `the WA Suri`)) FALSE
`the WA Zubaida` MEANS `a WA person` "Zubaida" "zubaida" (JUST (YMD 1950 4 2)) (`died on` (YMD 2020 1 5)) (LIST (`a WA legitimate child` `the WA Yasmin`), (`a WA legitimate child` `the WA Zainab`)) FALSE

