`an application to rectify made 10 months after a probate that was resealed` MEANS `the rectification case` (LIST `the probate resealed on 1 September 2020, granted on 1 March 2020`) TRUE (JUST (YMD 2021 1 1)) FALSE FALSE
-- The same dates, but the grant is a PLAIN grant of letters of administration
-- sealed under section 47(1). s 28(5) does not reach it, so the clock runs from
-- 1 March 2020 and an application on 1 January 2021 is ten months late.
-- (ROUND 2, R9.)
`an application to rectify made 10 months after letters of administration that were sealed` MEANS `the rectification case` (LIST `the letters of administration sealed on 1 September 2020, granted on 1 March 2020`) TRUE (JUST (YMD 2021 1 1)) FALSE FALSE
`an application to rectify whose date is not in evidence`     MEANS `the rectification case` (LIST `the grant of probate made on 1 March 2020`) TRUE NOTHING FALSE FALSE
-- The six-month boundary, both sides of it. The grant is 1 March 2020, so the
-- period ends on 1 September 2020: an application on that day is "no later
-- than", and one on 2 September 2020 is not.
`an application to rectify made on the last day of the 6 months`  MEANS `the rectification case` (LIST `the grant of probate made on 1 March 2020`) TRUE (JUST (YMD 2020 9 1)) FALSE FALSE
`an application to rectify made one day after the 6 months`       MEANS `the rectification case` (LIST `the grant of probate made on 1 March 2020`) TRUE (JUST (YMD 2020 9 2)) FALSE FALSE
-- The month-end boundary. Six months after 31 August 2020 is 28 February 2021,
-- February having no thirty-first day. An application on 1 March 2021 is late,
-- which the old day-carrying arithmetic reported as in time.
`an application to rectify made on 28 February 2021, the grant being on 31 August 2020` MEANS `the rectification case` (LIST `the grant of probate made on 31 August 2020`) TRUE (JUST (YMD 2021 2 28)) FALSE FALSE
`an application to rectify made on 1 March 2021, the grant being on 31 August 2020`     MEANS `the rectification case` (LIST `the grant of probate made on 31 August 2020`) TRUE (JUST (YMD 2021 3 1))  FALSE FALSE
-- Permission given, and the date of the application not in evidence. s 28(2)'s
-- excepting words lift the bar whatever the dates, so this is not indeterminate.
`an application to rectify whose date is not in evidence, made with the permission of a court` MEANS `the rectification case` (LIST `the grant of probate made on 1 March 2020`) TRUE NOTHING TRUE FALSE
-- Two grants, the earlier of which is undated. The application would be in time
-- measured from the DATED one, and that is exactly the answer this module must
-- not give.
`an application to rectify, one of the two grants being undated` MEANS `the rectification case` (LIST `a grant of probate whose date is not in evidence`, `the grant of probate made on 1 March 2020`) TRUE (JUST (YMD 2020 8 1)) FALSE FALSE


§§ `Running the rules on the facts`

-- ASSERTIONS, NOT DEMONSTRATIONS. Every directive below states the answer the
-- ACT gives and fails if the module gives another. That is a repair
-- (ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V15): this section used to be 123 `#EVAL`s with their
-- expected answers in trailing comments, so `l4 run` printed 123 values that
-- only a human eye compared, and a rule could regress without anything going
-- red. Three of the four serious defects found by review — s 18's co-presence,
-- s 6(3) being computed and never read, and s 5(3)'s "so far as" flattened into
-- a whole-will verdict — sat in exactly that gap.
--
-- THE EXPECTED ANSWERS WERE DERIVED FROM THE ACT AND THEN RUN, never the other
-- way round. Where the two disagreed the encoding was changed, not the
-- assertion.
--
-- THE BRANCHES ARE WALKED DELIBERATELY: every rule with more than one outcome
-- is asserted on a case that reaches each outcome; and every threshold — 21
-- years of age, two witnesses, 26 June 1992, 29 May 1992, 29 August 1938, six
-- months — is asserted on both sides of the boundary and, where the Act leaves
-- the day itself in neither limb, on the day itself.
--
-- THE DENIALS, AND THE TWO THAT CANNOT BE WALKED (corrected, ROUND 2, R6). An
-- earlier version of this note said, without qualification, that "every denial
-- is asserted on a case where its antecedent HOLDS". Five of the seven are:
-- ss 6(3)(a)-(e), 9, 10(2), 11 and 12 — the last of these only since the `Will`
-- constructor took its executors as a parameter, before which no case file could
-- make an attesting witness an executor. TWO ARE NOT, and cannot be: s 5(4) and
-- s 14 have no antecedent recorded anywhere in this cleanroom, because s 5(4)'s
-- is a fact that s 5(4) and s 7(2) between them give no effect at all, and
-- s 14's is a presumption that the section abolishes and nobody proves. Both
-- rules are therefore a constant FALSE, and the single `#ASSERT NOT` on each
-- holds whatever else the file says. That is inherent in the denial idiom for a
-- denial with nothing to deny, and it is named here rather than left for a
-- reader to discover by mutating the file.
--
-- AND THE ANTECEDENTS ARE ASSERTED SEPARATELY WHERE THEY ARE COMPUTED. A rule
-- that conjoins FALSE gives its antecedent no test, so a traversal or a
-- disjunction inside one is invisible to `#ASSERT NOT`. s 9's traversal and
-- s 6(3)(a)-(e)'s five-paragraph list are therefore named as decisions of their
-- own and asserted on both sides; ss 10(2), 11 and 12 read facts or predicates
-- that are asserted on both sides already.
--
-- WHAT IS STILL `#EVAL` AND WHY. Four directives print a value for a reader
-- rather than testing one: the three `MAYBE DATE` computations at s 28, where
-- the interesting question is which date the rule picked and the assertion
-- immediately below each one pins it, and one list-valued s 26 helper. They are
-- marked where they occur.
--
-- HOW THIS SUITE WAS MEASURED, AND WHAT IS STILL NOT COVERED (ROUND 2). An
-- assertion that would hold whatever the rule under test did passes exactly as
-- green as one that would not, so the suite was measured by MUTATING THE CODE
-- rather than the expectations: every conjunct and disjunct in every rule above
-- was deleted, one at a time, and the suite re-run. Of the 98 such lines, 31
-- cannot be deleted at all without a type error, 58 are caught — deleting them
-- turns at least one assertion red — and NINE ARE NOT CAUGHT. Every one of the
-- nine is named here rather than left to be rediscovered:
--
--   * FIVE ARE THE DENIAL IDIOM, and are inherent in it. A rule that ends
--     `AND FALSE` returns FALSE whatever its antecedent computes, so deleting
--     the antecedent from ss 6(3)(a)-(e), 9, 10(2), 11 or 12 changes nothing.
--     That is the point of the idiom — the antecedent is there to be READ, not
--     to have an effect — and it is why each of those five antecedents is
--     asserted separately, on both sides, as a decision or a fact of its own.
--   * FOUR ARE PRESUPPOSITIONS that another conjunct of the same rule entails
--     on any well-formed file: s 18's opening subject, s 13(1)'s "a will made
--     in exercise of a power of appointment", and both of s 26's first two
--     limbs. Each is stated because the section states it. They are listed
--     again in the closing section, under (5).
--
-- THE SAME MEASUREMENT ON THE MODULE AS IT STOOD BEFORE THIS ROUND: 92
-- candidate lines, 31 uncompilable, 31 caught and THIRTY NOT CAUGHT — with 218
-- assertions, all green. That is the number this section exists to report,
-- because it is the one a passing suite conceals. Among the thirty were s 18's
-- tie to the revoking act and s 6(3)'s evidential conjunct — two of the sixteen
-- repairs the previous round had just made, neither of which any assertion
-- constrained.
--
-- TWO OF TODAY'S NINE ARE LINES THAT DID NOT EXIST BEFORE, and that is a gain
-- rather than a regression: s 9's traversal and s 6(3)(a)-(e)'s list used to be
-- written inline inside their denials, spanning several lines, so this sweep
-- never generated a candidate for them and they were invisible to it as well as
-- to the suite. Naming them made them assertable — they are asserted on both
-- sides now — at the cost of one uncatchable line each, for the reason the
-- denial idiom gives above.
--
-- WHAT ACTUALLY GATES A WRONG ANSWER, WHICH IS NOT THE EXIT CODE (ROUND 2, R1).
-- A failed `#ASSERT` surfaces as an Error diagnostic in the output of `l4 run`
-- and NOWHERE ELSE: measured on this tree, `l4 run` on a module with three
-- failed assertions still exits 0, and so does `l4 check`. Anyone running these
-- as tests has to READ the output, or diff a golden that captures it. Saying
-- that the conversion from `#EVAL` to `#ASSERT` makes `l4 run` "fail" on a wrong
-- answer would be false of the process, and it is the exit code that a script
-- would rely on.

-- THE CROSS-MODULE ROUND, AND WHAT THE MEASUREMENT ABOVE NO LONGER COVERS. The
-- sweep reported above was run on the module as it stood at the end of round 2,
-- when it carried 328 assertions. It has NOT been re-run. Thirteen assertions
-- were added at the cross-module round and the count is now 341; the lines that
-- were added with them are named here rather than left inside a coverage claim
-- that no longer reaches them:
--   * s 27(3)'s new "of the infant children of the testator" limb — CAUGHT.
--     `the case of the will of Farid, appointing a guardian of a child who is
--     not his` differs from `the case of the will of Farid` in that limb alone,
--     and s 27(1) and s 27(2) are separately asserted TRUE on it so that the
--     gate cannot be the reason.
--   * the gate of `s 27(3) — the appointments contained in the will that are of
--     full force and effect` — CAUGHT, and by the fixtures that were already
--     there: Osman fails the commencement, Rashid fails the privilege.
--   * the distributive filter, as distinct from its emptiness — CAUGHT by the
--     two-guardian will, on which the boolean and the list disagree about how
--     much of the will was saved.
--   * the two join probes — asserted directly, on a case where they are
--     non-empty and on two where they are empty.
-- The nine uncaught lines from the round-2 sweep are unaffected: none of them
-- is in s 27(3), and nothing this round touched them.

-- The helpers ----------------------------------------------------------------
-- Not rules of the Act, but the boundary arithmetic every date rule stands on,
-- so the boundaries are asserted here once instead of five times over.

-- AMBIGUITY A2: the day named by a commencement falls in neither limb, so
-- `falls strictly after` must be FALSE on the day itself.
#ASSERT NOT `falls strictly after` (JUST (YMD 1992 6 26)) (YMD 1992 6 26)
#ASSERT     `falls strictly after` (JUST (YMD 1992 6 27)) (YMD 1992 6 26)
#ASSERT NOT `falls strictly after` (JUST (YMD 1992 6 25)) (YMD 1992 6 26)
-- An unproved date defeats the limb rather than being presumed either way.
#ASSERT NOT `falls strictly after` NOTHING (YMD 1992 6 26)

-- s 13(2) says "on or after", and that boundary runs the other way.
#ASSERT NOT `falls on or after` (JUST (YMD 1938 8 28)) (YMD 1938 8 29)
#ASSERT     `falls on or after` (JUST (YMD 1938 8 29)) (YMD 1938 8 29)
#ASSERT NOT `falls on or after` NOTHING                (YMD 1938 8 29)

-- AMBIGUITY A6: a person who died ON the day is not shown to have been living
-- at the moment of the death in question.
#ASSERT NOT `was living on` `the WA Ismail`  (YMD 2018 2 20)
#ASSERT     `was living on` `the WA Ismail`  (YMD 2018 2 19)
#ASSERT     `was living on` `the WA Jaya`    (YMD 2020 1 5)
#ASSERT NOT `was living on` `the WA Nirmala` (YMD 2020 1 5)

#ASSERT     `is proved to fall strictly before` (JUST (YMD 2015 3 1)) (JUST (YMD 2018 6 6))
#ASSERT NOT `is proved to fall strictly before` (JUST (YMD 2018 6 6)) (JUST (YMD 2015 3 1))
#ASSERT NOT `is proved to fall strictly before` (JUST (YMD 2015 3 1)) (JUST (YMD 2015 3 1))
#ASSERT NOT `is proved to fall strictly before` NOTHING               (JUST (YMD 2018 6 6))
#ASSERT NOT `is proved to fall strictly before` (JUST (YMD 2015 3 1)) NOTHING

#ASSERT `the twenty-first anniversary of` (YMD 2000 6 1) EQUALS YMD 2021 6 1
-- A LEAP-DAY BIRTH. `Date` is the lenient constructor, so the twenty-first
-- anniversary of 29 February 2000 falls on 1 March 2021, there being no
-- 29 February in 2021. That is the ordinary reading of attaining 21 years of
-- age for a leap-day birth, and it is why the anniversary is built with `Date`
-- and not `YMD`, which would refuse. Nothing used to assert it.
#ASSERT `the twenty-first anniversary of` (YMD 2000 2 29) EQUALS YMD 2021 3 1

-- s 28(2)'s six months. The month-end cases are the repair (V6): six months
-- after 31 August is the end of February, February having no thirty-first day.
#ASSERT `six months after` (YMD 2020 3 1)  EQUALS YMD 2020 9 1
#ASSERT `six months after` (YMD 2020 8 31) EQUALS YMD 2021 2 28
#ASSERT `six months after` (YMD 2023 8 31) EQUALS YMD 2024 2 29
#ASSERT `six months after` (YMD 2020 9 1)  EQUALS YMD 2021 3 1

#ASSERT `the earliest of` (LIST (YMD 2020 3 1), (YMD 2020 2 1)) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2020 2 1)
#ASSERT `the earliest of` (LIST (YMD 2020 2 1)) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2020 2 1)
-- No grant at all, so no date. The base case of the recursion, which nothing
-- used to assert.
#ASSERT `the earliest of` EMPTY EQUALS NOTHING

-- s 4 -----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Amrita was born in 1950 and made her will in 2015.
#ASSERT `the age of the testator when the will was made` `the will of Amrita` EQUALS `21 years of age or over`
-- Eugene was born on 1 June 2000 and made this will on 1 June 2019.
#ASSERT `the age of the testator when the will was made` `the will of Eugene, made at 19` EQUALS `under 21 years of age`
-- An undated will cannot be measured against a date of birth.
#ASSERT `the age of the testator when the will was made` `the will of Amrita, the date of execution not being in evidence` EQUALS `not in evidence, the date of birth of the testator or the date of execution of the will not being known`
-- THE BOUNDARY. Eugene attains 21 on 1 June 2021; a will made the day before is
-- an infant's and a will made on the day is not.
#ASSERT `the age of the testator when the will was made` `the will of Eugene, made on the day before his twenty-first birthday` EQUALS `under 21 years of age`
#ASSERT `the age of the testator when the will was made` `the will of Eugene, made on his twenty-first birthday` EQUALS `21 years of age or over`

#ASSERT     `s 4 — will of infant invalid` `the will of Eugene, made at 19`
#ASSERT NOT `s 4 — will of infant invalid` `the will of Amrita`
-- AMBIGUITY A1: an undated will is not PROVED to be an infant's ...
#ASSERT NOT `s 4 — will of infant invalid` `the will of Amrita, the date of execution not being in evidence`
-- ... and not proved to be an adult's either.
#ASSERT NOT `s 4 — the testator is proved to have been 21 years of age or over` `the will of Amrita, the date of execution not being in evidence`
#ASSERT     `s 4 — the testator is proved to have been 21 years of age or over` `the will of Amrita`
#ASSERT     `s 4 — the testator is proved to have been 21 years of age or over` `the will of Eugene, made on his twenty-first birthday`

-- s 5 -----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The will executed abroad fails s 6(2) outright ...
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` `the facts about the will of Amrita, executed abroad`
-- ... but conforms to the lex loci actus, and Amrita died in 2020.
#ASSERT     `s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 2020`
#ASSERT     `s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 2020`
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(3)(a) — a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 2020`
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(3)(b)-(d) — the will is treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of it` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 2020`
#ASSERT `s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 2020` EQUALS `treated as properly executed`
#ASSERT     `s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 2020`
#ASSERT     `the will is properly executed` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 2020`

-- Kamala died on 3 March 1990, before 26 June 1992: s 5(9) shuts the section,
-- though the connecting factor is made out on its own terms.
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 1990`
#ASSERT     `s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 1990`
#ASSERT `s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 1990` EQUALS `not treated as properly executed under section 5`
#ASSERT NOT `s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 1990`
#ASSERT NOT `the will is properly executed` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 1990`

-- THE "SO FAR AS" PARAGRAPHS (repair V4). A will conforming to no law but the
-- lex situs of its immovable property is reached by s 5(3)(b) SO FAR ONLY AS it
-- disposes of that property. It is not a properly executed will.
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the place where its immovable property is situated`
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(3)(a) — a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the place where its immovable property is situated`
#ASSERT     `s 5(3)(b)-(d) — the will is treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of it` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the place where its immovable property is situated`
#ASSERT `s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the place where its immovable property is situated` EQUALS `treated as properly executed so far only as the will disposes of immovable property, revokes a will or a provision, or exercises a power of appointment`
#ASSERT NOT `s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the place where its immovable property is situated`
#ASSERT NOT `the will is properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the place where its immovable property is situated`

-- s 5 was never reached at all, the date of death not being in evidence.
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator` `the case of a will executed abroad, the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence`
#ASSERT     `s 5 — some connecting factor is made out, leaving aside the commencement in subsection (9)` `the case of a will executed abroad, the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence`
#ASSERT NOT `s 5 — some connecting factor is made out, leaving aside the commencement in subsection (9)` `the plain case of Amrita`

-- s 5(9)'s OWN BOUNDARY, AT THE DAY IT NAMES (ROUND 2). The section-level test
-- for s 5(9) used to be pitched at a death in 2020 against one in 1990, so the
-- subsection could have carried any literal between them and passed. It names
-- 26 June 1992, and on AMBIGUITY A2's reading the day itself falls in neither
-- limb — "died before 26 June 1992" excludes it and "dies after that date" does
-- not include it.
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator` `the case of a testator who died on 26 June 1992`
#ASSERT     `s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator` `the case of a testator who died on 27 June 1992`
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator` `the case of a testator who died on 30 May 1992`

-- The denial in s 5(4).
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(4) — the will is treated as improperly executed by reason only that its execution was not in accordance with formal requirements contained in the instrument creating the power`

-- EVERY LIMB OF s 5, ONE AT A TIME (ROUND 2, R9). Only two of the nine used to
-- have a fixture at all — s 5(2)(a) and s 5(3)(b) — so six paragraphs of the
-- section could be deleted with every assertion still green, and s 5(3)(a)'s
-- rung in the whole-will branch was asserted only in the negative. Each case
-- below is the same will, failing s 6(2) in every particular, conforming to the
-- one law the case is named for and to no other.
--
-- THE FOUR REMAINING CONNECTING FACTORS OF s 5(2). Each is a whole-will limb.
#ASSERT `s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the testator’s domicile when it was executed`
#ASSERT `s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the testator’s domicile at his death`
#ASSERT `s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the place of the testator’s habitual residence`
#ASSERT `s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the state of the testator’s nationality`
#ASSERT `s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the testator’s domicile when it was executed` EQUALS `treated as properly executed`
#ASSERT `s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the state of the testator’s nationality` EQUALS `treated as properly executed`

-- s 5(3)(a): a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft. There is no
-- "so far as" in paragraph (a), so this reaches the whole will — which is the
-- rung the whole-will branch of `s 5 — the extent ...` carries and nothing used
-- to exercise.
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(2) — the will is to be treated as properly executed` `the case of a will executed on board a vessel`
#ASSERT     `s 5(3)(a) — a will executed on board a vessel or an aircraft is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will executed on board a vessel`
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(3)(b)-(d) — the will is treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of it` `the case of a will executed on board a vessel`
#ASSERT `s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will executed on board a vessel` EQUALS `treated as properly executed`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will executed on board a vessel` EQUALS `valid`

-- s 5(3)(c) and s 5(3)(d), each alone. Both are "so far as" paragraphs, so
-- neither makes the will good as a whole.
#ASSERT     `s 5(3)(b)-(d) — the will is treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of it` `the case of a will conforming only to the law by reference to which the will it revokes was properly executed`
#ASSERT     `s 5(3)(b)-(d) — the will is treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of it` `the case of a will conforming only to the law governing the essential validity of the power`
#ASSERT `s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law by reference to which the will it revokes was properly executed` EQUALS `treated as properly executed so far only as the will disposes of immovable property, revokes a will or a provision, or exercises a power of appointment`
#ASSERT `s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` `the case of a will conforming only to the law governing the essential validity of the power` EQUALS `treated as properly executed so far only as the will disposes of immovable property, revokes a will or a provision, or exercises a power of appointment`

-- THE APPOINTMENT SLICE, which s 7(1) reads (AMBIGUITY A11). The two whole-will
-- routes reach the appointment and so does s 5(3)(d); s 5(3)(b) does not, and
-- neither does anything at all once s 5(9) shuts the section.
#ASSERT     `s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed so far as it exercises a power of appointment` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 2020`
#ASSERT     `s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed so far as it exercises a power of appointment` `the case of a will executed on board a vessel`
#ASSERT     `s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed so far as it exercises a power of appointment` `the case of a will conforming only to the law governing the essential validity of the power`
#ASSERT NOT `s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed so far as it exercises a power of appointment` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the place where its immovable property is situated`
#ASSERT NOT `s 5 — the will is treated as properly executed so far as it exercises a power of appointment` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 1990`

-- s 6 -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ASSERT     `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` `the facts about the will of Amrita`
#ASSERT     `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` `the facts about the will of Amrita`
#ASSERT     `executed in the manner required by this Act` `the facts about the will of Amrita`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` `the facts about the will of Amrita, not in writing`
-- THE WITNESS THRESHOLD, both sides. One subscribing witness is not enough; two
-- are; three are.
#ASSERT NOT `at least 2 of the attesting witnesses subscribed the will in the presence of the testator` `the will of Amrita, only one witness having subscribed in her presence`
#ASSERT     `at least 2 of the attesting witnesses subscribed the will in the presence of the testator` `the will of Amrita`
#ASSERT     `at least 2 of the attesting witnesses subscribed the will in the presence of the testator` `the will of Amrita, a legacy to one of her three witnesses`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` `the facts about the will of Amrita, only one witness having subscribed in her presence`
-- s 6(2)'s SECOND LIMB, LEFT ALONE TO DECIDE (ROUND 2, R4). In writing, signed
-- at the foot, both witnesses subscribing in her presence — everything the
-- subsection asks except that the signature was never made or acknowledged
-- before 2 or more witnesses present at the same time. The only fixture that
-- used to falsify this limb, the will executed abroad, fails all three at once
-- and has no witnesses, so the limb could be deleted with the suite still green.
#ASSERT     `at least 2 of the attesting witnesses subscribed the will in the presence of the testator` `the will of Amrita, the signature never acknowledged before the witnesses`
#ASSERT     `s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature` `the facts about the will of Amrita, the signature never acknowledged before the witnesses`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` `the facts about the will of Amrita, the signature never acknowledged before the witnesses`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` `the facts about the will of Amrita, the signature never acknowledged before the witnesses`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will whose signature was never acknowledged before the witnesses` EQUALS `not valid, the will not being in writing or not executed in the manner mentioned in section 6(2), and not being treated as properly executed under section 5`

-- s 6(3): THE DEEMING, AND THE POSITIONAL ANTECEDENT IT TURNS ON (repairs V2
-- and V3). A signature at the foot answers it; so does one beside the end; one
-- at the head of the will, above every dispositive word, does not, however
-- plain the testator's intention.
#ASSERT     `s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature` `the facts about the will of Amrita`
#ASSERT     `s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed beside the end`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed at the head`
-- And the deeming now does the work s 6(3) exists to do: it satisfies s 6(2)'s
-- first limb for a will not signed at the foot or end.
#ASSERT     `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed beside the end`
#ASSERT     `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed beside the end`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed at the head`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed at the head`
-- s 6(3)'s OTHER CONJUNCT, AND ITS PRESUPPOSITION, EACH LEFT ALONE TO DECIDE
-- (ROUND 2, R7 and R11). All three of these wills have the signature beside the
-- end, so the positional conjunct is TRUE in each and exactly one other thing
-- differs from `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed beside the end`,
-- which is deemed valid:
--   the first has the same signature in the same place, but it is NOT apparent
--     on the face of the will that the testator intended to give effect by it —
--     the evidential half of the "so ... that", which used to be hardcoded TRUE
--     in the only `Will` constructor and could not be falsified;
--   the second records no signature of the testator and none by anyone signing
--     for her in her presence and by her direction, so there is no "signature
--     of the testator, or of the person signing for him as mentioned in
--     subsection (2)" whose position could be excused.
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed beside the end, the intention not apparent`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed beside the end, the intention not apparent`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed beside the end, the intention not apparent`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature` `the facts about the will of Amrita, the position stated but nobody having signed`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` `the facts about the will of Amrita, the position stated but nobody having signed`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` `the facts about the will of Amrita, the position stated but nobody having signed`
-- s 6(2)'s FIRST LIMB HAS TWO ALTERNATIVES BESIDES THE DEEMING, and this is the
-- second: "signed at the foot or end thereof by the testator, OR BY SOME OTHER
-- PERSON IN HIS PRESENCE AND BY HIS DIRECTION". Amrita did not sign; another
-- did, at the foot, in her presence and by her direction. Until the constructor
-- took the fact as a parameter it was hardcoded FALSE, and the alternative could
-- be deleted from s 6(2) with the suite still green. (ROUND 2.)
#ASSERT     `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed for her by another`
#ASSERT     `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed for her by another`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will signed at the foot for the testator by another at her direction` EQUALS `valid`
-- And the same will with the s 6(3) route closed, so the amanuensis alternative
-- is the only thing holding the limb up: it is not apparent on the face of the
-- will that the testator intended to give effect by that signature, so the
-- deeming fails, and s 6(2) is satisfied all the same. On the will above both
-- routes hold at once — a signature at the foot is also a signature at the end —
-- so that one cannot separate them. (ROUND 2.)
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(3) — the will is deemed valid as far as regards the position of the signature` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed for her by another, the intention not apparent`
#ASSERT     `s 6(2) — the manner of execution` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed for her by another, the intention not apparent`
#ASSERT     `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` `the facts about the will of Amrita, signed for her by another, the intention not apparent`
-- And in s 10(3)'s counterfactual, where the same three alternatives stand.
#ASSERT     `the will is duly executed without the attestation of any such person` `the case of a will signed for the testator by another, the intention not apparent`

-- The denial in s 6(3)(a)-(e), run on a signature that IS in two of the five
-- enumerated positions, and on one that is in none of them.
-- Same shape as s 9 (ROUND 2): the list is asserted on both sides in its own
-- right, and then the denial is asserted over it.
#ASSERT     `one or more of the circumstances mentioned in paragraphs (a) to (e) is present` `the signature among the words of the attestation clause, after a blank space`
#ASSERT     `one or more of the circumstances mentioned in paragraphs (a) to (e) is present` `the signature beside the end of the will`
#ASSERT     `one or more of the circumstances mentioned in paragraphs (a) to (e) is present` `the signature at the head of the will, above all the dispositive words`
#ASSERT NOT `one or more of the circumstances mentioned in paragraphs (a) to (e) is present` `the signature in an unremarkable position`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(3)(a)-(e) — the will is affected by any of the enumerated circumstances` `the signature among the words of the attestation clause, after a blank space`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(3)(a)-(e) — the will is affected by any of the enumerated circumstances` `the signature in an unremarkable position`

-- s 6(4): the same gift, in three positions.
#ASSERT     `s 6(4) — the signature is operative to give effect to the disposition` (`the devise to Ismail` "above the signature")
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(4) — the signature is operative to give effect to the disposition` (`the devise to Ismail` "below the signature")
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(4) — the signature is operative to give effect to the disposition` (`the devise to Ismail` "inserted after the signature was made")

-- s 7 and s 8 ---------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 7(1) is a prohibition (repair V5): it says when an appointment is NOT
-- valid, and says nothing about when it is.
#ASSERT     `s 7(1) — the appointment made by the will in exercise of a power is invalid for want of execution in the manner required by this Act` `the case of an appointing will not in writing`
#ASSERT NOT `s 7(1) — the appointment made by the will in exercise of a power is invalid for want of execution in the manner required by this Act` `the case of an appointing will, Amrita marrying Gita in 2018`
-- No appointment is made at all, so there is none to invalidate.
#ASSERT NOT `s 7(1) — the appointment made by the will in exercise of a power is invalid for want of execution in the manner required by this Act` `the plain case of Amrita`
-- The same, on a will that is NOT validly executed: no appointment is made, so
-- there is none to invalidate however badly the will was executed. The plain
-- case above cannot show this, its will being good under s 6. (ROUND 2.)
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` (`the case of a will not in writing`'s `the will in question`)
#ASSERT NOT `s 7(1) — the appointment made by the will in exercise of a power is invalid for want of execution in the manner required by this Act` `the case of a will not in writing`
-- AMBIGUITY A11, AND THE CONTRADICTION IT REPAIRS (ROUND 2, R2). Both wills
-- below are the same appointing will, not in writing and so executed in no
-- manner s 6 requires. They differ in one fact: which law outside this Act the
-- execution conformed to.
--   The first conformed to "the law governing the essential validity of the
--   power", so s 5(3)(d) treats it as properly executed so far as it exercises
--   the appointment, and s 5(1) — "This section shall take effect
--   notwithstanding any other provisions of this Act" — stops s 7(1) taking
--   effect against that. Before the repair the module reported BOTH that the
--   will was treated as properly executed so far as it exercises a power of
--   appointment AND that the appointment was invalid for want of execution.
--   The second conformed only to the lex situs of its immovable property, which
--   is not the appointment, so s 5(1) has nothing to disarm and s 7(1) bites.
#ASSERT NOT `s 7(1) — the appointment made by the will in exercise of a power is invalid for want of execution in the manner required by this Act` `the case of an appointing will conforming only to the law governing the essential validity of the power`
#ASSERT     `s 7(1) — the appointment made by the will in exercise of a power is invalid for want of execution in the manner required by this Act` `the case of an appointing will conforming only to the lex situs of its immovable property`
-- The s 5 side of the same two cases, so that the fork is visible on the file.
#ASSERT `s 5 — the extent to which the will is treated as properly executed` `the case of an appointing will conforming only to the law governing the essential validity of the power` EQUALS `treated as properly executed so far only as the will disposes of immovable property, revokes a will or a provision, or exercises a power of appointment`
#ASSERT NOT `s 6(1) — the will is in writing and executed in the manner mentioned in subsection (2)` (`the case of an appointing will conforming only to the law governing the essential validity of the power`'s `the will in question`)
#ASSERT     `s 7(2) — the will is, so far as respects the execution and attestation thereof, a valid execution of a power of appointment by will` `the facts about the will of Amrita`
#ASSERT NOT `s 7(2) — the will is, so far as respects the execution and attestation thereof, a valid execution of a power of appointment by will` `the facts about the will of Amrita, not in writing`
#ASSERT     `s 8 — the will is valid without any other publication thereof` `the facts about the will of Amrita`
#ASSERT NOT `s 8 — the will is valid without any other publication thereof` `the facts about the will of Amrita, not in writing`

-- ss 9 to 12: the competency denials, each on a case where its antecedent holds
#ASSERT NOT `the attesting witness is to be admitted as a witness to prove the execution of the will` `the will of Amrita, one witness being incompetent` `Bala attesting, incompetent for a reason outside sections 10(2), 11 and 12`
#ASSERT     `the attesting witness is to be admitted as a witness to prove the execution of the will` `the will of Amrita, one witness being incompetent` `Chandra attesting`
-- THE ANTECEDENT, ASSERTED ON BOTH SIDES, AND THEN THE DENIAL (ROUND 2). Both
-- `#ASSERT NOT` on the denial hold whatever the traversal below computes, since
-- the rule conjoins FALSE; naming the antecedent is what gives the traversal a
-- test of its own, and what lets the second of the two denials do any work.
#ASSERT     `a person who attests the execution of the will is incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof` `the will of Amrita, one witness being incompetent`
#ASSERT NOT `a person who attests the execution of the will is incompetent to be admitted a witness to prove the execution thereof` `the will of Amrita`
#ASSERT NOT `s 9 — the will is invalid by reason that a person who attests its execution is incompetent to be admitted a witness` `the will of Amrita, one witness being incompetent`
#ASSERT NOT `s 9 — the will is invalid by reason that a person who attests its execution is incompetent to be admitted a witness` `the will of Amrita`
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(2) — the gift is a ground on which the attesting witness is not to be admitted a witness` `Devi attesting and taking a legacy`
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(2) — the gift is a ground on which the attesting witness is not to be admitted a witness` `Bala attesting`
-- s 11's antecedent is a single recorded fact, so it is asserted directly.
#ASSERT     `Bala attesting as a creditor whose debt is charged`'s `a creditor, or the wife or husband of a creditor, whose debt is charged on the estate by the will`
#ASSERT NOT `Bala attesting`'s `a creditor, or the wife or husband of a creditor, whose debt is charged on the estate by the will`
#ASSERT NOT `s 11 — the charge is a ground on which the creditor is not to be admitted a witness` `Bala attesting as a creditor whose debt is charged`
#ASSERT NOT `s 11 — the charge is a ground on which the creditor is not to be admitted a witness` `Bala attesting`
#ASSERT NOT `the attesting witness is an executor appointed by the will` `the will of Amrita` `Bala attesting`
#ASSERT NOT `s 12 — being an executor of the will is a ground on which the person is not to be admitted a witness` `the will of Amrita` `Bala attesting`
-- s 12's ANTECEDENT, ON A CASE WHERE IT HOLDS (ROUND 2, R6). Bala both attests
-- this will and is appointed executor by it. Until the `Will` constructor took
-- its executors as a parameter, the list was hardcoded EMPTY and this was the
-- one denial in the module whose antecedent no case file could reach — so
-- s 12's `AND FALSE` could be deleted with the whole suite still green, and
-- the run-section header's claim that every denial is asserted on a case where
-- its antecedent holds was untrue of it.
#ASSERT     `the attesting witness is an executor appointed by the will` `the will of Amrita, Bala being both an executor and an attesting witness` `Bala attesting`
#ASSERT NOT `the attesting witness is an executor appointed by the will` `the will of Amrita, Bala being both an executor and an attesting witness` `Chandra attesting`
#ASSERT NOT `s 12 — being an executor of the will is a ground on which the person is not to be admitted a witness` `the will of Amrita, Bala being both an executor and an attesting witness` `Bala attesting`
-- And being an executor does not make him incompetent, which is what s 12 says.
#ASSERT     `the attesting witness is to be admitted as a witness to prove the execution of the will` `the will of Amrita, Bala being both an executor and an attesting witness` `Bala attesting`

-- s 10 ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ASSERT     `a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1)` `Devi attesting and taking a legacy`
#ASSERT     `a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1)` `Bala attesting, his wife taking a legacy`
#ASSERT NOT `a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1)` `Bala attesting`
-- Two witnesses, one of whom takes: striking his attestation out leaves ONE, so
-- s 10(3) cannot save the legacy and it is utterly null and void.
#ASSERT NOT `the will is duly executed without the attestation of any such person` `the case of a legacy to one of two witnesses`
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(3) — the attestation of such a person is disregarded for the purposes of subsection (1)` `the case of a legacy to one of two witnesses`
#ASSERT     `s 10(1) — the gift to the attesting witness or to the witness’s wife or husband is utterly null and void` `the case of a legacy to one of two witnesses` `Devi attesting and taking a legacy`
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(1) — the gift to the attesting witness or to the witness’s wife or husband is utterly null and void` `the case of a legacy to one of two witnesses` `Bala attesting`
#ASSERT     `s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void` `the case of a legacy to one of two witnesses` `a pecuniary legacy of $10,000 to Devi`
-- THE COUNT, LEFT ALONE TO DECIDE (REPAIR, ROUND 2, R5). The comment above used
-- to credit the FALSE answer to the count, and the count was not what produced
-- it: `a plain case` supplies s 10(3)'s counterfactual presence limb as FALSE,
-- so that field alone decided both this case and its three-witness twin, and
-- lowering the threshold from 2 to 1 changed no answer anywhere. This case is
-- the same two-witness will with the presence limb PROVED, so the only thing
-- left that can defeat s 10(3) is that striking the interested witness out
-- leaves ONE disinterested subscribing witness.
#ASSERT NOT `the will is duly executed without the attestation of any such person` `the case of a legacy to one of two witnesses, the presence limb being proved`
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(3) — the attestation of such a person is disregarded for the purposes of subsection (1)` `the case of a legacy to one of two witnesses, the presence limb being proved`
#ASSERT     `s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void` `the case of a legacy to one of two witnesses, the presence limb being proved` `a pecuniary legacy of $10,000 to Devi`
-- And the SUBSCRIPTION half of the same count. Three witnesses, one of whom
-- takes; of the other two, one subscribed out of the testator's sight. The will
-- is duly executed as it stands — two of the three subscribed in her presence,
-- which is all s 6(2) asks — but only ONE disinterested witness both takes
-- nothing and subscribed, so s 10(3) still cannot save the legacy.
#ASSERT     `at least 2 of the attesting witnesses subscribed the will in the presence of the testator` `the will of Amrita, a legacy to one of her three witnesses, another subscribing out of her sight`
#ASSERT NOT `the will is duly executed without the attestation of any such person` `the case of a legacy to one of three witnesses, another subscribing out of her sight`
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(3) — the attestation of such a person is disregarded for the purposes of subsection (1)` `the case of a legacy to one of three witnesses, another subscribing out of her sight`
#ASSERT     `s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void` `the case of a legacy to one of three witnesses, another subscribing out of her sight` `a pecuniary legacy of $10,000 to Devi`
-- And the PRESENCE limb of the same rule, left alone to decide (ROUND 2). The
-- three-witness will again, two disinterested witnesses subscribing in her
-- presence — so the count is satisfied — but nobody has proved the counterfactual
-- s 6(2) presence limb. No case used to have the count made out and the presence
-- limb not, so the presence conjunct could be deleted with the suite still green.
#ASSERT NOT `the will is duly executed without the attestation of any such person` `the case of a legacy to one of three witnesses, the presence limb not being proved`
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(3) — the attestation of such a person is disregarded for the purposes of subsection (1)` `the case of a legacy to one of three witnesses, the presence limb not being proved`
-- Three witnesses: TWO disinterested ones remain, so the attestation is
-- disregarded and the legacy stands. This is the other side of the s 10(3)
-- threshold.
#ASSERT     `the will is duly executed without the attestation of any such person` `the case of a legacy to one of three witnesses`
#ASSERT     `s 10(4) — subsection (3) applies to the will of this testator` `the case of a legacy to one of three witnesses`
-- s 10(4)'s OWN BOUNDARY, AT THE DAY IT NAMES (ROUND 2). Same point as s 5(9)
-- above: the section-level test used to be 2020 against 1990. s 10(4) names
-- 29 May 1992, which is FOUR WEEKS before the day s 5(9) names, so a fixture
-- that cannot tell the two days apart cannot tell the two subsections apart
-- either. (The two dates are the third reading and the commencement of the
-- Wills (Amendment) Act 1992, per the Legislative History at the foot of the
-- source — which says of itself that "It is not part of the Act", and is cited
-- here only to explain why the Act carries two commencement days four weeks
-- apart.)
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(4) — subsection (3) applies to the will of this testator` `the case of a testator who died on 29 May 1992`
#ASSERT     `s 10(4) — subsection (3) applies to the will of this testator` `the case of a testator who died on 30 May 1992`
#ASSERT     `s 10(4) — subsection (3) applies to the will of this testator` `the case of a testator who died on 26 June 1992`
#ASSERT NOT `s 5(9) — this section applies to the will of this testator` `the case of a testator who died on 29 May 1992`
#ASSERT     `s 10(3) — the attestation of such a person is disregarded for the purposes of subsection (1)` `the case of a legacy to one of three witnesses`
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void` `the case of a legacy to one of three witnesses` `a pecuniary legacy of $10,000 to Devi`
-- The same three-witness will, the testator dying on 3 March 1990: s 10(4)
-- withholds s 10(3), and the legacy falls after all.
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(4) — subsection (3) applies to the will of this testator` `the case of a legacy to one of three witnesses, the testator dying in 1990`
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(3) — the attestation of such a person is disregarded for the purposes of subsection (1)` `the case of a legacy to one of three witnesses, the testator dying in 1990`
#ASSERT     `s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void` `the case of a legacy to one of three witnesses, the testator dying in 1990` `a pecuniary legacy of $10,000 to Devi`
-- A gift to a witness that IS a charge for the payment of a debt. It is not
-- avoided — but on THIS file three separate things account for that, and the
-- exception the comment used to credit is the one that does no work: the
-- attesting creditor takes nothing beneficially, so s 10(1)'s witness limb
-- fails of him anyway. Kept because the case is real; see the next block for
-- the one that isolates the exception. (ROUND 2, R8.)
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void` `the case of a will charging the estate with a debt owed to a witness` `a charge on the estate for a debt owed to Bala`

-- s 10(1)'s TWO REMAINING CONJUNCTS, EACH LEFT ALONE TO DECIDE (ROUND 2, R8).
-- One will, one attesting witness, three dispositions to him. He takes the
-- legacy beneficially, so s 10(1)'s witness limb is TRUE of him throughout and
-- each disposition turns on itself alone:
--   the legacy is beneficial and is not a charge, so it is utterly null and
--     void — which is the positive answer the other two are measured against;
--   the charge is beneficial too, and the ONLY thing that saves it is the
--     express exception "other than and except charges and directions for the
--     payment of any debt";
--   the devise is not a charge, and the ONLY thing that saves it is that he
--     does not take it beneficially — he holds it upon trust. Neither of those
--     two conjuncts used to change any answer in this file.
#ASSERT     `a person to whom or to whose spouse there is given or made any disposition as is described in subsection (1)` `Bala attesting, taking a legacy and a charge for his debt`
#ASSERT     `s 10(1) — the gift to the attesting witness or to the witness’s wife or husband is utterly null and void` `the case of a will giving its attesting creditor a legacy, a charge and a devise on trust` `Bala attesting, taking a legacy and a charge for his debt`
#ASSERT     `s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void` `the case of a will giving its attesting creditor a legacy, a charge and a devise on trust` `a pecuniary legacy of $10,000 to Bala`
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void` `the case of a will giving its attesting creditor a legacy, a charge and a devise on trust` `a charge on the estate for a debt owed to Bala`
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void` `the case of a will giving its attesting creditor a legacy, a charge and a devise on trust` `a devise to Bala upon trust, not beneficially`
-- A gift to a person who is not a witness at all.
#ASSERT NOT `s 10(1) — the disposition is utterly null and void` `the case of a legacy to one of two witnesses` `a pecuniary legacy of $10,000 to Chandra`

-- s 13 ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ASSERT NOT `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the plain case of Amrita`
#ASSERT     `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the case of Amrita marrying Gita in 2018, after the will`
-- The general-law gloss, not s 13(1)'s own words: a marriage before the will
-- does not revoke it.
#ASSERT NOT `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the case of Amrita marrying Gita in 2010, before the will`
#ASSERT     `the marriage was solemnised after the will was made` `the will of Amrita` (`a marriage` `the WA Gita` (JUST (YMD 2018 6 6)) TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT `the marriage was solemnised after the will was made` `the will of Amrita` (`a marriage` `the WA Gita` (JUST (YMD 2010 1 1)) TRUE)
-- s 13(1) alone says nothing about the order of the two events.
#ASSERT     `s 13(1) — the will is revoked by the marriage` `the will of Amrita` (`a marriage` `the WA Gita` (JUST (YMD 2010 1 1)) TRUE)
#ASSERT     `s 13(1) — the exception for a will made in exercise of a power of appointment` `the will of Amrita, made in exercise of a power of appointment`
#ASSERT NOT `s 13(1) — the exception for a will made in exercise of a power of appointment` `the will of Amrita`
#ASSERT NOT `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the case of an appointing will, Amrita marrying Gita in 2018`
-- AMBIGUITY A4, LEFT ALONE TO DECIDE (ROUND 2). The exception has two limbs and
-- the only appointing fixture made both out at once, so the "when" clause — the
-- whole of the fork A4 argues — could be deleted with the suite still green.
-- This will IS made in exercise of a power, but the appointed estate WOULD in
-- default pass to the takers s 13(1) names, so on reading (1) the exception does
-- not save it.
#ASSERT NOT `s 13(1) — the exception for a will made in exercise of a power of appointment` `the will of Amrita, made in exercise of a power, the appointed estate passing in default`
#ASSERT     `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the case of a will made under a power whose appointed estate passes in default, Amrita marrying Gita`
-- s 13(2) saves the will against the marriage contemplated and against no other.
#ASSERT NOT `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the case of a will in contemplation of the marriage to Gita, Amrita marrying Gita`
#ASSERT     `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the case of a will in contemplation of the marriage to Gita, Amrita marrying Harun`
#ASSERT     `s 13(2) — the will is expressed to be made in contemplation of this marriage` `the will of Amrita, in contemplation of her marriage to Gita` (`a marriage` `the WA Gita` (JUST (YMD 2018 6 6)) TRUE)
-- AND s 13(2) ASKS TWO THINGS (ROUND 2). This will names Gita as the person the
-- marriage contemplated was to be solemnised with, but it is not "expressed to
-- be made in contemplation of a marriage" — and naming a fiancee is not that.
-- Every fixture used to set the two facts together, so the boolean limb could be
-- deleted with the suite still green.
#ASSERT NOT `s 13(2) — the will is expressed to be made in contemplation of this marriage` `the will of Amrita, naming Gita but not expressed to be made in contemplation of the marriage` (`a marriage` `the WA Gita` (JUST (YMD 2018 6 6)) TRUE)
#ASSERT     `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the case of a will naming Gita but not expressed to be made in contemplation, Amrita marrying Gita`
-- THE 29 AUGUST 1938 BOUNDARY, both sides. s 13(2) reaches a will "made on or
-- after" that day, so Rahim's will of the 29th is saved and his will of the
-- 28th is revoked by the same marriage.
#ASSERT NOT `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the case of the will of Rahim, made on 29 August 1938`
#ASSERT     `s 13 — the will has been revoked by marriage` `the case of the will of Rahim, made on 28 August 1938`

-- s 14 ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ASSERT NOT `s 14 — the will is revoked by a presumption of an intention on the ground of an alteration in circumstances`

-- s 15 ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ASSERT NOT `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the plain case of Amrita`
#ASSERT     `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the case of Amrita marrying Gita in 2018, after the will`
#ASSERT     `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the case of a will revoked by a later will`
#ASSERT     `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the case of a will revoked by a writing declaring an intention to revoke it`
#ASSERT     `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the case of a will burnt by the testator with the intention of revoking it`
#ASSERT     `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the case of a will destroyed by another person at the testator’s direction, with the intention of revoking it`
-- No animus revocandi.
#ASSERT NOT `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the case of a will burnt by the testator without the intention of revoking it`
-- Destroyed by nobody s 15(d) names.
#ASSERT NOT `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the case of a will burnt by a stranger, the testator intending revocation`
-- s 15(d)'s two elements, taken apart.
#ASSERT     `s 15(d) — the will has been destroyed with the intention of revoking it` (`revoked` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE)
#ASSERT     `s 15(d) — the will has been destroyed with the intention of revoking it` (`revoked` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT `s 15(d) — the will has been destroyed with the intention of revoking it` (`revoked` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `s 15(d) — the will has been destroyed with the intention of revoking it` (`revoked` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE)

-- s 16 ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ASSERT NOT `s 16(1) — the alteration is valid and has effect` `an obliteration that leaves the earlier words legible, unattested`
#ASSERT     `s 16(1) — the alteration is valid and has effect` `an obliteration that leaves the earlier words illegible, unattested`
#ASSERT     `s 16(1) — the alteration is valid and has effect` `an interlineation, the earlier words legible, executed in the like manner as the will`
#ASSERT     `s 16(2) — the will, with such alteration as part thereof, is deemed to be duly executed` `an interlineation, the earlier words legible, signed in the margin`
#ASSERT     `s 16(2) — the will, with such alteration as part thereof, is deemed to be duly executed` `an interlineation, the earlier words legible, signed opposite a memorandum`
#ASSERT NOT `s 16(2) — the will, with such alteration as part thereof, is deemed to be duly executed` `an obliteration that leaves the earlier words legible, unattested`
#ASSERT     `s 16(1) — the alteration is valid and has effect` `an interlineation, the earlier words legible, signed in the margin`
#ASSERT     `s 16(1) — the alteration is valid and has effect` `an interlineation, the earlier words legible, signed opposite a memorandum`

-- s 17 ----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Nothing to revive.
#ASSERT `s 17 — the extent of the revival` `the case of a will re-executed but never revoked` EQUALS `not revived`
#ASSERT `s 17 — the extent of the revival` `the case of a will revoked by a later will` EQUALS `not revived`
#ASSERT `s 17 — the extent of the revival` `the case of a will revoked by a later will and then re-executed` EQUALS `revived in whole`
#ASSERT `s 17 — the extent of the revival` `the case of a will partly revoked, then wholly revoked, then revived by codicil` EQUALS `revived, save as to so much thereof as was revoked before the revocation of the whole thereof`
#ASSERT `s 17 — the extent of the revival` `the case of the same will, a contrary intention being shown` EQUALS `revived in whole`
#ASSERT     `s 17(1) — the will has been revived` (`revived` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT     `s 17(1) — the will has been revived` (`revived` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT `s 17(1) — the will has been revived` `no revival`

-- s 18 ----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A conveyance and no revocation: the will operates notwithstanding.
#ASSERT NOT `s 18 — a conveyance or other act made or done subsequently to the execution of the will prevents its operation` `the case of a conveyance made after the will, the will standing`
-- The exception: this conveyance IS the act by which the will was revoked.
#ASSERT     `s 18 — a conveyance or other act made or done subsequently to the execution of the will prevents its operation` `the case of a conveyance that is itself the act by which the will is revoked`
-- REPAIR V1. A conveyance, and a revocation — but the revocation is the
-- marriage, which is no part of any "conveyance or other act made or done
-- subsequently to the execution of a will of or relating to any real or
-- personal estate therein comprised". The old encoding returned TRUE here,
-- which is the opposite of what s 18 protects.
#ASSERT NOT `s 18 — a conveyance or other act made or done subsequently to the execution of the will prevents its operation` `the case of a conveyance made after the will, the will having been revoked by a marriage`
-- Revoked, but with no subsequent conveyance at all.
#ASSERT NOT `s 18 — a conveyance or other act made or done subsequently to the execution of the will prevents its operation` `the case of Amrita marrying Gita in 2018, after the will`
-- THE TWO CONJUNCTS THE REPAIR ADDED, EACH LEFT ALONE TO DECIDE (ROUND 2, R3).
-- The four cases above constrain only their conjunction — every one of them
-- makes both FALSE together, so either could be deleted with the whole suite
-- still green, the reviewer's own counterexample included. These two are the
-- inputs that separate them.
--
--   (i) The will WAS revoked in a way s 15 allows — by another will or codicil,
--       which is an act of the description s 15(b) names — but that act is not
--       this conveyance. The s 15 limb holds and only the tie is missing, so it
--       is the tie alone that keeps the conveyance from preventing the
--       operation of the will.
#ASSERT     `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the case of a conveyance made after the will, the will having been revoked by a later will`
#ASSERT NOT `s 18 — a conveyance or other act made or done subsequently to the execution of the will prevents its operation` `the case of a conveyance made after the will, the will having been revoked by a later will`
--  (ii) The conveyance is SAID to be the act by which the will is revoked, but
--       no mode of revocation in s 15 is made out. That is the case s 14 exists
--       for — "No will shall be revoked by any presumption of an intention on
--       the ground of an alteration in circumstances" — and s 18 says the same
--       thing from the other side: only an act by which the will is revoked "as
--       aforesaid" can prevent its operation, and "as aforesaid" is s 15. The
--       tie holds and only the s 15 limb is missing.
#ASSERT NOT `s 15 — the will has been revoked` `the case of a conveyance said to be the revoking act, no mode of revocation in section 15 being made out`
#ASSERT NOT `s 18 — a conveyance or other act made or done subsequently to the execution of the will prevents its operation` `the case of a conveyance said to be the revoking act, no mode of revocation in section 15 being made out`

-- s 19 ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ASSERT `s 19 — the time from which the will is construed to speak` `the plain case of Amrita` EQUALS `as if it had been executed immediately before the death of the testator`
#ASSERT `s 19 — the time from which the will is construed to speak` `the case of a will showing a contrary intention as to the time from which it speaks` EQUALS `otherwise, a contrary intention appearing by the will`

-- s 26 ----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Ismail, Amrita's son, took a specific devise and died on 20 February 2018,
-- in her lifetime, leaving Jaya, who was living when Amrita died in 2020.
#ASSERT     `s 26 — the devise or bequest shall not lapse` `the plain case of Amrita` `a specific devise to Ismail`
-- The same devise, but determinable at Ismail's death.
#ASSERT NOT `s 26 — the devise or bequest shall not lapse` `the plain case of Amrita` `a specific devise to Ismail, determinable at his death`
-- The same devise, but the will shows a contrary intention.
#ASSERT NOT `s 26 — the devise or bequest shall not lapse` `the case of a will showing a contrary intention as to lapse` `a specific devise to Ismail`
-- A legacy to a stranger to the blood is outside s 26 altogether.
#ASSERT NOT `s 26 — the devise or bequest shall not lapse` `the plain case of Amrita` `a pecuniary legacy of $10,000 to Devi`
-- LEFT AS #EVAL. The value is a list of whole `Person` records, which has no
-- literal a reader would want to write down; what CAN be stated about it is
-- asserted on either side of it.
#ASSERT NOT (null (`the donee, so far as he is a child or other issue of the testator` `the plain case of Amrita` `a specific devise to Ismail`))
#EVAL `the donee, so far as he is a child or other issue of the testator` `the plain case of Amrita` `a specific devise to Ismail`
#ASSERT null (`the donee, so far as he is a child or other issue of the testator` `the plain case of Amrita` `a pecuniary legacy of $10,000 to Devi`)
-- THE THREE LIMBS ARE ASKED OF ONE PERSON (ROUND 2, R9). On any well-formed
-- file `the donee as issue` is a singleton, because a reference is an identity,
-- so nothing distinguishes the single `any` over three conjuncts from three
-- independent traversals — and the note on the rule says a rule should not be
-- sound only because its input happens to be a singleton. This case is the
-- input that tells them apart, and it is a MALFORMED file kept for that one
-- purpose: two of Zubaida's children carry the same reference. Yasmin died in
-- Zubaida's lifetime and left no issue; Zainab is alive and has a living child.
-- Split across the two, all three limbs are satisfied SOMEWHERE and the devise
-- would not lapse; asked of one person, neither satisfies all three and it does.
#ASSERT NOT `s 26 — the devise or bequest shall not lapse` `the case of a devise to a reference two of the testator’s children share` `a specific devise to the reference Yasmin and Zainab share`

-- s 27 ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ASSERT     `s 27 — this section applies to the person` `the WA Farid`
#ASSERT     `s 27 — this section applies to the person` `the WA Osman`
#ASSERT NOT `s 27 — this section applies to the person` `the WA Amrita`
-- THE OTHER TWO ROUTES INTO s 27 (ROUND 2, R9). Until the `Person` constructor
-- took them as parameters, both were hardcoded FALSE and neither rung could be
-- reached: s 27(1)'s "any mariner or seaman being at sea", and s 27(4)'s member
-- of naval or marine forces "so circumstanced that if he were a soldier he
-- would be in actual military service". The aggregate's own verdict names the
-- mariner in terms, so the file asserted an outcome nothing could produce.
#ASSERT     `s 27 — this section applies to the person` `the WA Pak`
#ASSERT     `s 27 — this section applies to the person` `the WA Quek`
#ASSERT     `s 27 — this section applies to the person` `the WA Wahid`
#ASSERT     `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the case of the will of Pak, a mariner at sea`
#ASSERT     `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the case of the will of Quek, a member of the naval forces`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of the will of Pak, a mariner at sea` EQUALS `valid, being the will of a soldier being in actual military service, or of a mariner or seaman being at sea, disposing of his personal estate (section 27(1))`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of the will of Quek, a member of the naval forces` EQUALS `valid, being the will of a soldier being in actual military service, or of a mariner or seaman being at sea, disposing of his personal estate (section 27(1))`
#ASSERT     `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the case of the will of Farid`
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the case of the will of Farid, the old form not being made out`
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the plain case of Amrita`
-- s 27(2) fails on the same case for the same reason, and that used to be
-- unasserted: the old-form conjunct could be deleted from s 27(2) with the suite
-- still green. (ROUND 2.)
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid` `the case of the will of Farid, the old form not being made out`
-- AND THE `s 27 — this section applies to the person` CONJUNCT, LEFT ALONE TO
-- DECIDE (ROUND 2).
-- Rashid is a civilian who made exactly Farid's informal will, and it IS proved
-- that as a disposition of personal estate it would have been valid before the
-- making of this Act. s 27 does not apply to him, so neither subsection reaches
-- him. Every earlier fixture failed both conjuncts together.
#ASSERT NOT `s 27 — this section applies to the person` `the WA Rashid`
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the case of the informal will of Rashid, a civilian`
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid` `the case of the informal will of Rashid, a civilian`
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect` `the case of the informal will of Rashid, a civilian`
-- s 27(1) carries no commencement, so it reaches Osman, who died on
-- 29 August 1938 ...
#ASSERT     `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the case of the will of Osman`
-- ... but s 27(2) and s 27(3) both require a death AFTER that day, and on
-- AMBIGUITY A2's reading the day itself is in neither limb.
#ASSERT     `s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid` `the case of the will of Farid`
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid` `the case of the will of Osman`
#ASSERT     `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect` `the case of the will of Farid`
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect` `the case of the will of Osman`
-- THE 29 AUGUST 1938 BOUNDARY FROM THE OTHER SIDE (ROUND 2). Osman fixes the
-- day itself; Wahid died on the 30th, so s 27(2) and s 27(3) both reach him.
-- Without him the literal was pinned from below only.
#ASSERT     `s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid` `the case of the will of Wahid, who died on 30 August 1938`
#ASSERT     `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect` `the case of the will of Wahid, who died on 30 August 1938`

-- s 27(3)'s LAST CONJUNCT, LEFT ALONE TO DECIDE (ROUND 2, R9). Farid's will in
-- every particular except that it appoints nobody as guardian: the privilege
-- holds, the death is well after 29 August 1938, and there is simply no
-- appointment for s 27(3) to make good.
#ASSERT     `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the case of the will of Farid, appointing no guardian`
#ASSERT     `s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid` `the case of the will of Farid, appointing no guardian`
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect` `the case of the will of Farid, appointing no guardian`

-- No privilege, no appointment, and a death long after the day: over-determined
-- on every conjunct, and kept only because it is the ordinary case.
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect` `the plain case of Amrita`

-- s 27(3)'s "of the infant children of the testator" LIMB, LEFT ALONE TO DECIDE
-- (CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X1). Suri is not Farid's child. The two assertions above
-- the s 27(3) one are what make it a test of that limb and not of the gate: the
-- privilege holds and the old form is made out on exactly this case, so s 27(1)
-- and s 27(2) both answer TRUE and only the new conjunct can be the reason.
-- Before the repair this case and `the case of the will of Farid` were
-- indistinguishable to s 27(3).
#ASSERT     `s 27(1) — the testator may dispose of his personal estate as he might have done before the making of this Act` `the case of the will of Farid, appointing a guardian of a child who is not his`
#ASSERT     `s 27(2) — the testamentary disposition of real estate is valid` `the case of the will of Farid, appointing a guardian of a child who is not his`
#ASSERT NOT `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect` `the case of the will of Farid, appointing a guardian of a child who is not his`

-- s 27(3) IS DISTRIBUTIVE OVER THE APPOINTMENTS, and this is the pair that shows
-- it. One will, two appointments, one of Farid's own child and one of a
-- stranger's: the case-level boolean says only that SOMETHING in the will was
-- made good, and the list says which. A rule that answered the whole will would
-- either make the stranger's appointment good along with Jaya's or defeat Jaya's
-- along with the stranger's, and both are wrong.
#ASSERT     `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect` `the case of the will of Farid, appointing two guardians, one of them of a child who is not his`
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good` `the case of the will of Farid, appointing two guardians, one of them of a child who is not his` EQUALS LIST "bala"
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good` `the case of the will of Farid` EQUALS LIST "bala"
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good` `the case of the will of Farid, appointing a guardian of a child who is not his` EQUALS EMPTY
-- And where the gate itself fails, nothing is made good however well the
-- appointment names the testator's child: Osman died ON 29 August 1938.
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good` `the case of the will of Osman` EQUALS EMPTY

-- THE JOIN, EXERCISED (CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X4). On the one case file in this
-- module that carries facts on BOTH sides of the s 27(3) hand-off, the guardian
-- the Wills Act has made good and the guardian the estate records against the
-- infant are the same person. This is the assertion the cross-module audit found
-- missing from all four modules — not because any of them got the join wrong,
-- but because every `Deceased person` in the corpus hardcoded the estate side
-- empty, so a module that HAD got it wrong would have looked identical.
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians whose appointment this Act has made good` `the case of the will of Farid, Jaya being an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS LIST "bala"
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians recorded against the infants concerned in the estate` `the case of the will of Farid, Jaya being an infant concerned in the estate` EQUALS LIST "bala"
#ASSERT     `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the testator is of full force and effect` `the case of the will of Farid, Jaya being an infant concerned in the estate`
-- And the estate side is empty on every other case in the file, which is what
-- the default `a WA deceased person` says and is why the assertion above is not
-- vacuous.
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians recorded against the infants concerned in the estate` `the case of the will of Farid` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians recorded against the infants concerned in the estate` `the plain case of Amrita` EQUALS EMPTY

-- s 28 ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#ASSERT NOT `s 28(4) — the grant is to be disregarded` `no application to rectify has been made` `the grant of probate made on 1 March 2020`
#ASSERT     `s 28(4) — the grant is to be disregarded` `no application to rectify has been made` `the grant limited to settled land made on 1 February 2020`
#ASSERT     `s 28(4) — the grant is to be disregarded` `an application to rectify, only a grant limited to part of the estate having been made` `the grant limited to a part of the estate made on 1 February 2020`
-- The proviso to s 28(4)(c) restores the part-limited grant.
#ASSERT NOT `s 28(4) — the grant is to be disregarded` `an application to rectify, a grant of the remainder having also been made` `the grant limited to a part of the estate made on 1 February 2020`
-- s 28(5) re-dates a resealed probate to the date of sealing. LEFT AS #EVAL
-- beside the assertion that pins it, so a reader can see the date itself.
#EVAL `the date on which the grant is made` `the probate resealed on 1 September 2020, granted on 1 March 2020`
#ASSERT `the date on which the grant is made` `the probate resealed on 1 September 2020, granted on 1 March 2020` EQUALS JUST (YMD 2020 9 1)
#ASSERT `the date on which the grant is made` `the grant of probate made on 1 March 2020` EQUALS JUST (YMD 2020 3 1)
-- AND THE KIND IS TESTED, NOT JUST THE SEALING FLAG (ROUND 2, R9). s 28(5)
-- re-dates "any probate, or letters of administration WITH THE WILL ANNEXED,
-- sealed under section 47(1)" and nothing else, so a PLAIN grant of letters of
-- administration keeps the date of the grant however plainly it was sealed. The
-- module's note claimed this was tested and no fixture tested it: the kind
-- conjunct could be deleted with the whole suite still green.
#ASSERT `the date on which the grant is made` `the letters of administration sealed on 1 September 2020, granted on 1 March 2020` EQUALS JUST (YMD 2020 3 1)
#EVAL `s 28(2) — the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made` `an application to rectify made 5 months after the grant`
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made` `an application to rectify made 5 months after the grant` EQUALS JUST (YMD 2020 3 1)
-- Every grant on the file is disregarded, so none has been "first made".
#EVAL `s 28(2) — the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made` `an application to rectify, only a grant limited to settled land having been made`
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made` `an application to rectify, only a grant limited to settled land having been made` EQUALS NOTHING

#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `no application to rectify has been made` EQUALS `no application for an order under subsection (1) has been made`
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify made 5 months after the grant` EQUALS `within the time allowed`
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify made 8 months after the grant` EQUALS `made later than 6 months after the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made`
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify made 8 months after the grant, with the permission of a court` EQUALS `made with the permission of a court`
-- AMBIGUITY A7: the period runs FROM a grant, so before any qualifying grant it
-- has not begun.
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify, no grant having been made` EQUALS `within the time allowed, no grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate having yet been made`
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify, only a grant limited to settled land having been made` EQUALS `within the time allowed, no grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate having yet been made`
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify, only a grant limited to part of the estate having been made` EQUALS `within the time allowed, no grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate having yet been made`
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify, a grant of the remainder having also been made` EQUALS `made later than 6 months after the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made`
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify made 10 months after a probate that was resealed` EQUALS `within the time allowed`
-- The same two dates, but a plain grant of letters of administration: s 28(5)
-- does not reach it, the clock runs from 1 March 2020, and the application is
-- four months out of time.
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify made 10 months after letters of administration that were sealed` EQUALS `made later than 6 months after the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made`
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify whose date is not in evidence` EQUALS `the date of the first such grant, or the date of the application, not being in evidence`
-- THE SAME EVIDENCE GAP AS V7, WHERE IT REPEATS. One of the two grants has no
-- date, so it cannot be shown not to have been the first, and the six months
-- cannot be measured. Reported as indeterminate rather than measured from the
-- later grant, which would give the applicant time s 28(2) does not.
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify, one of the two grants being undated` EQUALS `the date of the first such grant, or the date of the application, not being in evidence`
-- REPAIR V8. The permission is dispositive whatever the dates: s 28(2)'s
-- excepting words lift the bar altogether, and a period that does not apply
-- cannot be missed. This used to come back as indeterminate.
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify whose date is not in evidence, made with the permission of a court` EQUALS `made with the permission of a court`
-- THE SIX-MONTH BOUNDARY, both sides: the grant is 1 March 2020, so 1 September
-- 2020 is "no later than 6 months after" it and 2 September 2020 is not.
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify made on the last day of the 6 months` EQUALS `within the time allowed`
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify made one day after the 6 months` EQUALS `made later than 6 months after the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made`
-- REPAIR V6, AND THE MONTH-END BOUNDARY. Six months after 31 August 2020 is
-- 28 February 2021. The old arithmetic rolled the non-existent 31 February
-- forward to 3 March 2021 and reported an application made on 1 March 2021 as
-- in time.
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify made on 28 February 2021, the grant being on 31 August 2020` EQUALS `within the time allowed`
#ASSERT `s 28(2) — whether the application for rectification is in time` `an application to rectify made on 1 March 2021, the grant being on 31 August 2020` EQUALS `made later than 6 months after the date on which a grant authorising the administration of the testator’s estate is first made`

-- Formal validity, all together --------------------------------------------
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the plain case of Amrita` EQUALS `valid`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of the will of Eugene` EQUALS `not valid, being a will made by a person under 21 years of age (section 4)`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of the will of Eugene, made the day before he turned 21` EQUALS `not valid, being a will made by a person under 21 years of age (section 4)`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of the will of Eugene, made on the day he turned 21` EQUALS `valid`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will not in writing` EQUALS `not valid, the will not being in writing or not executed in the manner mentioned in section 6(2), and not being treated as properly executed under section 5`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will one of whose two witnesses subscribed out of her sight` EQUALS `not valid, the will not being in writing or not executed in the manner mentioned in section 6(2), and not being treated as properly executed under section 5`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will whose date of execution is not in evidence` EQUALS `the age of the testator when the will was made not being in evidence, section 4 cannot be applied`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 2020` EQUALS `valid`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will executed abroad, the testator dying in 1990` EQUALS `not valid, the will not being in writing or not executed in the manner mentioned in section 6(2), and not being treated as properly executed under section 5`
-- REPAIR V7. s 5 was not applied and did not fail; it was never reached, the
-- date of death not being in evidence, and the aggregate now says which.
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will executed abroad, the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence` EQUALS `the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence, section 5 cannot be applied`
-- THE SAME BRANCH, REACHED THROUGH THE OTHER TWO DISJUNCTS, AND THEN NOT REACHED
-- AT ALL (ROUND 2). Only s 5(2) used to open this branch, so the s 5(3) disjuncts
-- of `s 5 — some connecting factor is made out, ...` could be deleted; and no
-- case had an unknown date of death with NO connecting factor, so the branch's
-- second conjunct could be deleted too and every will of a testator whose death
-- date was unproved would have reported an evidence gap it did not have.
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will conforming only to the lex situs, the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence` EQUALS `the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence, section 5 cannot be applied`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will executed on board a vessel, the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence` EQUALS `the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence, section 5 cannot be applied`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will conforming to no law outside this Act, the date of the death of the testator not being in evidence` EQUALS `not valid, the will not being in writing or not executed in the manner mentioned in section 6(2), and not being treated as properly executed under section 5`
-- REPAIR V4. s 5(3)(b) reaches this will so far only as it disposes of the
-- immovable property, and the aggregate no longer reports it simply `valid`.
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will conforming only to the law of the place where its immovable property is situated` EQUALS `treated as properly executed so far only as regards a part of the will (section 5(3)(b), (c) or (d)), and not otherwise valid`
-- REPAIRS V2 and V3, at the aggregate.
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will signed beside the end rather than at the foot` EQUALS `valid`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of a will signed at the head, above all the dispositive words` EQUALS `not valid, the will not being in writing or not executed in the manner mentioned in section 6(2), and not being treated as properly executed under section 5`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of the will of Farid` EQUALS `valid, being the will of a soldier being in actual military service, or of a mariner or seaman being at sea, disposing of his personal estate (section 27(1))`
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of the will of Osman` EQUALS `valid, being the will of a soldier being in actual military service, or of a mariner or seaman being at sea, disposing of his personal estate (section 27(1))`
-- Farid was 19, and without the s 27(1) privilege s 4 defeats his will.
#ASSERT `the formal validity of the will` `the case of the will of Farid, the old form not being made out` EQUALS `not valid, being a will made by a person under 21 years of age (section 4)`



§§ `What is NOT encoded, and why`

-- A reader who cannot tell a provision that is absent from the Act from one
-- that is merely absent from this file has been misled. Everything below is
-- real statutory material in the Wills Act 1838, and none of it is encoded.
-- The list is exhaustive: every section and subsection of the Act is either
-- encoded above or named here.
--
-- CARRIED INERT, BECAUSE THERE IS NOTHING TO DECIDE:
--
--   s 1  Short title. `s 1 — short title` above.
--   s 2  Interpretation. `s 2 — interpretation` above. Two of its five
--        definitions, "internal law" and "state", are read only by s 5 and
--        only to name a system of law this cleanroom does not model.
--
-- DECLARATORY OF THE EXTENT OF THE POWER, NOT A TEST:
--
--   s 3(1) "Subject to the provisions of this Act, every person may devise,
--        bequeath or dispose of by his will, executed in the manner required
--        under this Act, all real estate and all personal estate which he
--        shall be entitled to either at law or in equity at the time of his
--        death." Its only operative condition is that the will be executed in
--        the manner required under this Act, and that is s 6, encoded above.
--        What is left describes WHAT PROPERTY PASSES, which is a fact about
--        the estate and not a decision this Act makes.
--   s 3(2)(a)-(d) The four classes the power extends to — estates pur autre
--        vie, contingent, executory or other future interests, rights of entry
--        for conditions broken, and after-acquired property. Each is a
--        proposition about a KIND of property. family-domain records the
--        estate as four value cells, split by situs and by movability, because
--        that is the split the Intestate Succession Act and the Probate and
--        Administration Act make; nothing in any of the four Acts turns on
--        which of s 3(2)'s classes an asset falls into, so recording the
--        distinction would add a field no provision reads.
--
-- ABOUT THE CONTENT OF A FOREIGN LAW, WHICH ARRIVES HERE ALREADY PROVED:
--
--   s 5(5) "regard shall be had to the formal requirements of that law at the
--        time of execution, but this shall not prevent account being taken of
--        an alteration of law affecting wills executed at that time if the
--        alteration enables the will to be treated as properly executed."
--   s 5(6) A foreign law's special formalities for testators of a particular
--        description, and its qualifications for witnesses, "shall be treated,
--        notwithstanding any rule of that law to the contrary, as a formal
--        requirement only".
--        Both are directions about how to READ a foreign law. This module
--        takes conformity to that law as one proved fact per connecting factor
--        (see the DECLARE), so there is nothing for either subsection to
--        operate on. They are not encoded rather than being satisfied
--        silently: a case in which the foreign law changed after execution, or
--        in which its rule is characterised as substantive rather than formal,
--        has to be resolved before the fact reaches this file.
--   s 5(8)(a)-(b) The tie-break where a territory or state has "2 or more
--        systems of internal law relating to the formal validity of wills".
--        This cleanroom models no named territory or state at all, so there is
--        no second system for the tie-break to choose between.
--
--   s 5(7) "The construction of a will shall not be altered by reason of any
--        change in the testator’s domicile after the execution of the will."
--        A denial, like ss 9 and 14 — but its subject is the CONSTRUCTION of
--        the will, and this module construes no will. Nothing anywhere in this
--        cleanroom records a change of domicile after execution;
--        family-domain records only a Singapore / not-Singapore binary at
--        death. So the denial has no antecedent to deny and is named here
--        instead of being given a decision that could only return FALSE.
--
-- THE CONSTRUCTION RULES, ss 19 TO 25. s 19 IS ENCODED; ss 20 to 25 are not,
-- and each has its own reason. What they share is that they construe THE WORDS
-- OF A PARTICULAR INSTRUMENT, and nothing in the shared ontology records the
-- words of a gift — a `Disposition` has a donee, a kind, a value and two
-- booleans, and no text.
--
--   s 20 Residuary devises to include estates comprised in lapsed and void
--        devises. Two obstacles, and the second is the harder. Its middle limb
--        — a devise void "by reason of the devise being contrary to law or
--        otherwise incapable of taking effect" — is an open-textured
--        characterisation. And its OUTPUT is a redistribution of property
--        between gifts: the failed devise "shall be included in the residuary
--        devise (if any) contained in the will", and a `Disposition` carries a
--        value but no mechanism for augmenting another disposition's.
--   s 21(1) and s 21(2) A general devise or bequest to include property over
--        which the testator has a general power of appointment. Turns on
--        whether the gift is "otherwise described in a general manner" — a
--        question of construing the words used.
--   s 22 Devise without words of limitation. Turns on whether the devise was
--        made "without any words of limitation", which is a fact about the
--        text of the gift.
--   s 23(1) and s 23(2) Construction of words importing want or failure of
--        issue. s 23(1) construes particular words — "die without issue",
--        "die without leaving issue" — and s 23(2) carves out cases where
--        those words refer to "a preceding gift". Both need the words.
--   s 24 Devise of real estate to a trustee or executor. Turns on whether "a
--        definite term of years, absolute or determinable, or an estate of
--        freehold, shall thereby be given to him expressly or by implication".
--        Note that s 24 has NO contrary-intention limb; that quantifier is
--        what the exception is instead.
--   s 25 Devise of real estate to a trustee without limitation. Needs three
--        things the ontology does not carry: the absence of "any express
--        limitation of the estate to be taken by the trustee", to whom the
--        beneficial interest or the surplus rents and profits are given, and
--        whether "the purposes of the trust may continue beyond the life of
--        that person". s 24 and s 25 alone among ss 19 to 26 have no
--        contrary-intention limb, and s 25 alone has no exception limb of any
--        kind. (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW V9: this sentence used to claim the
--        superlative for s 25 alone, which contradicted the s 24 entry six
--        lines above — both cannot be true of the same set. s 24 has
--        no contrary-intention limb either; what it has instead, and s 25
--        lacks, is an exception — "unless a definite term of years, absolute
--        or determinable, or an estate of freehold, shall thereby be given to
--        him expressly or by implication".)
--
-- A DISCRETION, A SAVING, A RULE-MAKING POWER AND A DEFINITION — s 28:
--
--   s 28(1) "A court may order that a will be rectified so as to carry out the
--        testator’s intentions, if the court is satisfied that, as a
--        consequence of either or both of the following, the will is so
--        expressed that the will fails to carry out the testator’s
--        intentions". A
--        discretion, and encoding it as a boolean would be a lie: the output
--        is whether an order is made, which no rule determines. Its two
--        antecedents, "(a) a clerical error" and "(b) a failure to understand
--        the testator’s instructions", are findings about the drafting of a
--        particular instrument. What IS encoded is s 28(2)'s time bar, which
--        is determinate, together with s 28(4) and s 28(5), which say when the
--        clock starts.
--   s 28(3)(a)-(b) The saving for personal representatives who distribute
--        after the six months. Paragraph (a) denies a liability that this
--        section does not create in the first place — "this section does not
--        render the personal representatives liable" — so a decision for it
--        would return TRUE on every case and say nothing. Paragraph (b)
--        preserves "any power to recover, by reason of the making of an order
--        under subsection (1), any part of the testator’s estate that is so
--        distributed", and no such power is modelled anywhere in this
--        cleanroom.
--   s 28(6) A power for the Family Justice Rules Committee to make Family
--        Justice Rules regulating the procedure on an application. No
--        determinate output.
--   s 28(7) Definitions, and they are discharged by family-domain rather than
--        left out. "court" is `Court`, whose comment records that s 28(7)
--        opens "In this section —" and so gives the Wills Act no Act-wide
--        definition. "grant" is `Kind of grant` together with
--        `The court that made the grant`, which exists precisely so that
--        s 28(7)(a)-(b)'s "granted before 1 January 2015 by a District Court"
--        has a truthful value. Nothing in s 28(7) is left over.
--
-- NOT STATUTORY TEXT AT ALL: the Legislative History at the foot of the source
-- ("This Legislative History is a service provided by the Law Revision
-- Commission on a best-efforts basis. It is not part of the Act.") and the
-- table of Abbreviations after it. The Arrangement of Sections at the front is
-- likewise not encoded, though its marginal notes are the names used above.
--
-- SIX LIMITS THAT ARE NOT SECTIONS, restated here because a reader looking
-- for what this module cannot do should find all of it in one place. (The
-- heading said FOUR while the list had five; corrected at the cross-module
-- round, which also added the sixth.)
--
--   (1) s 10(1) AND A GIFT TO THE WITNESS'S SPOUSE. The per-witness decision
--       reaches it; the per-disposition decision does not, because
--       family-domain's `Marriage` is rooted at the deceased and there is no
--       edge from an attesting witness to his wife or husband. See the note on
--       the two s 10(1) rules.
--   (2) s 28(4)(c)'s PROVISO is one fact per case, not one per grant, because
--       `Grant` has no identity and its part is free text. See the note on
--       `A case under this Act`.
--   (3) PARTIAL REVOCATION (s 15, "or any part thereof") and PARTIAL
--       INEFFECTIVENESS OF AN ALTERATION (s 16(1), "except so far as") are
--       AMBIGUITIES A8 and A10 and are not computed.
--   (4) s 10(1) AND A PERSON CLAIMING UNDER THE WITNESS OR THE WITNESS'S
--       SPOUSE. s 10(1) avoids the gift "so far only as concerns the person
--       attesting the execution of the will, or the wife or husband of that
--       person, or any person claiming under that person or wife or husband" —
--       THREE classes, and this module reaches only the first two. There is no
--       derivative-claim edge anywhere in the shared ontology: family-domain's
--       `Person` records descent, marriage and adoption and nothing that says
--       one person takes THROUGH another, so a donee who claims under an
--       attesting witness (an assignee of the legacy, a trustee for the
--       witness, the witness's own devisee) is indistinguishable from a
--       stranger. Both s 10(1) decisions therefore under-avoid on such a file,
--       and the class has to be applied by hand. (REPAIR, ADVERSARIAL REVIEW
--       V11: the class was previously unencoded and unmentioned, so a reader
--       could not tell it from a class the Act does not have.)
--
--   (5) FOUR CONJUNCTS THAT NO FIXTURE CAN ISOLATE, because on a well-formed
--       file another conjunct of the same rule entails them. Each is stated
--       because the Act states it, and each is marked where it stands:
--       s 18's "No conveyance or other act made or done subsequently" (the
--       tie to the revoking act presupposes it); s 13(1)'s "except a will made
--       in exercise of a power of appointment" (its "when" clause presupposes
--       the appointment); s 26's "any person being a
--       child or other issue of the testator" and its "leaving issue" (both
--       subsumed by the survivorship traversal over the same list); and, in a
--       different way, s 5(4)'s and s 14's antecedents, which are not facts
--       anybody proves and are discussed in the run section. Deleting any of
--       them changes no answer in this file, and that is measured rather than
--       assumed. (ROUND 2.)
--
--   (6) s 27(3) AND THE WORD "INFANT". The subsection makes good an
--       appointment "of any person as guardian of the infant children of the
--       testator", and this module encodes only the CHILDREN half of that
--       phrase, matching the appointment against the testator's own children
--       by reference. The INFANT half is not encoded, and the reason is not
--       that the Act is silent about it — it is that the age of majority is an
--       already-construed input which family-domain expressly forbids this
--       module to read ("The WA is different and must NOT read this field").
--       s 4's own "under 21 years of age" cannot stand in: it is the Act's test
--       for the maker of a will, not for the children of a dead one. So the
--       decision OVER-INCLUDES — an appointment of a guardian of an ADULT child
--       of the testator is reported as of full force and effect — and a
--       consumer that needs the infancy limb must apply it from the age of
--       majority itself. Argued at the site; reported as a domain gap in the
--       closing section. (CROSS-MODULE ROUND, X2.)
--
-- TWO FIXTURES BELOW ARE DELIBERATELY MALFORMED, and neither is a legal
-- scenario. `the position stated as beside the end, though nobody signed the
-- will` records where a signature sits on a will that has none; Yasmin and
-- Zainab are two different people carrying one `the reference for this person`,
-- which is an identity. Each exists to pin one property of one rule that a
-- well-formed file cannot exercise — that s 6(3) presupposes a signature, and
-- that s 26 asks its three limbs of ONE person — and each says so at its site.
-- A probate file that looked like either of them would be a file with an error
-- in it. (ROUND 2, R11 and R9.)


§§ `The ambiguity register`

-- Each fork is argued at the site where it bites; this is the index. "TAKEN"
-- is the reading the code implements, and in every case the other reading is
-- defensible — that is what makes it a fork rather than an error.
--
--   A1   s 4      The age when the will was made is not in evidence.
--                 TAKEN: neither limb is made out, and `the formal validity of
--                 the will` reports that s 4 cannot be applied rather than
--                 answering valid or invalid. The neighbouring modules resolve
--                 the same evidential question in two other shapes — the PAA
--                 folds it to FALSE, the GIA propagates NOTHING — and the site
--                 now says so and says why this Act differs (X5).
--
--   A2   s 5(9), s 10(4), s 27(2), s 27(3)
--                 A commencement expressed as a death after a named day. A
--                 death ON that day falls in neither limb of s 5(9), which
--                 says "died before 26 June 1992" and "dies after that date".
--                 TAKEN: strictly after, uniformly.
--
--   A3   s 6(2)   "those witnesses shall subscribe the will in the presence of
--                 the testator" — every attesting witness, or at least two?
--                 TAKEN: at least two. Differs only where there are more than
--                 two attesting witnesses.
--
--   A4   s 13(1)  In the exception — "except a will made in exercise of a
--                 power of appointment, when the real or personal estate
--                 thereby appointed would not in default of such appointment
--                 pass to his or her heir, executor or administrator or the
--                 person entitled under the Intestate Succession Act 1967" —
--                 is "when" a further condition, or a description?
--                 TAKEN: a further condition, encoded as a conjunction.
--
--   A5   s 13(2)  The will is expressed to be made in contemplation of a
--                 marriage but the intended spouse is not identified.
--                 TAKEN: the saving does not apply, because s 13(2) saves the
--                 will only against "the marriage contemplated".
--
--   A6   s 26     "living at the time of the death of the testator", where the
--                 issue died on the same DATE as the testator.
--                 TAKEN: not living. A commorientes case needs a time, which
--                 the ontology does not record.
--
--   A7   s 28(2)  An application to rectify made before any grant.
--                 TAKEN: in time — the period has not begun. Reported under
--                 its own name so the case is visibly decided.
--
--   A8   s 15     "or any part thereof" — partial revocation.
--                 TAKEN: not encoded. The decision answers only whether the
--                 INSTRUMENT is revoked. s 17(2)'s partial-revocation fact is
--                 recorded and used; nothing else divides a will into parts.
--
--   A9   s 10(3)  "duly executed without his attestation" — the whole of s 6,
--                 re-asked, or only the attestation requirements?
--                 TAKEN: the whole of s 6.
--
--   A10  s 16(1)  "except so far as the words or effect of the will before
--                 such alteration shall not be apparent" — pro tanto, or all
--                 or nothing for a given alteration?
--                 TAKEN: all or nothing, because nothing divides an alteration
--                 into its legible and illegible parts. The pro tanto reading
--                 is orthodox and is not contradicted, only not computed.
--
--   A11  s 7(1), s 5(1), s 5(3)(d)
--                 Can s 5 save an appointment from s 7(1)? s 7(1) invalidates
--                 an appointment "unless the will is executed in the manner
--                 required by this Act"; s 5(1) says s 5 takes effect
--                 "notwithstanding any other provisions of this Act"; and
--                 s 5(3)(d) treats "a will so far as it exercises a power of
--                 appointment" as properly executed where the execution
--                 conformed to the law governing the essential validity of the
--                 power.
--                 TAKEN: it can. A notwithstanding clause stops another
--                 provision taking effect AGAINST the section, so it is no
--                 answer that s 7's phrase is s 6's. The other reading — that
--                 "executed in the manner required by this Act" means s 6 and
--                 nothing else — was stated as settled in a note on s 7(2), and
--                 produced a contradiction on one case file: the module
--                 reported the will as treated as properly executed so far as
--                 it exercises the appointment AND the appointment as invalid
--                 for want of execution. It bites only where s 5 is made out
--                 and s 6 is not, which is the foreign-execution case s 5 was
--                 inserted for. (ROUND 2, R2.)


§§ `Where this Act joins the other three`

-- FIVE JOINS, and the count changed at the cross-module round. Three are
-- reaches OUT of the Wills Act into its neighbours: two of those are textual —
-- the Act names the other statute — and one is structural. The fourth and
-- fifth were added because the audit that read all four modules together found
-- them, and found this Act silent on both: one is a reach IN, from the
-- Guardianship of Infants Act to a gift this Act has decided; the other is not
-- a reach at all but a place where three of the four Acts answer the same
-- evidential question in three different shapes.
--
-- HOW TO READ THE FIRST THREE. Each says what this Act hands over and to
-- which provision. NONE of them says that the other module takes it: that is a
-- fact about the other module, and where it is known it is stated as a
-- measurement with a date, not as a description of how the corpus works.
--
--   s 27(3) -> GUARDIANSHIP OF INFANTS ACT 1934, s 7. THE MAIN JOIN, and it
--     has its own note above. A will rendered valid (or which would have been
--     rendered valid) by s 27 makes good "any appointment contained in that
--     will of any person as guardian of the infant children of the testator".
--     The appointment itself is GIA s 7(1)-(2); everything that happens to the
--     guardian afterwards is GIA ss 7(3)-(6), 8 and 10. The hand-off is
--     already typed, because family-domain makes
--     `the guardians of infant children appointed by the will` a
--     LIST OF `Guardian` and gives `How the guardian was appointed` the
--     members `appointed by the father by will` and
--     `appointed by the mother by will`. What crosses is now a VALUE and not a
--     verdict: `s 27(3) — the appointments contained in the will that are of
--     full force and effect` is a LIST OF `Guardian`, so a will appointing two
--     guardians can hand over one of them (X1). Whether anything on the other
--     side takes it is a separate question and is answered — as at this round,
--     no — at the foot of this section.
--
--   s 13(1) -> INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT 1967. The Wills Act's only express
--     mention of another of the four Acts: the exception saves an appointing
--     will where the appointed estate "would not in default of such
--     appointment pass to his or her heir, executor or administrator or the
--     person entitled under the Intestate Succession Act 1967". It is a fact
--     and not a computation, because it asks what would have happened under a
--     counterfactual instrument, not what happens under this estate; and it is
--     recorded as a fact on `Will` by family-domain for that reason.
--
--   s 28(2), s 28(4)-(5) -> PROBATE AND ADMINISTRATION ACT 1934. A Wills Act
--     deadline measured off a Probate and Administration Act event. The clock
--     starts at the first GRANT, which the PAA makes; s 28(4) filters the
--     grants by their limitation; and s 28(5) re-dates a grant "sealed under
--     section 47(1) of the Probate and Administration Act 1934" to the date of
--     sealing. All three facts are on family-domain's `Grant`.
--
--   GIA s 17 -> THIS ACT'S ss 10(1) AND 26. A REACH IN, added at the
--     cross-module round (X7) because the audit found the pointer running in
--     one direction only: guardianship-of-infants-act.l4 states this join well
--     from its own side and nothing here acknowledged it. This Act decides
--     whether a gift takes effect; GIA s 17 decides that a guardian of an
--     infant's property cannot give a good discharge for a legacy, which is
--     what stands between the gift standing and the gift being paid over. This
--     module reads nothing for it, because no provision of the Wills Act turns
--     on who may discharge a legacy. The argument is at the site, after
--     `s 26 — the devise or bequest shall not lapse`.
--
--   THE AGE OF MAJORITY -> A NON-JOIN, AND IT IS DELIBERATE. The PAA and the
--     GIA both read family-domain's `the date on which the person attains the
--     age of majority`. This module reads it NOWHERE, and family-domain's own
--     note tells it not to: "The WA is different and must NOT read this
--     field", because s 4 states its own numeric test and s 27(1) disapplies
--     that test for a soldier or a mariner. Two consequences are recorded
--     rather than left to be discovered. (a) The three modules that ask "the
--     age is not in evidence — now what?" answer in three different SHAPES —
--     a third enum member here, FALSE in the PAA, NOTHING in the GIA — and the
--     comparison is argued at AMBIGUITY A1 (X5). (b) s 27(3)'s "the infant
--     children of the testator" is the one place this Act would want the
--     shared field and is forbidden it, so that limb is not encoded; see the
--     note on s 27(3), and the domain-gap section below.
--
-- AND WHAT THE OTHER SIDE DOES WITH THE MAIN JOIN — A MEASUREMENT OF ANOTHER
-- FILE AT A MOMENT, NOT A CLAIM ABOUT HOW THE CORPUS BEHAVES. Check it before
-- relying on it; it went stale once while this section was being written.
-- The cross-module audit found guardianship-of-infants-act.l4 with no joins
-- section, one occurrence of the string "Wills Act" in 4,659 lines and no
-- occurrence of the other two Acts. Re-measured after that module's own
-- cross-module round, at 5,187 lines: it has a
-- `Where this Act joins the other three` section whose FIRST join is this one,
-- and names the Wills Act 20 times. It still does not READ the answer — no
-- decision in it takes a `Will` — and takes the appointment as already
-- construed instead, instructing a case file to set
-- `appointed by the father by will` only where this Act's decision is TRUE.
-- The full argument, and what this module did about it, is at the s 27(3) site
-- above (X3).
--
-- And one place where a join is often expected and is NOT here: s 3 does not
-- reach the ISA. s 3(1) confers the power — "every person may devise,
-- bequeath or dispose of by his will, executed in the manner required under
-- this Act, all real estate and all personal estate which he shall be
-- entitled to either at law or in equity at the time of his death" — and stops
-- there. How much of the estate a will fails to dispose of, and what then
-- happens to the rest, is ISA s 3, whose definition of "intestate" reaches a
-- person who leaves a will but dies intestate as to some beneficial interest
-- in his property, and ISA s 10. Both are the Intestate Succession Act's to
-- decide, from `the dispositions made by the will`; nothing here computes
-- them.


§§ `Where the shared ontology stopped short`

-- ADDED AT THE CROSS-MODULE ROUND. Everything below is a limit of
-- family-domain.l4, not of this Act or of this file, and none of it was
-- changed here: three other agents are encoding against that file and it must
-- stay still while they do. This section exists so that the gaps are REPORTED
-- rather than worked around silently in four places at once.
--
--   (1) EVERY SHARED RECORD HAS FOUR PRIVATE CONSTRUCTORS AND NO PUBLIC ONE.
--       `Person`, `Child`, `Deceased person`, `Estate`, `Family`, `Grant` and
--       `Infant` are declared once, in family-domain, and then built four
--       times — once per Act module, by four sets of test scaffolding that
--       reached for the same obvious names. Measured before the repair: the
--       four modules could NOT be imported together, because L4 raises a hard
--       error at the first use of a colliding name. Measured: FIFTEEN distinct
--       names collided, in twenty-one module-pair collisions. Thirteen of the
--       fifteen were this module's — the fixture people
--       `Bala`, `Chandra`, `Devi`, `Farid`, `Gita`, `Ismail`, `Rahim`, `Tariq`
--       and `Zainab`, and the constructors `a person`, `a legitimate child`,
--       `a deceased person` and `a grant`. All of them now carry this Act's
--       tag — `the WA Bala`, `a WA deceased person` — and every fixture person
--       in the file carries it, not only the nine that collided, so that a
--       reader is never left wondering why one name is tagged and the next is
--       not.
--       THE TAG IS A WORKAROUND AND SHOULD NOT SURVIVE. `a WA deceased person`
--       and `an ISA deceased person` build the same record for the same
--       purpose, and nothing makes them agree. What the corpus wants is ONE
--       constructor per shared record, owned where the record is declared or
--       in the `family-cases.l4` the cleanroom brief's own file table names
--       and which does not exist. Until then, four copies drift.
--
--   (2) THERE IS NO ROUTE FROM A `Deceased person` TO AN `Entitlement`.
--       `Entitlement` is declared in family-domain and no field of any record
--       has its type, so the Intestate Succession Act's answer to "who takes
--       what" is computed and then has nowhere to be recorded. Checked here:
--       intestate-succession-act.l4 has decisions returning `Entitlement` and
--       `LIST OF Entitlement`, and no `DECLARE` in family-domain gives any
--       field that type. Also self-reported by probate-administration-act.l4,
--       which needs it for s 29(5)'s "the administrator is entitled to the
--       whole of the estate" (source/PAA1934.txt s 29(5)) and receives that
--       instead as a sworn boolean. It does not bite on this module —
--       the Wills Act never asks who takes — but it is the reason nothing can
--       reconcile the ISA's computed shares with the PAA's proved fact, and a
--       case file may assert both and have them contradict.
--
--   (3) THE s 57 / s 58 FIELDS ON `Estate` ARE WRITTEN AND NOT READ. `the debts
--       and liabilities of the deceased`, `the value of the interest in
--       property which at the time of his death is charged with the payment of
--       money`, `the amount of the charge on that interest` and `the deceased
--       has by will, deed or other document signified a contrary or other
--       intention` were declared, family-domain says, so that
--       probate-administration-act.l4 "can state s 57 completely"; that module
--       then put ss 57-58 and both Schedules out of scope. WHAT THIS FILE CAN
--       VOUCH FOR: it writes all four, as nil, in `an estate of no recorded
--       value`, and reads none of them, no Wills Act provision asking about
--       any. WHAT IT REPORTS AT SECOND HAND, because it is another module's
--       measurement and will go stale: probate-administration-act.l4 reports
--       the same gap in its own out-of-scope section, counts FIVE fields rather
--       than four (adding the s 58 facts beside them), and says they are "read
--       by nothing, in any of the four Act modules". A field written by
--       fixtures and read by nobody looks exactly like a field that is used,
--       which is why it is named here as well as there.
--
--   (4) THE INSTRUCTION ON THE AGE OF MAJORITY IS WIDER THAN ITS REASON.
--       family-domain says of `the date on which the person attains the age of
--       majority`: "The WA is different and must NOT read this field", and
--       gives as the reason that s 4 states its own numeric test and s 27(1)
--       disapplies it. That reason is about the TESTATOR'S OWN AGE. It does
--       not reach s 27(3)'s "the infant children of the testator", which is
--       about the children, is not answered by any number in the Wills Act,
--       and is the one place this module would want exactly that already-
--       construed field — the same field the PAA and the GIA both read. This
--       module honours the instruction as written and leaves the infancy limb
--       of s 27(3) unencoded, saying so at the site. The ontology should
--       either narrow the instruction to s 4 and s 27(1) or say expressly that
--       s 27(3)'s infancy is somebody else's to apply.
--
--   (5) `Grant` HAS NO IDENTITY. Already recorded above as a limit on
--       s 28(4)(c); repeated here because it is the ontology's and not this
--       Act's. A grant is a record with no key, so nothing can decide whether
--       one grant's part is another's remainder, and the proviso is carried as
--       one fact per case.
--
--   (6) NO SHARED FIELD CARRIES s 27(3)'s ANSWER, and the Guardianship of
--       Infants Act module says so in terms — it treats a will-appointed
--       guardian as already construed because "no field the two modules share
--       carries it". Two halves of one appointment therefore sit in two
--       modules with nothing between them: `Guardian`'s
--       `how the guardian was appointed` records THAT the appointment was by
--       will, and nothing records whether the Wills Act made it good. Since
--       this round the modules can at least be co-imported, so a combined file
--       can call `s 27(3) — the appointments contained in the will that are of
--       full force and effect` directly. A FIELD would still be better: it
--       would let a case file record the answer without either Act module
--       importing the other, which is the dependency direction both modules
--       have deliberately refused. The right shape is family-domain's to
--       choose — a field on `Guardian`, or a member of
--       `How the guardian was appointed` that says the appointment was by a
--       will rendered valid by Wills Act s 27 — and it is not chosen here.
--
--   (7) THERE IS NO EDGE FROM AN ATTESTING WITNESS TO HIS SPOUSE, AND NO
--       DERIVATIVE-CLAIM EDGE AT ALL. Both are recorded above among the four
--       limits that are not sections; both are shapes the shared ontology
--       would have to grow, since `Marriage` is rooted at the deceased and
--       `Person` records descent, marriage and adoption and nothing that says
--       one person takes THROUGH another.
